\subsection{Angular momentum operators}
Following the classical definition of the angular momentum $\bm{l}_{i}=\bm{r}_{i}\times\bm{p}_{i}$, the total angular momentum operator $\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}=\hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{l}}_{1}+\hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{l}}_{2}=\left[L_{x},L_{y},L_{z}\right]^\mathsf{T}$ of a system of two quasiparticles in the translated laboratory-fixed reference frame ($x\,y\,z$)  (see Fig.~\ref{fig:comparison}) is defined as
    \begin{equation}\label{amocc}
    \hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz} = \underbrace{-\textbf{\textit{i}}\!\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
         y_{1}\partial_{z_{1}}&\!\!\!-\!\!\!&z_{1}\partial_{y_{1}}   \\
         z_{1}\partial_{x_{1}}&\!\!\!-\!\!\!&x_{1}\partial_{z_{1}}  \\
         x_{1}\partial_{y_{1}}&\!\!\!-\!\!\!&y_{1}\partial_{x_{1}} 
    \end{array}\right]}_{\hat{\hspace{4pt}{\bm{l}}_{1}}}
    \underbrace{\!-\textbf{\textit{i}}\!\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
         y_{2}\partial_{z_{2}}&\!\!\!-\!\!\!&z_{2}\partial_{y_{2}}  \\
         z_{2}\partial_{x_{2}}&\!\!\!-\!\!\!&x_{2}\partial_{z_{2}} \\
         x_{2}\partial_{y_{2}}&\!\!\!-\!\!\!&y_{2}\partial_{x_{2}}
    \end{array}\right]}_{\hat{\hspace{4pt}{\bm{l}}_{2}}}
\end{equation}
where $\textbf{\textit{i}}=\sqrt{-1}$. Direct calculations show that both the operators $\hat{L}_{z}$ and $\hat{L}^{2}=\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}\boldsymbol{\cdot}\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}$ commute with the Hamiltonian operator $\hat{\mathcal{H}}$ in Eq.~\eqref{secm2}, ensuring that $\Psi(\bm{r}_{1}, \bm{r}_{2})$ is simultaneously an eigenfunction of $\hat{\mathcal{H}}$, $\hat{L}^{2}$, and $\hat{L}_{z}$. These eigenproperties of $\Psi(\bm{r}_{1}, \bm{r}_{2})$ allow us to separate the rotational and internal degrees of freedom. To achieve this goal, we consider the $\left|L,M\right\rangle$ invariant subspaces, labeled by the quantum numbers $L$ and $M$ corresponding to the operators $\hat{L}^2$ and $\hat{L}_{z}$, respectively, and for each such subspace, we choose a suitable angular generator basis. Minimal bipolar harmonics $\Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi}(\theta_{1},\phi_{1},\theta_2,\phi_2)$  (MBH) or the Wigner functions $\mathcal{D}^{MK}_L(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ constitute a customary choice of such an angular basis. In order to discuss the properties of $\Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi}$ and $\mathcal{D}^{MK}_L$ needed for further derivations, we introduce first various angular momentum operators in the bispherical coordinates $(r_{1},\theta_{1},\phi_{1},r_{2},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$ and in the (internal + Euler angles) coordinates  $(r_{1},r_{2},\theta,\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$.

The operator $\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}$ can be expressed in the bispherical coordinates  $(r_{1},\theta_{1},\phi_{1},r_{2},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$ following the usual relation between the Cartesian and the spherical coordinates
\begin{equation}\label{ctspc}
    \left[ x,y,z\right]^\mathsf{T} = \left[ r \sin\theta \cos\phi, r \sin\theta \sin\phi, r \cos\theta\right]^\mathsf{T}    
\end{equation}
giving
    \begin{equation} \label{eq:Lbisph}
        \hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}\! =\!\!\left[\begin{array}{r}
        \!\hat{L}_x\! \\ \!\hat{L}_y\! \\ \!\hat{L}_z\! \end{array}\right] \!\!= \textbf{\textit{i}} \sum_{i=1}^2 \!\left[\begin{array}{r}
        \!+ \sin\phi_{i}\,\partial_{\theta_{i}} \!+\frac{\cos\theta_{i}\cos{\phi_i}}{\sin\theta_{i}}\,\partial_{\phi_{i}} \!\!  \\
        \!- \cos\phi_{i}\,\partial_{\theta_{i}} \!+\frac{\cos\theta_{i}\sin{\phi_i}}{\sin\theta_{i}}\,\partial_{\phi_{i}} \!\!   \\
        -\,\partial_{\phi_{i}} \!\!
    \end{array}\right]
    \end{equation}
The bispherical representation of the operator $\hat{L}^{2}=\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}\boldsymbol{\cdot}\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}$  follows directly from Eq.~\eqref{eq:Lbisph}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:L2bisph}
        \hspace{-15pt}\hat{L}^2 &=& \hat{l}_{1}^{\hspace{1pt}2}\!\! +\! \hat{l}_{2}^{\hspace{1pt}2} -{\scriptstyle 2\sin\left(\phi_{1}\!-\!\phi_{2}\right)}\!\!\left(\!\tfrac{\cos\theta_{2}}{\sin\theta_{2}}\partial_{\theta_{1}\phi_{2}}\!\!-\!\tfrac{\cos\theta_{1}}{\sin\theta_{1}}\partial_{\theta_{2}\phi_{1}}\!\!\right)\!\nonumber \\[-3pt]
        && \hspace{-25pt}-2\!\left(\!1\!+\! \tfrac{\cos\theta_{1}\cos\theta_{2}}{\sin\theta_{1}\sin\theta_{2}} \,  { \scriptstyle \cos\left(\phi_{1}\!-\!\phi_{2}\right)}\!\right) \partial_{\phi_{1}\phi_{2}}\!\!-{\scriptstyle 2\cos\left(\phi_{1}\!-\!\phi_{2}\right)}\,\partial_{\theta_{1}\theta_{2}}
\end{eqnarray}
where 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:l2op}
   \hat{l}_{i}^{\hspace{1pt}2} = -\partial_{\theta_{i}\theta_{i}} - \tfrac{\cos{\theta_{i}}}{\sin\theta_{i}}\,\partial_{\theta_{i}}-\tfrac{1}{\left(\sin\theta_{i}\right)^2}\,\partial_{\phi_{i}\phi_{i}} 
\end{equation}
is the usual square-of-angular-momentum operator of the quasiparticle $i$.

\begin{figure}[htb]
    \centering
    \scalebox{0.8}{\input{body_frame}}
    \caption{The orientation of the body-fixed $(X\,Y\,Z)$ reference is determined by two non-axial vectors $\bm{r}_{1}$ and $\bm{r}_{2}$ lying on the plane $XY$. The origin of the frame is located at the position of the third particle. The $X$-axis coincides with the bisector of the angle $\theta = \arccos\frac{\bm{r}_{1}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\bm{r}_{2}}{r_{1}r_{2}}$, while the $Z$ axis coincides with the vector $\bm{r}_{1}\times\bm{r}_{2}$.}
    \label{fig:body_frame}
\end{figure}
Analogous expressions in the coordinates $(r_{1},r_{2},\theta,\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ can be constructed by introducing the so-called body-fixed frame $(X\,Y\,Z)$ description of the two quasiparticles using three shape variables $\left(r_{1},r_{2},\theta\right)$ (for details, see Fig.~\ref{fig:body_frame}) and relating it to the laboratory-fixed frame $(x\,y\,z)$ by three Euler angles $\left(\alpha,\beta,\gamma\right)$.The positions of the quasiparticles in the frame $(X\,Y\,Z)$ are chosen to be 
\begin{equation} \label{bodyfixed}
    \left[ \begin{array}{c} X_{i} \\ Y_{i} \\ Z_{i} \end{array}\right] = \left[ \begin{array}{c} \phantom{(-1)^i~ }r_{i} ~\cos\left(\frac{\theta}{2}\right) \\[2pt] (-1)^i~ r_{i}~ \sin\left(\frac{\theta}{2}\right)  \\[2pt] \phantom{(-1)^i~ }0 ~\phantom{\sin\left(\frac{\theta}{2}\right)}\end{array}\right]\!\!, \,\,i=1,2    
\end{equation}
The frame $(X\,Y\,Z)$  can be reoriented to match the frame $(x\,y\,z)$  through three consecutive Euler rotations. Here, we use three proper Euler angles $\left(\alpha,\beta,\gamma\right)$ associated with the following sequence of rotations 
\begin{equation}\label{eulerrotation0}
    X\!Y\!Z\! \xrightarrow{\left(Z\phantom{^{'}}\!\!,\gamma\right)} X^{'}\!Y^{'}\!Z^{'} \xrightarrow{\left(Y^{'}\!\!,\beta\right)} X^{''}\!Y^{''}\!Z^{''} \xrightarrow{\left(Z^{''}\!\!,\alpha\right)} x\,y\,z
\end{equation}
where the notation $\left(W,\vartheta\right)$ denotes a counterclockwise rotation by the angle $\vartheta$ about the axis $W$ and where $X^{'}\!Y^{'}\!Z^{'}$ and $X^{''}\!Y^{''}\!Z^{''}$ denote two intermediate coordinate frames in the transition from $\left(X\,Y\,Z\right)$ to $\left(x\,y\,z\right)$ \cite{Biedenharn1984}. The general theory of Euler angle rotations, restricting the values of the Euler angles to $\alpha\in \left[0,2\pi\right]$, $\beta\in \left[0,\pi\right]$, $\gamma\in \left[0,2\pi\right]$ \cite{wigner2013group}, permits to relate the coordinates $\left[X,Y,Z\right]^\mathsf{T}$ of an arbitrary point in the frame $\left(X\,Y\,Z\right)$ to the coordinates $\left[x,y,z\right]^\mathsf{T}$ in the frame $\left(x\,y\,z\right)$  via the following relation originating from Eq.~\eqref{eulerrotation0}
\begin{equation}\label{eulerrotation1}
    \left[\begin{array}{c} x \\ y \\ z \end{array}\right] = \bm{R}_{\texttt{z}}^{\alpha}\,\bm{R}_{\texttt{y}}^{\beta}\,\bm{R}_{\texttt{z}}^{\gamma}\left[\begin{array}{c} X \\ Y \\ Z \end{array}\right]
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}\label{rmatrix}
    \bm{R}_{\texttt{z}}^{\vartheta} \!=\!\! \left[\!\!\begin{array}{ccc}
       \cos{\vartheta}  & \!\!-\!\sin{\vartheta} & \!0\\
       \sin{\vartheta} & \!\!+\!\cos{\vartheta} & \!0\\
       0 & 0 & \!1
    \end{array}\!\!\right]\!\text{ and }\bm{R}_{\texttt{y}}^{\vartheta}\! =\!\! \left[\!\!\begin{array}{ccc}
       \!+\!\cos{\vartheta}  & \!0\! & \sin{\vartheta}\\
       0 & \!1\! & 0\\
       \!-\!\sin{\vartheta}  & \!0\! & \cos{\vartheta}
    \end{array}\!\!\right]
\end{equation}
Eq.~\eqref{eulerrotation1} in combination with Eq.~\eqref{bodyfixed} yields the following transformation relation between the coordinates $\left(x_{1},y_{1},z_{1},x_{2},y_{2},z_{2}\right)$ and $(r_{1},r_{2},\theta,\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$
\begin{equation}\label{ctetrans}
    \left[\begin{array}{c}
         x_{i} \\
         y_{i} \\
         z_{i} 
    \end{array}\right] = r_{i}\!\!\left[\begin{array}{l}
         +\cos\gamma_{i}\cos\beta\cos\alpha-\sin\gamma_{i}\sin\alpha  \\
         +\cos\gamma_{i}\cos\beta\sin\alpha+\sin\gamma_{i}\cos\alpha \\
         -\cos\gamma_{i}\sin\beta
    \end{array}\right]
\end{equation}
where $\gamma_{i}=\left(\gamma+(-1)^{i}\,\frac{\theta}{2}\right)$ and $i=1,2$. 

The explicit form of the operator $\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}$ in Eq.~\eqref{amocc},  expressed in the coordinates $(r_{1},r_{2},\theta,\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$  using the transformation relations given by Eq.~\eqref{ctetrans}, is
\begin{equation}\label{lineuler}
    \hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz} = -\textbf{\textit{i}}\!\left[\begin{array}{c}
         -\frac{\cos\alpha\cos\beta}{\sin\beta}\,\partial_{\alpha}-\sin\alpha\,\partial_{\beta}+\frac{\cos\alpha}{\sin\beta}\,\partial_{\gamma}  \\
         -\frac{\sin\alpha\cos\beta}{\sin\beta}\,\partial_{\alpha}+\cos\alpha\,\partial_{\beta}+\frac{\sin\alpha}{\sin\beta}\,\partial_{\gamma} \\
         \phantom{-\frac{\sin\alpha\cos\beta}{\sin\beta}\,}\partial_{\alpha}\phantom{+\cos\alpha\,\partial_{\beta}+\frac{\sin\alpha}{\sin\beta}\,\partial_{\gamma} }
    \end{array}\right]
\end{equation}
and the corresponding form of the operator $\hat{L}^2$  is
\begin{equation}\label{l2eeler}
    \hat{L}^2 \!=\! \tfrac{-1}{\vphantom{{}^{\dagger}}\left(\sin\beta\right)^2}\!\left(\partial_{\alpha\alpha}\!\!+\!\partial_{\gamma\gamma}\!\!-\!2\cos\beta\,\partial_{\alpha\gamma}\right)-\tfrac{\cos\beta}{\sin\beta}\,\partial_{\beta}-\partial_{\beta\beta}
\end{equation}
It is advantageous for the forthcoming considerations to express the components of the angular momentum operator $\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}$ in the body-fixed frame $\left(X\,Y\,Z\right)$  by defining implicitly  a new operator $\hat{\bm{L}}_{XYZ}=\left[\hat{L}_{X},\hat{L}_{Y},\hat{L}_{Z}\right]^\mathsf{T}$ as
\begin{equation}\label{leularbf}
    \left[\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \hat{L}_{x}  \\
         \hat{L}_{y} \\
         \hat{L}_{z}
    \end{array}\!\!\right] = \bm{R}_{\texttt{z}}^{\alpha}\,\bm{R}_{\texttt{y}}^{\beta}\,\bm{R}_{\texttt{z}}^{\gamma}\left[\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \hat{L}_{X}  \\
         \hat{L}_{Y} \\
         \hat{L}_{Z}
    \end{array}\!\!\right]
\end{equation}
in analogy with Eq.~(\ref{eulerrotation1}). Using the orthogonality of the operator $\bm{R}_{\texttt{z}}^{\alpha}\,\bm{R}_{\texttt{y}}^{\beta}\,\bm{R}_{\texttt{z}}^{\gamma}$, i.e., the fact that $\left[\bm{R}_{\texttt{z}}^{\alpha}\,\bm{R}_{\texttt{y}}^{\beta}\,\bm{R}_{\texttt{z}}^{\gamma}\right]^{-1}=\bm{R}_{\texttt{z}}^{-\gamma}\,\bm{R}_{\texttt{y}}^{-\beta}\,\bm{R}_{\texttt{z}}^{-\alpha}$, it is a straightforward exercise to show that $\hat{\bm{L}}_{XYZ}$ is given by
\begin{equation}\label{LXYZeuler}
    \hat{\bm{L}}_{XYZ}= -\textbf{\textit{i}}\!\left[\!\begin{array}{l}
    +\frac{\cos\gamma\cos\beta}{\sin\beta}\,\partial_{\gamma}+\sin\gamma\,\partial_{\beta}-\frac{\cos\gamma}{\sin\beta}\,\partial_{\alpha}  \\
    -\frac{\sin\gamma\cos\beta}{\sin\beta}\,\partial_{\gamma}+\cos\gamma\,\partial_{\beta}+\frac{\sin\gamma}{\sin\beta}\,\partial_{\alpha} \\
    \phantom{-\frac{\sin\gamma\cos\beta}{\sin\beta}\,}\partial_{\gamma}
    \end{array}\!\right]\!\!
\end{equation}
Direct computation shows that $\hat{\bm{L}}_{XYZ}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\hat{\bm{L}}_{XYZ}\!=\!\hat{L}^2\!=\!\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}$. The operators $\hat{L}_z$ and $\hat{L}_Z$ can be considered then as the projections of the total angular momentum on the laboratory-fixed axis $z$ and the body-fixed axis $Z$, respectively. 

The commutation relations for the components of $\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}$ and $\hat{\bm{L}}_{XYZ}$ are given by $\left[\hat{L}_{v},\hat{L}_{V}\right] = 0$ for $V\in \{X,Y,Z\}$ and $v \in \{x,y,z\}$, in addition to the commutation relations
\begin{equation}\label{LaLb}
     \left[\hat{L}_{x},\hat{L}_{y}\right] \!=\! \textbf{\textit{i}}\hat{L}_{z}\text{, }
    \left[\hat{L}_{y},\hat{L}_{z}\right] \!=\! \textbf{\textit{i}}\hat{L}_{x}\text{, }
    \left[\hat{L}_{z},\hat{L}_{x}\right] \!=\! \textbf{\textit{i}}\hat{L}_{y}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{LALB}
    \left[\hat{L}_{X},\hat{L}_{Y}\right] \!=\! -\textbf{\textit{i}}\hat{L}_{Z}\text{, }
    \left[\hat{L}_{Y},\hat{L}_{Z}\right] \!=\! -\textbf{\textit{i}}\hat{L}_{X}\text{, }
    \left[\hat{L}_{Z},\hat{L}_{X}\right] \!=\! -\textbf{\textit{i}}\hat{L}_{Y}
\end{equation}
and $\left[\hat{L}^2,\hat{L}_{V}\right] =\left[\hat{L}^2,\hat{L}_{v}\right] =0$ expected for the components of angular momentum. (Note, however, the anomalous sign appearing in Eq.~(\ref{LALB}) for the components of $\hat{\bm{L}}_{XYZ}$.) The fact that $\{\hat{L}^2,\hat{L}_z,\hat{L}_Z\}$ form a set of simultaneously commuting operators will be used in the next Section to construct the Wigner functions $\mathcal{D}^{MK}_L(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ spanning the $\left|L,M\right\rangle$ invariant spaces. The construction process becomes particularly simple if we introduce the following four ladder operators
\begin{equation}\label{ladderlabeuler}
    \hat{L}_{\pm} = \hat{L}_{x}\pm\textbf{\textit{i}}\hat{L}_{y} = e^{\pm\textbf{\textit{i}}\alpha}\!\left(\!\textbf{\textit{i}}\,\tfrac{-\partial_{\gamma}\!+\cos\hspace{-1pt}\beta \, \partial_{\alpha}}{\sin\hspace{-1pt}\beta}\pm\partial_{\beta}\!\right)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{ladderbodyeuler}
    \hat{L}^{\pm} = \hat{L}_{X}\mp\textbf{\textit{i}}\hat{L}_{Y} = e^{\pm\textbf{\textit{i}}\gamma}\!\left(\!\textbf{\textit{i}}\,\tfrac{\partial_{\alpha}\!-\cos\hspace{-1pt}\beta \, \partial_{\gamma}}{\sin\hspace{-1pt}\beta}\mp\partial_{\beta}\!\right)
\end{equation}
Note that the unusual sign convention in Eq.~(\ref{ladderbodyeuler}) for $\hat{L}^{\pm}= \hat{L}_{X}\mp\textbf{\textit{i}}\hat{L}_{Y}$ follows from the unusual commutation rules for $\bm{L}_{XYZ}$ appearing in Eq.~\eqref{LALB}.

For completeness, we give also below the explicit representation of the operator $\hat{\bm{L}}_{XYZ}$ in the bispherical coordinates $(r_{1},\theta_{1},\phi_{1},r_{2},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$. Using the connection between  $(r_{1},r_{2},\theta,\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$
and $(r_{1},\theta_{1},\phi_{1},r_{2},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$ implicitly originating from Eqs.~(\ref{ctspc}) and~(\ref{ctetrans})
\begin{eqnarray}\label{bpctoeulr}
    \cos{\theta_{i}} &=&  -\sin{\beta}\cos{\gamma_{i}}\nonumber\\
    \sin{\theta_{i}} &=&  [1-\sin^2{\beta}\cos^2{\gamma_{i}}]^{\frac{1}{2}}\nonumber\\
    \cos{\phi_{i}} &=&  \tfrac{\cos{\gamma_{i}}\cos{\beta}\cos{\alpha}-\sin{\gamma_{i}}\sin{\alpha}}{\sin{\theta_{i}}}\nonumber\\
    \sin{\phi_{i}} &=&  \tfrac{\cos{\gamma_{i}}\cos{\beta}\sin{\alpha}+\sin{\gamma_{i}}\cos{\alpha}}{\sin{\theta_{i}}}
\end{eqnarray}
with $\gamma_{i}=\left(\gamma+(-1)^{i}\,\frac{\theta}{2}\right)$ and $i=1,2$, the resulting formulas, given below in Eqs.~(\ref{eq:lXsph}--\ref{eq:lZsph}),  can be obtained by combining Eq.~(\ref{leularbf}) and Eq.~(\ref{eq:Lbisph}). By considering the scalar
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:r1dotr2} \bm{r_{1}}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\bm{r_{2}} = r_{1}r_{2} \cos \theta 
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:costheta} \cos \theta \hspace{3pt} =  \cos{\theta_{1}}\cos{\theta_{2}}\!+\sin{\theta_{1}}\sin{\theta_{2}}\cos{\left(\phi_{2}\!-\!\phi_{1}\right)}
\end{equation}
and the pseudovector $\bm{Q}=\bm{r_{1}}\times\bm{r_{2}}$ 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:pseudvQ}
   \bm{Q} = r_{1}r_{2}\!\left[\!\!\begin{array}{l}
         \sin{\theta_{1}}\sin{\phi_{1}}\cos{\theta_{2}}\!-\!\sin{\theta_{2}}\sin{\phi_{2}}\cos{\theta_{1}} \\
         \sin{\theta_{2}}\cos{\phi_{2}}\cos{\theta_{1}}\!-\!\sin{\theta_{1}}\cos{\phi_{1}}\cos{\theta_{2}} \\
         \sin{\theta_{1}}\sin{\theta_{2}}\sin{\left(\phi_{2}-\phi_{1}\right)}
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right] 
\end{equation}
with components $\bm{Q}=\left[Q_{x},Q_{y},Q_{z}\right]^\mathsf{T}$ and  magnitude
\begin{eqnarray} \label{eq:Qmagn}
    Q=\vert \bm{Q}\vert&=&r_{1}r_{2} \sin \theta = r_{1}r_{2} \sqrt{1-\cos^2 \theta }
\end{eqnarray}
the inversion of the relations in Eq.~(\ref{ctetrans}) yields
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:eutocart}
    \cos{\theta} &=& \tfrac{\vphantom{|}\bm{r_{1}}\!\text{$\boldsymbol{\cdot}$}\bm{r_{2}}}{r_{1}r_{2}} ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    \sin{\theta} = \tfrac{\vphantom{|}Q}{r_{1}r_{2}}\nonumber\\
    \cos{\tfrac{\theta}{2}} &=& \sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\!\!+\!\!\tfrac{\vphantom{|}\bm{r_{1}}\!\text{$\boldsymbol{\cdot}$}\bm{r_{2}}}{2\,r_{1}r_{2}}}~~~~~~~~~~~
    \sin{\tfrac{\theta}{2}} = \sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\!\!-\!\!\tfrac{\vphantom{|}\bm{r_{1}}\!\text{$\boldsymbol{\cdot}$}\bm{r_{2}}}{2\,r_{1}r_{2}}}\nonumber\\
    \cos{\alpha} &=& \tfrac{\vphantom{\big|}Q_{x}}{\sqrt{Q_{x}^2+Q_{y}^2}} ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    \sin{\alpha} = \tfrac{\vphantom{\big|}Q_{y}}{\sqrt{Q_{x}^2+Q_{y}^2}}\\
    \cos{\beta} &=& \tfrac{\vphantom{|}Q_{z}}{Q} ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    \sin{\beta} = \tfrac{\sqrt{Q_{x}^2+Q_{y}^2}}{Q}\nonumber\\
    \cos{\gamma} &=& \tfrac{\frac{-Q\,\left(\frac{z_{1}\vphantom{y_y}}{r_{1}\vphantom{y_y}}+\frac{z_{2}\vphantom{y_y}}{r_{2}\vphantom{y_y}}\right)}{\sqrt{Q_{x}^2+Q_{y}^2}}}{2\,\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}+\frac{\vphantom{y_y}\bm{r_{1}}\!\text{$\boldsymbol{\cdot}$}\bm{r_{2}}}{2\,r_{1}r_{2}}}} ~~~~~~~~~~
    \sin{\gamma} = \tfrac{\frac{-Q\,\left(\frac{z_{1}\vphantom{y_y}}{r_{1}\vphantom{y_y}}-\frac{z_{2}\vphantom{y_y}}{r_{2}\vphantom{y_y}}\right)}{\sqrt{Q_{x}^2+Q_{y}^2}}}{2\,\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}-\frac{\vphantom{y_y}\bm{r_{1}}\!\text{$\boldsymbol{\cdot}$}\bm{r_{2}}}{2\,r_{1}r_{2}}}}\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
The resulting expressions for the components of $\hat{\bm{L}}_{XYZ}$ in the bispherical coordinates $(r_{1},\theta_{1},\phi_{1},r_{2},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$ are obtained by substituting these formulas into Eq.~(\ref{leularbf}) with $\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}$ given by Eq.~(\ref{eq:Lbisph}). Note that the sequence of algebraic operations required to cast the obtained expressions in the form given below is rather non-obvious; after  some effort, we get
\begin{eqnarray}
    \hat{L}_{X} &=& \frac{-\textbf{\textit{i}}}{2\cos{\frac{\theta}{2}}}\left[\vphantom{\tfrac{\cos{\theta_{2}}}{\sin{\theta_{1}}}} \sin{\left(\phi_{2}\!-\!\phi_{1}\right)}
    \left(\sin{\theta_{2}}\partial_{\theta_{1}}\!\!-\sin{\theta_{1}}\partial_{\theta_{2}}\right)\right.\nonumber\\
    &&\left. +\tfrac{\cos{\theta_{2\vphantom{y}}}-\cos{\theta_{1}}\cos{\theta}}{\sin^{2^{\vphantom{a}}}{\theta_{1}}}\partial_{\phi_{1}}\!\!+\!\tfrac{\cos{\theta_{1}}-\cos{\theta_{2\vphantom{y}}}\cos{\theta}}{\sin^{2^{\vphantom{a}}}{\theta_{2}}}\partial_{\phi_{2}}\right] \label{eq:lXsph}\\
    \hat{L}_{Y} &=& \frac{-\textbf{\textit{i}}}{2\sin{\frac{\theta}{2}}}\left[\vphantom{\tfrac{\cos{\theta_{2}}}{\sin{\theta_{1}}}} \sin{\left(\phi_{2}\!-\!\phi_{1}\right)}
    \left(\sin{\theta_{2}}\partial_{\theta_{1}}\!\!+\sin{\theta_{1}}\partial_{\theta_{2}}\right)\right.\nonumber\\
    &&\left. +\tfrac{\cos{\theta_{2\vphantom{y}}}-\cos{\theta_{1}}\cos{\theta}}{\sin^{2^{\vphantom{a}}}{\theta_{1}}}\partial_{\phi_{1}}\!\!-\!\tfrac{\cos{\theta_{1}}-\cos{\theta_{2\vphantom{y}}}\cos{\theta}}{\sin^{2^{\vphantom{a}}}{\theta_{2}}}\partial_{\phi_{2}}\right]\label{eq:lYsph}\\
    \hspace{-10pt}\hat{L}_{Z} &=& \frac{-\textbf{\textit{i}}}{\sin{\theta}} \!\left[\sin{\left(\phi_{2}\!\!-\!\phi_{1}\right)} \left( \tfrac{\sin{\theta_{2\vphantom{y}}}}{\sin{\theta_1}}\partial_{\phi_1} \!\!+ \tfrac{\sin{\theta_{1\vphantom{y}}}}{\sin{\theta_2}}\partial_{\phi_2}\!\right)\right.\nonumber\\
    &&\left. -\tfrac{\cos{\theta_{2\vphantom{y}}}-\cos{\theta_1}\cos{\theta}}{\sin{\theta_1}}\partial_{\theta_1}\!\!
    +\!\tfrac{\cos{\theta_{1\vphantom{y}}}-\cos{\theta_2}\cos{\theta}}{\sin{\theta_2}}\partial_{\theta_2}\vphantom{\tfrac{\sin{\theta_2}}{\sin{\theta_2}}}\right] \label{eq:lZsph}
\end{eqnarray}
where $\cos \theta$ is given by Eq.~(\ref{eq:costheta}), $\sin \theta$ by Eq.~(\ref{eq:Qmagn}), and $\sin \frac{\theta}{2}$ and $\cos \frac{\theta}{2}$ by Eq.~(\ref{eq:eutocart}).

The expressions for $\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}$ and $\hat{\bm{L}}_{XYZ}$ in the bispherical coordinates $(r_{1},\theta_{1},\phi_{1},r_{2},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$, given in Eq.~(\ref{eq:Lbisph}) and Eqs.~(\ref{eq:lXsph}--\ref{eq:lZsph}), respectively, can be used to construct the corresponding ladder operators $\hat{L}_{\pm}$ and $\hat{\!L}^{\pm}$; we get
\begin{eqnarray}\label{ladderlabbsph}
    \hat{L}_{\pm} &=& \hat{L}_{x}\pm \textbf{\textit{i}}\hat{L}_{y} = \sum_{i=1}^{2} e^{\pm\textbf{\textit{i}}\phi_{i}} \left(\frac{\textbf{\textit{i}}\cos\theta_{i}}{\sin\theta_{i}}\partial_{\phi_{i}}\pm\partial_{\theta_{i}}\right) \\
    \label{ladderbodybsph} \hat{\!L}^{\pm} & = & \hat{L}_{X}\mp\textbf{\textit{i}}\hat{L}_{Y} =\\
    &\pm &
    \frac{e^{\pm\textbf{\textit{i}}\frac{\theta}{2}}}{\sin{\theta}} \left(\!
    \sin{\left(\phi_{1}\!-\!\phi_{2}\right)}
    \sin{\theta_{2}}\partial_{\theta_{1}} \!\!-\! \tfrac{\cos{\theta_{2\vphantom{y}}}-\cos{\theta_{1}}\cos{\theta}}{\sin^{2^{\vphantom{a}}}{\theta_{1}}}\partial_{\phi_{1}}\!\right)\nonumber\\
    &\pm & \frac{e^{\mp\textbf{\textit{i}}\frac{\theta}{2}}}{\sin{\theta}}\left(\! \sin{\left(\phi_{1}\!-\!\phi_{2}\right)}
    \sin{\theta_{1}}\partial_{\theta_{2}} \!\!+\! \tfrac{\cos{\theta_{1}}-\cos{\theta_{2\vphantom{y}}}\cos{\theta}}{\sin^{2^{\vphantom{a}}}{\theta_{2}}}\partial_{\phi_{2}}\!\right)\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}

\subsection{Wigner functions \texorpdfstring{$\mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$}~ }
The Wigner functions $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ \cite{wigner2013group, BHATIA1964} are the simultaneous eigenfunctions of the angular momentum operators $\hat{L}^2$, $\hat{L}_{z}$, and $\hat{L}_{Z}$ as given in Eq.~\eqref{l2eeler},~\eqref{lineuler}, and~\eqref{LXYZeuler}, respectively. They are labeled using three quantum numbers $L$, $M$ and $K$ corresponding to three commuting operators.  The corresponding eigenvalue equations are
\begin{equation}\label{L2LzLZdmkl}
    \begin{aligned}
    \hat{L}^{2}\,\mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma) &= L(L+1)\,\mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)\\
    \hat{L}_{z}\,\mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma) &= M\,\mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)\\
    \hat{L}_{Z}\,\mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma) &= K\,\mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Since $\hat{L}_{Z}$ corresponds to the rotation about the body-fixed axis $Z$, the eigenvalue $K$ can be interpreted as the projection of the total angular momentum $L$ on the body-fixed axis $Z$. Similarly,  $M$ can be interpreted as the projection of the total angular momentum $L$ on the laboratory-fixed axis $z$. Consequently, the quantum numbers $M$ and $K$ take on $2L+1$ distinct integer values: $M,K\in\{-L,\ldots,L\}$.

The solution to Eqs.~\eqref{L2LzLZdmkl} can be obtained in the usual way using the separation of variables and the Frebenius method. The physically meaningful solutions $\mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ can be expressed as
\begin{eqnarray}\label{wignerd}
    \mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)&=&\mathscr{N}^{MK}_{L}  \left(\tfrac{1+\cos{\beta}}{2}\right) ^ {\frac{\vert  K + M \vert}{2} }\left(\tfrac{1-\cos{\beta}}{2}\right) ^ {\frac{\vert  K - M \vert}{2} }\nonumber\\
    &&\hspace{-40pt}e^{\textbf{\textit{i}} M \alpha} e^{\textbf{\textit{i}} K \gamma} {}_2F_1\left[\!\begin{array}{c}
         {\scriptstyle -L+\lambda,\, L+1+\lambda} \\
         {\scriptstyle{1+\left|K+M\right|}}
    \end{array};\tfrac{1+\cos\beta}{2}\right]
\end{eqnarray}
where $\lambda = \max\left(\left|K\right|\!,\!\left|M\right|\right) = \frac{\left|K+M\right|+\left|K-M\right|}{2}$, and where ${}_2F_1$ denotes Gauss hypergeometric function \cite{slater1966}
\begin{equation}\label{ghgf}
    {}_2F_1\left[\!\begin{array}{c}
     a,b  \\
     c
\end{array};x\right]=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{(a)_{k}(b)_{k}}{(c)_{k}}\frac{x^k}{k!}
\end{equation}
with 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:poch}
    (\alpha)_k\!=\!\alpha(\alpha\hspace{-1pt}+\!1)\cdot\! \ldots\!\cdot(\alpha\hspace{-1pt}+\!k\!-\!1) 
\end{equation}
denoting Pochhammer symbols \cite{slater1966}. Since $-L+\lambda\leq 0$, one of the upper indices of ${}_2F_1$ in Eq.~\eqref{wignerd} is always a non-positive integer, which signifies that ${}_2F_1$ terminates and reduces to a polynomial of degree $L-\lambda$ in the variable $\cos\beta$. The normalization constant 
\begin{eqnarray}\label{ncwignerd}
    \mathscr{N}^{MK}_{L} &=& \tfrac{\scriptstyle (-1)^{\frac{\left|K+M\right|+K-M}{2}}}{2\pi}\left[\tfrac{\left(2L+1\right)}{2}\right]^{\frac{1}{2}}\nonumber\\
    &&\cdot\!\left[\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle L+\frac{\left|K+M\right|+K-M}{2}  \\
         \scriptstyle \left|K+M\right| 
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
          \scriptstyle {L+\frac{\left|K+M\right|-K+M}{2}}\\
          \scriptstyle \left|K+M\right|
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\right]^{\frac{1}{2}}
\end{eqnarray}
orthonormalizes the functions $\mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L}\equiv \mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ 
\begin{equation}\label{oncwd}
    \left\langle\mathcal{D}^{M^{'}K^{'}}_{L^{'}} \right|\left.\!\vphantom{\mathcal{D}^{M^{'}K^{'}}_{L^{'}}}\mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L} \right\rangle = \delta_{LL^{'}}\delta_{MM^{'}}\delta_{KK^{'}}
\end{equation}
with respect to the following integral
\begin{equation}\label{dtau}
    \left\langle f\vphantom{g} \right|\left.\!g\vphantom{f} \right\rangle =\!\!\int\limits_{0}^{2\pi}\hspace{-4pt} d\alpha\! \int\limits_{0}^{\pi}\hspace{-3pt}\sin{\beta}\,d\beta \hspace{-3pt}\int\limits_{0}^{2\pi}\hspace{-3pt} d\gamma \left(f^\ast \cdot g\right)
\end{equation}
where $f^\ast$ denotes the complex conjugate of $f$.

Alternatively, the set of the Wigner functions $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ corresponding to a definite value of $L$ can be generated recursively using the ladder operators defined in Eq.~\eqref{ladderlabeuler} and Eq.~\eqref{ladderbodyeuler} from the maximal Wigner function $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{LL}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ given by a particularly simple expression
\begin{equation} \label{eq:DLLL}
    \mathcal{D}_{L}^{LL}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma) \!=\! (-1)^{\!L}\, \tfrac{\sqrt{L+\frac{1}{2}}}{2\pi}\,e^{\textbf{\textit{i}}L\left(\alpha+\gamma\right)}\!\left(\tfrac{1+\cos\beta}{2}\right)^{\!\!L}
\end{equation}
The action of ladder operators $\hat{L}_{\pm}$ and $\hat{L}^{\pm}$, defined originally by Eqs.~(\ref{ladderlabeuler}) and~(\ref{ladderbodyeuler}), on $\mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L}$
\begin{equation} \label{eq:Lpmdown}
    \hat{L}_{\pm}~\mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L} = \sqrt{L\left(L+1\right)-M\left(M\pm1\right)}~\mathcal{D}^{M\pm1,K}_{L}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation} \label{eq:Lpmup}
    \hat{L}^{\pm}~\mathcal{D}^{MK}_{L} = \sqrt{L\left(L+1\right)-K\left(K\pm1\right)}~\mathcal{D}^{M,K\pm1}_{L}
\end{equation}
enabled owing to the choice of the phase factor $(-1)^{\frac{\left|K+M\right|+K-M}{2}}$ in Eq.~\eqref{ncwignerd} gives
\begin{equation} \label{eq:DLMKalt}
    \mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK} = b_{MK}^{-1} ~\underbrace{\hat{L}_{-}\ldots \hat{L}_{-}}_{L-M~\text{times}}\,\underbrace{\vphantom{\hat{L}_{-}}\hat{L}^{-}\ldots \hat{L}^{-}}_{L-K~\text{times}}~\mathcal{D}_{L}^{LL}
\end{equation}
where the numerical factor $b_{MK}$ is given by
\begin{eqnarray} \label{eq:bMK}
    b_{MK} &=&  {\textstyle \prod_{\mu=M+1}^{L}}\sqrt{L\left(L+1\right)-\mu\left(\mu-1\right)}\\ &\hspace{5pt}\cdot&{\textstyle \prod_{\kappa=K+1}^{L}}\sqrt{L\left(L+1\right)-\kappa\left(\kappa-1\right)}\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
Of course, the choice of the maximal Wigner function $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{LL}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ in Eqs.~(\ref{eq:DLLL}) and~(\ref{eq:DLMKalt}) and the operators $L_{-}$ and $L^{-}$ in Eq.~(\ref{eq:DLMKalt}) is arbitrary; one could equally well start the construction process for example from the maximal Wigner function $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{L,-L}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$
\begin{equation} \label{eq:DLL-L}
    \mathcal{D}_{L}^{L,-L}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma) \!=\! (-1)^{\!L}\, \tfrac{\sqrt{L+\frac{1}{2}}}{2\pi}\,e^{\textbf{\textit{i}}L\left(\alpha-\gamma\right)}\!\left(\tfrac{1-\cos\beta}{2}\right)^{\!\!L}
\end{equation}
and the operators $\hat{L}_{-}$ and $\hat{L}^{+}$.

\subsection{Parity and antisymmetry}
The parity operation corresponds to the simultaneous inversion of the coordinates of the quasiparticles. The inversion operator $\hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}: \left[x,y,z\right]^\mathsf{T} \mapsto \left[-x,-y,-z\right]^\mathsf{T}$ has the following effect on functions of the vectors $\bm{r}_{1}$ and $\bm{r}_{2}$
\begin{equation}\label{invop}
\begin{aligned}
    \hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\left(\bm{r}_{1}\right)&\rightarrow -\bm{r}_{1},~~~  \hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\left(\bm{r}_{1}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\bm{r}_{2}\right)~\rightarrow \bm{r}_{1}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\bm{r}_{2}\\
    \hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\left(\bm{r}_{2}\right)&\rightarrow -\bm{r}_{2},~~~  \hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\left(\bm{r}_{1}\!\!\times\!\bm{r}_{2}\right)\rightarrow \bm{r}_{1}\!\times\bm{r}_{2}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Consequently, the behavior of the sine and cosine functions of the Euler angles under the inversion operation, as derived using Eqs.~\eqref{eq:eutocart}, is given by
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        \hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\left(\sin{\alpha}\right) &\rightarrow +\sin{\alpha},~~~~ \hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\left(\cos{\alpha}\right) \rightarrow +\cos{\alpha}\\
        \hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\left(\sin{\beta}\right) &\rightarrow +\sin{\beta},~~~~ \hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\left(\cos{\beta}\right) \rightarrow +\cos{\beta}\\
        \hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\left(\sin{\gamma}\right) &\rightarrow -\sin{\gamma},~~~~ \hat{\bm{i}}\left(\cos{\gamma}\right) \rightarrow -\cos{\gamma}
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
This suggests the following transformation properties of the Euler angles under inversion
\begin{equation}
    \hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\left(\alpha\right)\rightarrow\alpha,~~~ \hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\left(\beta\right)\rightarrow\beta, ~~~\hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\left(\gamma\right)\rightarrow\gamma+\pi
\end{equation}
These results determine the behavior of the Wigner functions $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ under inversion as
\begin{equation} \label{eq:wignerdpar}
  \hspace{1pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\left(\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)\right) \,\,\rightarrow \,\, \left(-1\right)^{K}\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma) 
\end{equation}
because according to Eq.~(\ref{wignerd})  $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma+\pi) = e^{\textbf{\textit{i}} K \pi}\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$. This signifies that the Wigner functions $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ possess even space parity when $K$ is even, and odd space parity when $K$ is odd.

In case when two out of three particles (say  1 and 2) are identical, the system possesses an additional discrete symmetry: antisymmetry under the permutation  $\hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}$ of the identical fermions. The effect of the permutation operator $\hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}$ on functions of the vectors $\bm{r}_{1}$ and $\bm{r}_{2}$
\begin{equation}\label{permutop}
\begin{aligned}
    \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\left(\bm{r}_{1}\right)&\rightarrow \bm{r}_{2},~~~  \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\left(\bm{r}_{1}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\bm{r}_{2}\right)~\rightarrow \phantom{-}\bm{r}_{1}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\bm{r}_{2}\\
    \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\left(\bm{r}_{2}\right)&\rightarrow \bm{r}_{1},~~~  \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\left(\bm{r}_{1}\!\!\times\!\bm{r}_{2}\right)\rightarrow -\bm{r}_{1}\!\times\bm{r}_{2}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
stipulates according to Eqs.~\eqref{eq:eutocart} that
\begin{equation} \label{eq:sincosperm}
    \begin{aligned}
        \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\left(\sin{\alpha}\right) &\rightarrow -\sin{\alpha},~~~~ \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\left(\cos{\alpha}\right) \rightarrow -\cos{\alpha}\\
        \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\left(\sin{\beta}\right) &\rightarrow +\sin{\beta},~~~~ \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\left(\cos{\beta}\right) \rightarrow -\cos{\beta}\\
        \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\left(\sin{\gamma}\right) &\rightarrow -\sin{\gamma},~~~~ \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\left(\cos{\gamma}\right) \rightarrow +\cos{\gamma}
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
which conveys the following properties of the Euler angles $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$ under permutation 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:abgperm}
    \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\!\left(\alpha\right)\!\rightarrow\alpha\!+\!\pi,\,\,\,\hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\!\left(\beta\right)\!\rightarrow\pi\! -\! \beta, \,\,\,\hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\!\left(\gamma\right)\!\rightarrow2\pi\!-\!\gamma
\end{equation}
Following Wigner (p.~216 of \cite{wigner2013group}) and Edmonds (Sec.~4.2 of \cite{Edmonds1974}), it can be established that
\begin{equation} \label{eq:wignerdperm}
  \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\!\left(\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)\right) \,\,\rightarrow \,\, \left(-1\right)^{L}\mathcal{D}_{L}^{M,-K}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma) 
\end{equation}
An elementary demonstration of this fact can be also obtained from the action of the operator $\hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}$ onto the Wigner function $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}$ given by Eq.~(\ref{eq:DLMKalt}), and onto
the operators $\hat{L}_{\pm}$ and $\hat{L}^{\pm}$ given by Eqs.~(\ref{ladderlabeuler}) and~(\ref{ladderbodyeuler})
\begin{equation} \label{eq:Lpmperm}
    \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\!\left(\hat{L}^{\pm}\right)\!\rightarrow \hat{L}^{\mp},\hspace{20pt}\hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\!\left(\hat{L}_{\pm}\right)\!\rightarrow \hat{L}_{\pm}
\end{equation}
easily established taking into account Eq.~(\ref{eq:sincosperm}) and the relations $\hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\!\left(\partial_\alpha\right)\!\rightarrow\partial_\alpha$, $\hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\!\left(\partial_\beta\right)\!\rightarrow\!-\partial_\beta$, and  $\hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\!\left(\partial_\gamma\right)\!\rightarrow\!-\partial_\gamma$ following naturally from Eq.~(\ref{eq:abgperm}). We have
\[
  \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\!\left(\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}\right) \,\,\rightarrow \,\, = b_{MK}^{-1} ~\underbrace{\hat{L}_{-}\ldots \hat{L}_{-}}_{L-M~\text{times}}\,\underbrace{\vphantom{\hat{L}_{-}}\hat{L}^{+}\ldots \hat{L}^{+}}_{L-K~\text{times}}~\hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\!\left(\mathcal{D}_{L}^{LL}\right) 
\]
It  follows from Eqs.~(\ref{eq:DLLL}),~(\ref{eq:sincosperm}), and~(\ref{eq:DLL-L}) that
\[
\hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\!\left(\mathcal{D}_{L}^{LL}\right) = (-1)^{\!L}~ \mathcal{D}_{L}^{L,-L}  
\]
The proof of Eq.~(\ref{eq:wignerdperm}) follows immediately from the definition of $b_{MK}$ in Eq.~(\ref{eq:bMK}) and the usual ladder algebra generated by Eqs.~(\ref{eq:Lpmup}) and~(\ref{eq:Lpmdown}) by the operators $\hat{L}_\pm$ and $\hat{L}^\pm$, if one uses the fact that 
\[
\prod_{\kappa=K+1}^{L}\hspace{-7pt}\sqrt{L\left(L\!+\!1\right)\!-\!\kappa\left(\kappa\!-\!1\right)} = \hspace{-5pt}\prod^{-K-1}_{\kappa=-L}\hspace{-5pt}\sqrt{L\left(L\!+\!1\right)\!-\!\kappa\left(\kappa\!+\!1\right)}
\] 

\subsection{Minimal bipolar harmonics \texorpdfstring{$\bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LM\uppi}(\theta_{1},\phi_{1},\theta_2,\phi_2)$}~  }

Consider two quasiparticles carrying individually angular momenta $l_1$ and $l_2$. They are usually described by two spherical harmonics (SHs) $Y_{m_1}^{l_1}(\theta_1,\phi_1)$ and $Y_{m_2}^{l_2}(\theta_2,\phi_2)$ defined implicitly by the eigenequations
\begin{eqnarray}
  \hat{l}_{i}^{\hspace{1pt}2} ~Y_{m_i}^{l_i}(\theta_i,\phi_i) & = & l_{i}(l_{i}\hspace{-2pt}+\hspace{-2pt}1) ~Y_{m_i}^{l_i}(\theta_i,\phi_i) \\
  \hat{l}_{iz} ~Y_{m_i}^{l_i}(\theta_i,\phi_i) & = & m_i ~Y_{m_i}^{l_i}(\theta_i,\phi_i)
\end{eqnarray}
with $\hat{l}_{i}^{\hspace{1pt}2}$ given by Eq.~(\ref{eq:l2op}) and $\hat{l}_{iz}=-\textbf{\textit{i}}\partial_{\phi_{i}}$ implied by Eqs.~(\ref{amocc}) and~(\ref{eq:Lbisph}), and explicitly by
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:sph}
    Y_{m}^{l}(\theta,\phi) &=& N_{lm}e^{\textbf{\textit{i}}m\phi}(\sin{\theta})^{\vert m\vert}\nonumber\\
    && {}_2F_1\left[\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         {\scriptstyle -l+\left|m\right|,l+\left|m \right|+1} \\
         {\scriptstyle{1+\left|m\right|}}
    \end{array}\!\!;\tfrac{1+\cos\theta}{2}\right]
\end{eqnarray}
where $N_{lm}$ is the normalized phase factor
\begin{eqnarray}
    N_{lm} = \frac{\left(-1\right)^{l+\frac{m-\vert m\vert }{2}}}{\vert m\vert ! ~2^{1+\vert m\vert}}~\sqrt{\frac{(2l+1)(l+\vert m\vert)!}{\pi~(l-\vert m\vert)!}}
\end{eqnarray}
Coupling of these two individual momenta produces total angular momentum $L$ that can take any integer value from $\left| l_1 - l_2 \right|$ to $l_1+l_2$. The resulting eigenfunctions $\Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M}\equiv \Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}\vphantom{y}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M} \hspace{-0.7pt}(\theta_{1}\hspace{-0.5pt},\phi_{1}\hspace{-0.7pt},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$, usually referred to in the literature as the bispherical harmonics (BHs), have the following eigenproperties 
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq: mbheve}
    &\hat{L}^{\hspace{-0.7pt}2}~\Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M} = L\hspace{-0.5pt}(L\hspace{-2.5pt}+\hspace{-3pt}1)\hspace{2pt}\Omega_{l_{1}  \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M} & \hspace{20pt}\hat{L}_{z}\hspace{2pt}\Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M} =M\hspace{2pt}\Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M} \\
    &\hat{l}_{i}^{\hspace{1pt}2}\hspace{3pt}\Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M} =\hspace{1pt}l_{i}\hspace{-1pt}(l_{i}\hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-3pt}1)\hspace{2pt}\Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M} & \hspace{23pt}\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\hspace{7pt}\Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M} =(-1)^{l_1\hspace{-1.4pt}+\hspace{-0.6pt}l_2}\hspace{2pt}\Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M} \nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
BHs are constructed from products of the individual SHs using the usual angular momentum coupling scheme
\begin{equation}\label{bsheqn0}
\Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M} = 
\sum_{\mu}
C_{l_1,\mu,l_2,M\hspace{-1.5pt}-\hspace{-1pt}\mu}^{LM}~
Y_{\mu}^{l_1}\!(\theta_{1},\!\phi_{1}\hspace{-0.7pt}) ~
Y_{M\!-\!\mu}^{l_2}\hspace{-1.5pt}(\theta_{2},\!\phi_{2}\hspace{-0.5pt})
\end{equation}
where a compact form of the Clebsch-Gordan  coefficients $C_{l_1\!,m_1\!,l_2\hspace{-0.6pt},m_2}^{LM}$ was given by Shimpuku in Eq.~(1) of \cite{Shimpuku1963} as
\begin{eqnarray}
    &&C_{l_1\!,m_1\!,l_2\hspace{-0.6pt},m_2}^{LM} = \sqrt{\tfrac{\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2l_{1}  \\
         \scriptstyle l_{1}+l_{2}-L
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle  2l_{2}  \\
         \scriptstyle l_{1}+l_{2}-L
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)}{\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle l_{1}+l_{2}+L+1  \\
         \scriptstyle l_{1}+l_{2}-L
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2l_{1}  \\
         \scriptstyle l_{1}-m_{1}
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2l_{2}  \\
         \scriptstyle l_{2}-m_{2}
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2L  \\
         \scriptstyle L-M
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)}}\nonumber\\
    &&\hspace{30pt}\cdot\hspace{-5pt}\sum_{\kappa=0}^{l_{1}\!-\hspace{-0.5pt}m_{1}}\hspace{-5pt}\left(-1\right)^{\kappa}\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle l_{1}+l_{2}-L  \\
         \scriptstyle \kappa
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle l_{1}-l_{2}+L  \\
         \scriptstyle l_{1}-m_{1}-\kappa
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle -l_{1}+l_{2}+L  \\
         \scriptstyle l_{2}+m_{2}-\kappa
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right) \label{eq:Ccoef}
\end{eqnarray}
when $\left| l_1 - l_2 \right| \leq L \leq l_1+l_2$ and $m_1+m_2=M$, and as $C_{l_1\!,m_1\!,l_2\hspace{-0.6pt},m_2}^{LM} = 0$ in the remaining cases.

For any  values of $L$, $M$, and parity $\uppi=(-1)^d$ with  $d\in\{0,1\}$, the set 
\begin{equation}
    U^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} = \left\{ \Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}: l_1,l_2 \in \mathbb{N}_0  \right\}
\end{equation}
contains infinitely many elements. However, as Schwartz first observed (see Appendix~I of \cite{Schwartz1961}) and King \cite{King1967} and later Manakov, Marmo, and Meremianin (see Sec.~2.2 of~\cite{Manakov1996}) demonstrated, the set $U^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}$ can be generated from its finite subset 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:Ugen}
    \mathscr{U}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} = \left\{\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}: l=d,\ldots,L \right\} \subset U^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}
\end{equation}
consisting of only a fixed number of $L+1-d$ generators $\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} \equiv \Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}  \hspace{-0.7pt}(\theta_{1}\hspace{-0.5pt},\phi_{1}\hspace{-0.7pt},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$, whose explicit definition  is given in Eq.~(\ref{bsheqn1}) below. This set of generators,  $\mathscr{U}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}$, is referred to as the  minimal bipolar harmonics (MBHs) \cite{Schwartz1961,MEREMIANIN2003, King1967, Manakov1998} with angular momentum $L$ and $M$ and parity $\uppi$. The generators $\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}$ span the $\left|L,M,\uppi\right\rangle$ invariant subspaces, which signifies that every function $\Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of the generators $\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} $ with some coefficients $b_l=b_l(\cos \theta)$ depending only on the internal shape angle $\theta$ defined implicitly by Eq.~(\ref{eq:costheta}) and depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:body_frame}.  

 The parameter $d$ appearing earlier in Eqs.~(\ref{wfa1}),~(\ref{eq:Ugen}), and~(\ref{wfa2}) can be treated as a quantum number corresponding to definite parity $\uppi$ 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:d}
    d = \left\{ \begin{array}{llr}
        0 & \text{for }  \,\uppi\!=\!n\!\equiv\!\phantom{-}(-1)^L &\text{(natural parity)} \\
        1 & \text{for } \,\uppi\!=\!u\!\equiv\!-(-1)^L &\text{(unnatural parity)} 
    \end{array}\right.
\end{equation}
The reader is reminded here that the term ``natural'' signifies  that the parity $\uppi=(-1)^L$ of a many-particle state with angular momentum $L$ is a natural extension of the parity $(-1)^l$ expected for a single-particle state with angular momentum $l$ corresponding to a spherical harmonic $Y_m^l(\theta,\phi$). Note that in the process of constructing quantum angular theory of many particles the designation of states with natural ($n$) and unnatural ($u$) parity leads to a more transparent way of presenting the final formulas than the usual designation of states with even ($e$) and odd ($o$) parity used commonly in the literature on the subject.

For definite values of $L$, $M$, $\uppi$ and $l$, the explicit form of MBH is given as a linear combination of products of two spherical harmonics
\begin{equation}\label{bsheqn1}
\Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi} \!=\hspace{-8pt} 
\sum_{\mu=\mu_{\text{min}}}^{\mu_{\text{max}}}\hspace{-6pt}
C_{l\hspace{-0.8pt},\mu\hspace{-0.8pt},L\hspace{-0.6pt}+\hspace{-0.6pt}d\hspace{-0.8pt}-\hspace{-0.8pt}l\hspace{-0.8pt},M\!-\!\mu}^{LM\uppi}\,
Y_{\mu}^{l}\hspace{-1pt}(\theta_{1}\hspace{-0.5pt},\hspace{-1pt}\phi_{1}\hspace{-0.7pt}) \,
Y_{M\!-\!\mu}^{L\hspace{-0.6pt}+\hspace{-0.6pt}d\hspace{-0.8pt}-\hspace{-0.8pt}l}\hspace{-1pt}(\theta_{2},\!\phi_{2}\hspace{-0.5pt})
\end{equation}
where $\mu_{\text{min}}=-\min(l,L+d-l-M)$ and $\mu_{\text{max}}=\min(l,L+d-l+M)$, and where the parity-adapted Clebsch-Gordan coefficient $C_{l_1\!,m_1\!,l_2\hspace{-0.6pt},m_2}^{LM\uppi}$ can be obtained from Eq.~(\ref{eq:Ccoef}) in the following compact form 
\begin{equation}
    C_{l_1\!,m_1\!,l_2\hspace{-0.6pt},m_2}^{LM\uppi}\!\! =\!\! \sum_{\kappa=0}^{d}\!\!\tfrac{(-1)^{\kappa}\!\!\sqrt{\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2l_{1}  \\
         \scriptstyle d
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2l_{2}  \\
         \scriptstyle d
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)}\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2l_{1}-d  \\
         \scriptstyle l_{1}-m_{1}-\kappa 
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2l_{2}-d  \\
         \scriptstyle l_{2}+m_{2}-\kappa 
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)}{\sqrt{\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2L+1+d  \\
         \scriptstyle d 
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2l_{1}  \\
         \scriptstyle l_{1}-m_{1}
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2l_{2}  \\
         \scriptstyle l_{2}-m_{2}
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2L  \\
         \scriptstyle L-M
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!}}
\end{equation}
The orthonormalization conditions for MBHs are
\begin{eqnarray} \label{eq:omegaorth}
    \left\langle\Omega_{l'}^{L'M'\pi} \right|\left.\!\vphantom{\Omega_{l'}^{L'M'\pi}}\Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi} \right\rangle = \delta_{LL^{'}}\delta_{MM^{'}}\delta_{ll^{'}}
\end{eqnarray}
where $\langle \cdot| \cdot \rangle$ denotes the following integral
\begin{equation}\label{bipolnormcondunp}
    \left\langle f\vphantom{g} \right|\left.\!g\vphantom{f} \right\rangle =\!\!\int\limits_{0}^{2\pi} \hspace{-3pt}d\phi_{1\!\!}\int\limits_{0}^{\pi}\hspace{-2.5pt} \sin{\theta_{1}}\,d\theta_{1}\!\!\int\limits_{0}^{2\pi} \hspace{-3pt}d\phi_{2}\!\!\int\limits_{0}^{\pi}\hspace{-2.5pt} \sin{\theta_{2}}\,d\theta_{2} \left(f^\ast \cdot g\right)
\end{equation}

It is easy to verify that MBHs have the following eigen and symmetry properties
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq: mbheprop}
    \hat{l}_{1}^{\hspace{1pt}2}\hspace{3pt}\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} &=& \hspace{1pt}l(l\hspace{-2.5pt}+\hspace{-3pt}1)\hspace{2pt}\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}  \nonumber \\
    \hat{l}_{2}^{\hspace{1pt}2}\hspace{3pt}\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} &=&\hspace{1pt}\left(L\hspace{-2.2pt}-\hspace{-2.2pt}l\hspace{-2.2pt}+\hspace{-2pt}d\right)\hspace{-1pt}(L\hspace{-2.2pt}-\hspace{-2.2pt}l\hspace{-2.2pt}+\hspace{-2pt}d\hspace{-2.5pt}+\hspace{-3pt}1)\hspace{2pt}\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}   \nonumber\\
    \hat{L}^{\hspace{-0.7pt}2}~\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} &=& L(L\hspace{-2.2pt}+\hspace{-3pt}1)\hspace{2pt}\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}\\  
    \hspace{20pt}\hat{L}_{z}\hspace{2pt}\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} &=& M\hspace{2pt}\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} \nonumber \\
    \hat{\bm{p}}_{{12}}\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} &=& (-1)^d\hspace{2pt}\Omega_{L-l+d}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} \hspace{15pt}\nonumber\\
    \hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}\hspace{7pt}\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} &=& (-1)^{L\hspace{-1.4pt}+\hspace{-0.6pt}d}\hspace{2pt}\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} \nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
in large part induced by Eqs.~(\ref{eq: mbheve}) and by the inclusion relation $\mathscr{U}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}  \subset U^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}$. The action of the ladder operators $\hat{L}_{\pm}$ defined in Eq.~(\ref{ladderlabbsph}) on $\Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi}$ is given by 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:ladderM}
    \hat{L}_{\pm}~\Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi} = \sqrt{L\left(L+1\right)-M\left(M\pm1\right)}~\Omega_{l}^{LM\hspace{-0.8pt}\pm\hspace{-0.7pt} 1\hspace{0.5pt}\pi}
\end{equation}
Note that the ladder operators $\hat{L}^{\pm}$ defined in Eq.~(\ref{ladderbodybsph}) do not play any pronounced role in the theory of MBHs, mainly because the generators $\Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi}$ are not eigenfunctions of the operator $L_Z$ defined in Eq.~(\ref{eq:lZsph}), in contrast to the Wigner functions $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$. This signifies that every $\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}  \hspace{-0.7pt}(\theta_{1}\hspace{-0.5pt},\phi_{1}\hspace{-0.7pt},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$ with $l\in\left\{d,\ldots,L \right\}$ corresponds to a linear combination of Wigner functions $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ with $K\in \left\{ -L,\ldots,L \right\}$ and with coefficients being functions of $\theta$; a detailed discussion of this dependence is given later in the text following  Eq.~(\ref{mbhwdr}). 

\subsection{Relation between the Wigner functions \texorpdfstring{$\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$}~ and minimal bipolar harmonics \texorpdfstring{$\Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi}(\theta_{1},\phi_{1},\theta_2,\phi_2)$}~  }

Both sets of generators, 
$\left\{\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}: K=-L,\ldots,L\right\}$
and
$\left\{\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}n}  \hspace{-0.7pt}: l=0,\ldots,L\right\} \cup \left\{\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}u}  \hspace{-0.7pt}: l=1,\ldots,L\right\}$, span the same $(2L$+$1)$-dimensional, $\left|L,M\right\rangle$ invariant space comprising of functions of both natural and unnatural---or alternatively: even and odd---parity. Consequently, it is possible to express one set of generators as linear combinations of the other one and vice versa. In the further part of the current exposition we need to express a general MBH $\Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi}(\theta_{1},\phi_{1},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$  as a linear combination of the Wigner functions $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ in Eq.~(\ref{angularint1}) defining the angular integrals $\mathscr{W}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{L\uppi}$. Such an expansion can be written as
\begin{equation}\label{mbhwdr}
    \Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi}(\theta_{1},\phi_{1},\theta_{2},\phi_{2}) =\!\!\! \sum_{K=-L}^{L}\!\!\!\Lambda_{Kl}^{L\uppi}\!\left(\theta\right)\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}\!\!\left(\alpha,\beta,\gamma\right)
\end{equation}
where the expansion coefficients \begin{eqnarray}\label{intlambda}
    \Lambda_{Kl}^{L\uppi}\left(\theta\right) = \left\langle\left.\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}\right|\Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi}\right\rangle
\end{eqnarray}
with the inner product  $\langle \cdot| \cdot \rangle$ defined in Eq.~\eqref{dtau} are functions of $\theta$ (as we demonstrate below). It is to be noted that owing to the parity symmetry properties of the Wigner functions $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}\!\!\left(\alpha,\beta,\gamma\right)$ (see Eq.~(\ref{eq:wignerdpar}) for details), the summation in Eq.~(\ref{mbhwdr}) involves only even values of $K$ for MBHs with even parity,  and odd values of $K$ for MBHs with odd parity, showing that approximately half of the coefficients $\Lambda_{Kl}^{L\uppi}\left(\theta\right)$ in Eq.~(\ref{mbhwdr}) is identically equal to 0. 

The coefficients $\Lambda_{Kl}^{L\uppi}\left(\theta\right)$ do  not  depend explicitly on $M$. This fact can be shown easily by plugging the following identity originating from Eq.~\eqref{eq:ladderM}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mlumbh}
    \Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi} = \tfrac{\overbrace{\scriptstyle{\hat{L}_{-}\ldots\hat{L}_{-}}}^{\text{$L
    \!\!-\!\!M\!$ times}}\,\overbrace{\scriptstyle{\hat{L}_{+}\ldots\hat{L}_{+}}}^{\text{$L
    \!\!-\!\!M\!$ times}}}{\prod_{\mu=M}^{L^{\vphantom{l}}-1}\left[L(L+1)-\mu(\mu+1)\right]} \,\Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi}
\end{equation}
into Eq.~(\ref{intlambda}) and using the obvious fact (see Eq.~(\ref{ladderlabeuler})) that $\hat{L}_{-}^\dagger=\hat{L}_{+}$. We have
\begin{eqnarray}
    \left\langle\left.\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK} \right|\Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi}\right\rangle\nonumber \!&=&\!
    \left\langle \vphantom{\tfrac{\scriptstyle{\hat{L}_{+}\ldots\hat{L}_{+}}\mathcal{D}_{L_{\vphantom{y}}}^{MK}}{\prod\sqrt{L(L+1)-\mu(\mu+1)}}} \right.
    \hspace{-5pt}
    \tfrac{\overbrace{\scriptstyle{\hat{L}_{+}\ldots\hat{L}_{+}}}^{L-M~\text{times}}\mathcal{D}_{L_{\vphantom{y}}}^{MK}}{\prod\hspace{-1pt}\sqrt{L(L+\hspace{-1pt}1)-\mu(\mu+\hspace{-1pt}1)}}\hspace{-3pt}
    \left.\vphantom{\tfrac{\scriptstyle{\hat{L}_{+}\ldots\hat{L}_{+}}\mathcal{D}_{L_{\vphantom{y}}}^{MK}}{\prod\hspace{-1pt}\sqrt{L(L+1)-\mu(\mu+1)}}}\right|
    \!\tfrac{\overbrace{\scriptstyle{\hat{L}_{+}\ldots\hat{L}_{+}}}^{L-M~\text{times}}\Omega_{l_{\vphantom{y}}}^{LM\uppi}}{\prod\hspace{-1pt}\sqrt{L(L+\hspace{-1pt}1)-\mu(\mu+\hspace{-1pt}1)}}\hspace{-6pt}
    \left. \vphantom{\tfrac{\scriptstyle{\hat{L}_{+}\ldots\hat{L}_{+}}\mathcal{D}_{L_{\vphantom{y}}}^{MK}}{\prod\hspace{-1pt}\sqrt{L(L+1)-\mu(\mu+1)}}}\right\rangle \nonumber\\[5pt]
     &=&\left\langle\left.\mathcal{D}_{L}^{LK} \right|\Omega_{l}^{LL\uppi}\right\rangle =  \Lambda_{Kl}^{L\uppi}\left(\theta\right) \label{eq:Mind}
\end{eqnarray}
showing that $\Lambda_{Kl}^{L\uppi}\left(\theta\right)$ does not depend on the initial value of $M$, as claimed above. Note that $\mu$ in Eq.~(\ref{eq:Mind}) ranges from $M$ to $L\hspace{-2pt}-\hspace{-2pt}1$, as follows from Eq.~(\ref{eq:mlumbh}).

To evaluate the integral over the Euler angles $\left(\alpha, \beta, \gamma\right)$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:Mind}, we express $\Omega_{l}^{LL\uppi}$ (given explicitly later in our exposition by Eqs.~\ref{mbhsLL} and~\ref{mbhsLL1}) in terms of the Euler angles using the relations provided by Eqs.~\eqref{bpctoeulr}. The linear expansion coefficients $\Lambda_{Kl}^{L\uppi}\left(\theta\right)$ can be then evaluated as septuple finite sums by performing elementary integrations of the integrand expanded in $\sin$ and $\cos$ of the Euler angles $\beta$ and $\gamma$ (details of this process are given in Section~I of Supplementary Material \cite{supplemental}). Much more compact formulas 
\begin{eqnarray}
   \Lambda_{Kl}^{L\uppi}(\theta) &=& \left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
      \overline{\Lambda}_{Kl}^{L\uppi}(\theta)  & \text{ when } L+K+d \text{ is even} \\[5pt]
      0  & \text{ when } L+K+d \text{ is odd}
   \end{array}\right. \label{eq:Lambdapcs}  
\end{eqnarray}
are obtained by expressing the resulting sums as hypergeometric functions of $\theta$, where
\begin{eqnarray}
    \overline{\Lambda}_{Kl}^{L\uppi}(\theta)\! &=&\! (-1)^{\frac{L-K-d}{2}}\!\! \left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle L  \\
         \scriptstyle l\!-\!d
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\! \sqrt{\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle \frac{L}{2}+\frac{K}{2}+\frac{d}{2}-\frac{1}{2}  \\
         \scriptstyle d-\frac{1}{2}
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\! \left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle \frac{L}{2}-\frac{K}{2} +\frac{d}{2}-\frac{1}{2} \\
         \scriptstyle d-\frac{1}{2}
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right) }  \nonumber\\
     &&\hspace{-40pt}\cdot\sqrt{\!\!\tfrac{\left(\!L+1-d\left(\!l-\frac{1}{2}\!\right)\!\right)} {\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2l  \\
         \scriptstyle d
    \end{array}\!\!\right)\!\! \left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2L+2  \\
         \scriptstyle 2l+1
    \end{array}\!\!\right) }}  \hspace{-1pt}  \sum_{\kappa=0}^{d} \hspace{-2pt}\hspace{2pt}{}_2F_1\!\left[\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         {\scriptstyle d-l,-\frac{L}{2}-\epsilon\frac{K}{2}+\frac{d}{2}} \\
         {\scriptstyle{-L}}
    \end{array}\!\!;1\!-e^{-2\textbf{\textit{i}}\epsilon\theta}\right]^{\vphantom{2}} \nonumber\\
    && \hspace{-40pt}\cdot  \epsilon \, e^{\textit{\textbf{i}}\hspace{1pt}\left(\!\frac{K}{2}+\epsilon(l-2d)\!\right)\theta} \label{Lambdapi} ~~~~~[\text{with $\epsilon=(-1)^\kappa$}]
\end{eqnarray}
The last expression can be given is somewhat more transparent form when specialized to the natural and unnatural parity; we have
\begin{eqnarray}
    \overline{\Lambda}_{Kl}^{Ln}(\theta) &=& (-1)^{\frac{L-K}{2}} \left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle L  \\
         \scriptstyle l
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\! \sqrt{\tfrac{\left(\!L+1\!\right)\!\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)_{\frac{L}{2}\!+\!\frac{K}{2_{\vphantom{a}}}}\!\!\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)_{\frac{L}{2}\!-\!\frac{K}{2}}}{\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2L+2  \\
         \scriptstyle 2l+1
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\! \left(\frac{L}{2}\!+\!\frac{K}{2}\right)!\left(\frac{L}{2}\!-\!\frac{K}{2}\right)!}}  \nonumber\\
    && \cdot{}_2F_1\hspace{-3pt}\left[\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         {\scriptstyle -l,-\frac{L}{2}-\frac{K}{2}} \\
         {\scriptstyle{-L}}
    \end{array}\!\!;1\!-e^{-2\textbf{\textit{i}}\theta}\right] e^{\textit{\textbf{i}}\hspace{1pt}\left(\!\frac{K}{2}+l\right)\theta} \label{Lambdan}
\end{eqnarray}
and
\begin{eqnarray}
    \overline{\Lambda}_{Kl}^{Lu}(\theta) &=& (-1)^{\frac{L-K-1}{2}}\!\! \left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle L  \\
         \scriptstyle l\!-\!1
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\! \sqrt{\tfrac{4\left(\!L-l+\frac{3}{2}\!\right) \left(1\right)_{\frac{L}{2}\!+\!\frac{K}{2}_{\vphantom{a}}}\left(1\right)_{\frac{L}{2}\!-\!\frac{K}{2}} } {2l\pi^{2}\! \left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2L+2  \\
         \scriptstyle 2l+1
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)_{\frac{L}{2}\!+\!\frac{K}{2}}\!\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)_{\frac{L}{2}\!-\!\frac{K}{2}}}}  \nonumber\\
    && \hspace{-45pt}\cdot \hspace{-12pt} \sum_{\kappa{\scriptscriptstyle \in\{1,-1\}}} \hspace{-8pt} \kappa \hspace{2pt}{}_2F_1\hspace{-3pt}\left[\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         {\scriptstyle 1-l,-\frac{L}{2}-\kappa\frac{K}{2}+\frac{1}{2}} \\
         {\scriptstyle{-L}}
    \end{array}\!\!;1\hspace{-2.5pt}-\hspace{-1pt}e^{-2\textbf{\textit{i}}\kappa\theta}\right]^{\vphantom{2}} \! e^{\textit{\textbf{i}}\hspace{1pt}\left(\!\frac{K}{2}+\kappa(l-2)\!\right)\theta} \label{Lambdau} 
\end{eqnarray}
Note that the negative value of the parameter in the denominator of ${}_{2}F_{1}$ in Eqs.~(\ref{Lambdapi}--\ref{Lambdau}) is acceptable, as the (less) negative parameter in the numerator guarantees that the ${}_{2}F_{1}$ series terminates before arriving at the singular terms, making the series a finite polynomial. 

An attempt to derive Eqs.~(\ref{Lambdapi}--\ref{Lambdau}) was made earlier by Pont and Shakeshaft (see Eq.~(A13) of \cite{Pont1995}), with $\Lambda_{Kl}^{L\uppi}(\theta)$ denoted by $C_{Kl}^{L}(\theta)$, which were defined formally by Eqs.~(A11) and~(A12) of \cite{Pont1995}. Unfortunately, Eq.~(A13) of \cite{Pont1995} seems to be erroneous as it does not agree with particular values of $C_{Kl}^{L}(\theta)$ computed directly from Eqs.~(A22) and~(A23) of \cite{Pont1995}. The connection between $\Lambda_{Kl}^{Ln}(\theta)$, defined by Eq.~\eqref{Lambdan}, and $C_{Kl}^{L}(\theta)$ given by Eq.~(A13) of \cite{Pont1995} is given by
\begin{equation} \label{eq:pserror}
    \frac{\Lambda_{Kl}^{Ln}(\theta)}{C_{Kl}^{L}(\theta)}\! =\! \tfrac{(-1)^L\!+(-1)_{\phantom{l}}^K}{32\pi^22^{L^{\phantom{l}}}l!}\!\sqrt{\!\scriptstyle{(L-l+1)\hspace{-1pt}(2l+1)!}\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2L+1  \\
         \scriptstyle L+K
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2L-2l+2  \\
         \scriptstyle L-l+1
    \end{array}\!\!\!\right)\!} 
\end{equation}
showing that multiple factors were missing or were misidentified in Eq.~(A13) of \cite{Pont1995}. Further details are given in Section~II of Supplementary Material \cite{supplemental}. Similar connection expression for unnatural parity states has not been investigated here.

Another attempt to find $\Lambda_{Kl}^{Ln}(\theta)$ was made by Nikitin and Ostrovsky \cite{Nikitin1985a,Nikitin1985b}, who derived a recurrence relation (Eq.~(4.7) of \cite{Nikitin1985a}) allowing to determine $\Lambda_{Kl}^{Ln}(\theta)$ (denoted as $F^{LK}_{l_{1}l_{2}}(\theta)$ in \cite{Nikitin1985a}) for arbitrary values of $L$ and $K$, with the recurrence boundary condition given by Eqs.~(4.1) and~(4.8) of \cite{Nikitin1985a}. The resulting coefficients $F^{LK}_{l_{1}l_{2}}(\theta)$ can be used together with Eq.~(3.11) of \cite{Nikitin1985a} in a manner analogous to Eq.~\eqref{mbhwdr}. Two drawbacks of this approach are apparent: (i)~No general solution for the recurrence relation (Eq.~(4.7) of \cite{Nikitin1985a} is offered. (ii)~The amount of work associated with the determination of all necessary coefficients $F^{LK}_{l_{1}l_{2}}(\theta)$ with $K=0,\ldots,L$ grows with $L$. On the other hand, the method suggested by  Nikitin and Ostrovsky \cite{Nikitin1985a} can be used to determine the connection between (general) bispherical harmonics (BHs) $\Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M}\equiv \Omega_{l_{1} \hspace{-0.7pt}l_{2}\vphantom{y}}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M} \hspace{-0.7pt}(\theta_{1}\hspace{-0.5pt},\phi_{1}\hspace{-0.7pt},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$, while the equations derived by us correspond only to a subset of these functions, i.e., the minimal bipolar harmonics (MBH) $\Omega_{l}^{LM\uppi}(\theta_{1},\phi_{1},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$. %For this subset, the recursive approach of Nikitin and Ostrovsky produces the same results as Eqs.~(\ref{eq:Lambdapcs}) and~(\ref{Lambdan}), as verified on a finite sample of examples.

\subsection{Expansion of the wave function  \texorpdfstring{$\Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$}~ in the angular generator bases }

We have discussed above two distinct bases of angular subspaces generators $\mathscr{Y}_{l}^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$: 
\begin{eqnarray}
\text{Wigner matrices: } && \left\{\mathcal{D}_{L}^{MK}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma): K=-L,\ldots,L\right\} \nonumber\\
\text{MBHs: }  && \left\{\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}  \hspace{-0.7pt}(\theta_{1}\hspace{-0.5pt},\phi_{1}\hspace{-0.7pt},\theta_{2},\phi_{2}): l=d,\ldots,L\right\} \nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
that can be used to construct the most general wave function  $\Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$ with definite values of $L$, $M$, and $\uppi$ defined before symbolically by Eq.~(\ref{wfa1}). The two distinct expansions generated by these bases can be expressed as follows
\begin{eqnarray}\label{wfa2}
    &&\Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2}) \!=\! \sum_{l=d}^{L}\psi_{l}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12}) \,\,\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}M\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}\hspace{-0.7pt}(\theta_{1}\hspace{-0.5pt},\phi_{1}\hspace{-0.7pt},\theta_{2},\phi_{2}) \nonumber\\
    &&\Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2}) \!=\hspace{-12pt} \sum_{k=-\frac{L-d}{2}}^{\frac{L-d}{2}}\hspace{-9pt}\varphi_{2k}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12}) \,\,\mathcal{D}_{L}^{M\hspace{0.2pt}2k}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)
\end{eqnarray}
where the summation range in the expansion over the Wigner functions can be readily established from the parity properties of $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{M\hspace{0.2pt}2k}(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$  given by Eq.~(\ref{eq:wignerdpar}). The partial wave components $\psi_{l}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12})$ and $\varphi_{2k}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12})$ do not depend on $M$ owing to the following relations
\begin{eqnarray}
    \hat{L}_\pm\,\,  \psi_{l}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12}) & =  &0 \label{pmLani}\\
    \hat{L}_\pm\,\, \varphi_{2k}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12}) & = & 0 \label{Lpmani}
\end{eqnarray}
which originate from the fact that $\hat{L}_\pm$ defined in Eq.~(\ref{ladderlabeuler}) consists only of derivatives with respect to the Euler angles (but not with respect to the internal shape variables $r_1$, $r_2$, and $r_{12}$), and because $\hat{L}_\pm$ defined in Eq.~(\ref{ladderlabbsph}), while consisting of derivatives with respect to the angles $\theta_{1},\phi_{1},\theta_{2}$, and $\phi_{2}$, obeys also a relation $\hat{L}_\pm\left(\cos \theta\right) = 0$ (easily verified using Eq.~(\ref{eq:costheta}) and Eq.~(\ref{ladderlabbsph})), which stipulates that $\hat{L}_\pm\left(r_{12}\right)=\hat{L}_\pm\left( (r_1^2\!+\!r_2^2\!-\!2\,r_1r_2\cos\theta)^{\sfrac{1}{2}}\right)= 0$.

It is easy to verify that both the wave functions $\Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$ in Eq.~(\ref{wfa2}) are eigenfunctions of the operators $\hat{L}^{\hspace{-0.7pt}2}$, $\hat{L}_{z}$, and $\hat{\hspace{-1pt}\bm{i}}$. Indeed, the angular momentum operator $\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}$ defined by Eq.~(\ref{amocc}) annihilates the partial wave components:  $\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}\left( \psi_{l}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12})\right) = \left[0,0,0\right]^\mathsf{T}$  and $\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}\left( \varphi_{2k}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12})\right) = \left[0,0,0\right]^\mathsf{T}$, properties which follow readily from the chain rule and from the obvious and easy-to-verify relations $\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}\left( r_{1}\right) =\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}\left( r_{2}\right) =\hat{\bm{L}}_{xyz}\left( r_{12}\right) = \left[0,0,0\right]^\mathsf{T}$.  On the other hand, it should be obvious that a most general wave function  $\Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$ with definite values of $L$, $M$, and $\uppi$ can be always written as a linear combination of the generators spanning the $\left|L,M,\uppi\right\rangle$ invariant subspaces with coefficients depending only on the internal shape variables $r_1$, $r_2$, and $r_{12}$.

It can be established easily by the chain rule that a function $f(r_1,r_2,r_{12})$ depending only on the internal shape variables $r_1$, $r_2$, and $r_{12}$---so in particular also the partial wave components $\psi_{l}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12})$ and $\varphi_{2k}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12})$---obeys the following first-order differential relation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:diff1ri}
    \nabla_{i}\,f(r_1,r_2,r_{12}) \!=\! \left(\!\tfrac{\bm{r}_{i{\vphantom{y}}}}{r_i} \partial_{r_i}  \!\!\!+\!\! \tfrac{\bm{r}_{i{\vphantom{y}}}-\bm{r}_{3-i}}{r_{12}}\partial_{r_{12}}\!\right)f(r_1,r_2,r_{12})
\end{equation}
valid for $i=1,2$. Using the elementary identities
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:eleident}
    \bm{r}_1 \boldsymbol{\cdot} \bm{r}_2 & = & \tfrac{1}{2} \left( r_1^2+r_2^2-r_{12}^2\right) \nonumber\\
    \bm{r}_1 \boldsymbol{\cdot} \left(\bm{r}_1 - \bm{r}_2\right) & = & \tfrac{1}{2} \left( r_1^2-r_2^2+r_{12}^2\right) \nonumber\\
    \bm{r}_2 \boldsymbol{\cdot} \left(\bm{r}_2 - \bm{r}_1\right) & = & \tfrac{1}{2} \left( r_2^2-r_1^2+r_{12}^2\right) \\
    \nabla_{i} \boldsymbol{\cdot} \tfrac{\bm{r}_{j{\vphantom{y}}}}{r_j} & =& \tfrac{2}{r_j} \delta_{ij}\nonumber\\
    \nabla_{1} \boldsymbol{\cdot} \tfrac{\bm{r}_{1{\vphantom{y}}}-\bm{r}_{2{\vphantom{y}}}}{r_{12}} &=& \nabla_{2} \boldsymbol{\cdot} \tfrac{\bm{r}_{2{\vphantom{y}}}-\bm{r}_{1{\vphantom{y}}}}{r_{12}} = \tfrac{2}{r_{12}}\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
it is straightforward to extend Eq.~(\ref{eq:diff1ri}) to the following second-order differential relations
\begin{equation} \label{eq:deldelf}
    \nabla_{i}\boldsymbol{\cdot} \nabla_{j}\,f(r_1,r_2,r_{12}) \!\!=\!\!\left\{\!\!\begin{array}{cc}
         \hat{t}_{ii}\,f(r_1,r_2,r_{12}) &\!\! \text{when $i\!=\!j$} \\
         \hat{t}_{ij}\,f(r_1,r_2,r_{12}) &\!\! \text{when $i\!\neq\! j$}
    \end{array}\right.
\end{equation}
where the operators $\hat{t}_{11}$, $\hat{t}_{22}$,  and $\hat{t}_{12}=\hat{t}_{21}$ are given by

\begin{eqnarray}
    \hspace{-10pt}\hat{t}_{11}\! &=& \!\partial_{r_{1}r_{1}} \!\!+\! \tfrac{2}{r_{1}}\partial_{r_1}  \!\! +\! \partial_{r_{12}r_{12}} \!\!+\! \tfrac{2}{r_{12}}\partial_{r_{12}} \!\! +\!\tfrac{r_{1\vphantom{y}}^2-r_{2}^2+r_{12}^2}{r_{1}r_{12}}                       \partial_{r_{1}r_{12}} \label{eq:diff2r1}\\
    \hspace{-10pt}\hat{t}_{22}\! &=& \! \partial_{r_{2}r_{2}} \!\!+\! \tfrac{2}{r_{2}}\partial_{r_2}  \!\! +\! \partial_{r_{12}r_{12}} \!\!+\! \tfrac{2}{r_{12}}\partial_{r_{12}} \!\! +\!\tfrac{r_{2\vphantom{y}}^2-r_{1}^2+r_{12}^2}{r_{2}r_{12}}                       \partial_{r_{2}r_{12}}\label{eq:diff2r2}\\
    \hspace{-10pt}\hat{t}_{12}\! &=& \! -\partial_{r_{12}r_{12}}\! - \!\tfrac{2}{r_{12}}\partial_{r_{12}}\!+\!\tfrac{r_{1\vphantom{y}}^2+r_{2}^2-r_{12}^2}{2r_{1}r_{2}}\partial_{r_{1}r_{2}} \label{eq:diff2r12}\\ 
    &&   -\tfrac{r_{1\vphantom{y}}^2-r_{2}^2+r_{12}^2}{2r_{1}r_{12}}~\partial_{r_{1}r_{12}}\!-\! \tfrac{r_{2\vphantom{y}}^2-r_{1}^2+r_{12}^2}{2r_{2}r_{12}}~\partial_{r_{2}r_{12}}\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
The operators $\hat{t}_{11}$, $\hat{t}_{22}$,  and $\hat{t}_{12}=\hat{t}_{21}$ are used later in Section~\ref{method} to derive the RSEs.
\subsection{Variants of MBHs  with \texorpdfstring{$M=L$}~ }

We have demonstrated in the previous Section that  $\psi_{l}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12})$ are independent of the quantum number $M$. Consequently, the derivation of the reduced Schr\"{o}dinger's equations (RSEs) for the partial wave coefficients $\psi_{l}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12})$ can be performed for an arbitrary $M$, always leading to the same set of RSEs. It turns out that the choice $M\!=\!L$ (or  $M\!=\!-L$) simplifies considerably the analytical form of MBHs  and makes the RSEs derivation significantly simpler. All the following considerations presented in the remainder of this paper use the choice of $M\!=\!L$. It is advantageous for the further discussion to consider explicitly four variants of specialized MBHs with $M\!=\!L$ with slightly different properties:

(1)~The usual minimal bipolar harmonics (MBHs) $\Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}L\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} \equiv \Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}L\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi} \hspace{-0.7pt}(\theta_{1}\hspace{-0.5pt},\phi_{1}\hspace{-0.7pt},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$, given by Eq.~(\ref{bsheqn1}) using an extra condition $M\!=\!L$. They are normalized according to Eq.~(\ref{eq:omegaorth}) and depend only on the angular variables. The specialization $M\!=\!L$ allows us to express MBHs in a particularly simple form 
\begin{eqnarray}\label{mbhsLL}
    \Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}L\hspace{-0.7pt}n}  \hspace{-0.7pt} &\!=& N_{l}^{Ln}\left(\sin{\theta_{1}} \, e^{\textbf{\textit{i}}\phi_{1}}\right)^{\!l}
    \left(\sin{\theta_{2}} \, e^{\textbf{\textit{i}}\phi_{2}}\right)^{\!L-l}\\
    \Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}L\hspace{-0.7pt}u}  \hspace{-0.7pt} &\!=& N_{l}^{Lu}\left[\left(\sin{\theta_{1}} \, e^{\textbf{\textit{i}}\phi_{1}}\right)^{\!l}
    \left(\sin{\theta_{2}} \, e^{\textbf{\textit{i}}\phi_{2}}\right)^{\!L-l}\cos{\theta_{2}}\right. \nonumber\\
    &-& \left.\!\cos{\theta_{1}}\left(\sin{\theta_{1}} \, e^{\textbf{\textit{i}}\phi_{1}}\right)^{\!l-1}
    \left(\sin{\theta_{2}} \, e^{\textbf{\textit{i}}\phi_{2}}\right)^{\!L-l+1}\right] \label{mbhsLL1}
\end{eqnarray}
with the normalizing phase factors $N_{l}^{L\uppi}$ given by
\begin{equation}\label{nlpik}
    N_{l}^{L\uppi} = \frac{(-1)^L}{4\pi}\sqrt{\frac{2^d\left(L+1-d\right)!\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)_{l}\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)_{L-l+d}}{(l-d)!(L-l)!(L+1)!}}
\end{equation}
where $(\alpha)_k$ denotes Pochammer symbols given by Eq.~(\ref{eq:poch}) and where $d$ is given by Eq.~(\ref{eq:d}).

(2)~The unnormalized minimal bipolar harmonics (UMBHs) $\overline{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi} \equiv \overline{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi}(\theta_{1},\phi_{1},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})$ defined implictly by the relation
\begin{equation}\label{nbshnp}
    \Omega_{l}^{L\hspace{-0.7pt}L\hspace{-0.7pt}\uppi}  \hspace{-0.7pt}(\theta_{1}\hspace{-0.5pt},\phi_{1}\hspace{-0.7pt},\theta_{2},\phi_{2}) = N_{l}^{L\uppi}~\overline{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi}(\theta_{1},\phi_{1},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})
\end{equation}
and given explicitly by the following particularly simple formulas \cite{Gu2001}
\begin{equation}\label{ubshnp}
    \overline{\Omega}_{l}^{LLn} = \left(\sin{\theta_{1}} \, e^{\textbf{\textit{i}}\phi_{1}}\right)^{l}
    \left(\sin{\theta_{2}} \, e^{\textbf{\textit{i}}\phi_{2}}\right)^{L-l}
\end{equation}
for natural parity $\uppi=n$ and 
\begin{eqnarray}\label{ubshup}
    \overline{\Omega}_{l}^{LLu} = \cos{\theta_{2}}~\overline{\Omega}_{l}^{LLn}-\cos{\theta_{1}}~\overline{\Omega}_{l-1}^{LLn}
\end{eqnarray}
for unnatural parity $\uppi=u$. 

(3)~Solid minimal bipolar harmonics (SMBHs) $\bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi} \equiv \bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$ defined explicitly by the following equation
\begin{equation}\label{nsbph}
    \bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2}) = r_{1}^l r_{2}^{L-l+d}~\Omega_{l}^{LL\uppi}(\theta_{1},\phi_{1},\theta_{2},\phi_{2})
\end{equation}

(4)~Unnormalized solid minimal bipolar harmonics (USMBHs) $\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi} \equiv \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$ defined implicitly by the following equation
\begin{eqnarray}\label{nsbshccnp}
    \bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi} = N_{l}^{L\uppi}~\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi}
\end{eqnarray}
with $N_{l}^{L\uppi}$ given previously by Eq.~(\ref{nlpik}). These functions have particularly simple representation \cite{Gu2001} in the Cartesian coordinates $\left(x_{1},y_{1},z_{1},x_{2},y_{2},z_{2}\right)$
\begin{eqnarray}\label{usbshccnp}
    \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LLn} \!= \,\left(x_{1}+\textbf{\textit{i}}y_{1}\right)^{l} \left(x_{2}+\textbf{\textit{i}}y_{2}\right)^{L-l}
\end{eqnarray}
for natural parity $\uppi=n$ and 
\begin{eqnarray}\label{usbshccup}
    \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LLu} \!= \,z_{2} ~\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LLn}-z_{1} ~\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l-1}^{LLn}
\end{eqnarray}
for unnatural parity $\uppi=u$. Note that USMBHs  possess also the  following ladder-like property
\begin{eqnarray}
    (x_{1}+\textbf{\textit{i}}y_{1}) ~\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi} \!&=& \,\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l+1}^{L+1,L\uppi} \label{sprel1} \\
    (x_{2}+\textbf{\textit{i}}y_{2}) ~\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi} \!&=& \,\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{L+1,L\uppi} \label{sprel2}    
\end{eqnarray}
valid for arbitrary $l\in\left\{d,\ldots,L \right\}$, which will be used extensively in the forthcoming Sections.

The evaluation of the action of the kinetic energy operator on $\Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$  relies heavily on the following properties of USMBHs 
\begin{eqnarray}
    \hspace{-15pt}\nabla_{1}\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LLn} \hspace{-7pt} &=&\hspace{-2pt}  \left[\!\!\begin{array}{c}
     1 \\ \textbf{\textit{i}} \\ 0 \end{array}\!\!\right]l ~\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l-1}^{L-1,Ln}  \label{eq:nablanp1}\\
        \hspace{-15pt}
   \nabla_{2}\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LLn} \hspace{-7pt}&=&\hspace{-2pt} \left[\!\!\begin{array}{c}
      1 \\ \textbf{\textit{i}} \\ 0 \end{array}\!\!\right]\! \left(L\!-\!l\right) \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{L-1,Ln} \label{eq:nablanp2}\\
    \hspace{-15pt}\nabla_{1}\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LLu}\hspace{-7pt} &=&\hspace{-2pt} \left[\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         1  \\ \textbf{\textit{i}} \\ 0 \end{array}\!\!\right]\!\!\!\left(\!\!(\!l\hspace{-2.5pt}-\hspace{-2.5pt}1)\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l-1}^{L\hspace{-1pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1,Lu}\!\!+\!z_{2}\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l-1}^{L\hspace{-1pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1,Ln}\!\right)\!\!-\!\!\left[\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         0 \\ 0 \\ 1
    \end{array}\!\!\right]\!\!\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l-1}^{LLn}\label{eq:nabla1up}\\
    \hspace{-15pt}\nabla_{2}\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LLu} \hspace{-7pt}&=&\hspace{-2pt}\left[\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         1\\\textbf{\textit{i}} \\ 0
    \end{array}\!\!\right]\!\!\!\left(\!\!(\!L\hspace{-2.5pt}-\hspace{-2.5pt}l)\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{L\hspace{-1pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1,Lu}\!\!-\!z_{1}\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l-1}^{L\hspace{-1pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1,Ln}\!\right)\!\!+\!\!\left[\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         0  \\
        0 \\
        1
    \end{array}\!\!\right]\!\!\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LLn}\label{eq:nabla2up}
\end{eqnarray}
Consecutive application of these identities produces the following relations
\begin{eqnarray}
        \Delta_{i} \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi} = \nabla_{i}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\nabla_{i} \,\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi}  &= 0& \label{ldoafi0}\\[5pt]
        \nabla_{1}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\nabla_{2} \,\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi} &= 0& \label{ldoaf30}
\end{eqnarray}
valid also for SMBHs $\bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi}$ because of Eq.~\eqref{nsbshccnp}. These formulas are equivalent to Eqs.~(17) and~(18)  of  \cite{Efros1986} derived by \'{E}fros using irreducible tensor algebra. Note that Eq.~(\ref{ldoafi0}), stating that $\Delta_{i} \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi} \!=0$ for $i\in \{1,2\}$, justifies the name bispherical harmonic used for these generators, as these functions are harmonic with respect to the Laplacians of both quasiparticles.

\section{Derivation of the RSE\MakeLowercase{s}}\label{method}

\subsection{Action of Hamiltonian on \texorpdfstring{$\Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$}~  and the separation of the angular functions}


Consider a general wave function  $\Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$ with definite values of $L$, $M$, and $\uppi$, which by virtue of Eq.~\eqref{wfa2} can be expanded  as
\begin{equation}\label{twf}
    \Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1}\hspace{-1pt},\hspace{-1pt}\bm{r}_{2}) \!=\! \sum_{l=d}^{L}\psi_{l}^{L\uppi}(r_{1}\hspace{-1pt},\hspace{-1pt}r_{2},\hspace{-1pt}r_{12}) \,\,\bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1}\hspace{-1pt},\hspace{-1pt}\bm{r}_{2})
\end{equation}
where $\bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$ are defined by Eq.~(\ref{nsbph}) and $\psi_{l}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12})$ are presently undetermined. To compute the action of the Hamiltonian introduced in Eq.~(\ref{secm0})
\begin{equation}\label{ham}
    \hat{\mathcal{H}}\! =\! \underbrace{-\tfrac{\nabla_{1\vphantom{y}}\hspace{-1pt}\boldsymbol{\cdot}\hspace{-1pt}\nabla_{1}}{2\mu_1}\!-\!\tfrac{\nabla_{2\vphantom{y}}\hspace{-1pt}\boldsymbol{\cdot}\hspace{-1pt}\nabla_{2}}{2\mu_2}\! -\!\tfrac{\nabla_{1\vphantom{y}}\hspace{-1pt}\boldsymbol{\cdot}\hspace{-1pt}\nabla_{2}}{m_{3}}}_{\hat{\mathcal{T}}}  \underbrace{+ \tfrac{q_{1\vphantom{y}}q_{2}}{r_{12}}\!+\!\tfrac{q_{1\vphantom{y}}q_{3}}{r_{1}\vphantom{\mu_2}}\!+\!\tfrac{q_{2\vphantom{y}}q_{3}}{r_{2}}}_{\hat{\mathcal{V}}}
\end{equation}
on $\Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$, let us first consider the action of $\hat{\mathcal{T}}$ on the product  $\psi_{l}\,\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l} \equiv \psi_{l}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12})~  \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$, where $\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$ are the USMBHs defined by Eq.~(\ref{nsbshccnp}). $\hat{\mathcal{T}}\, \psi_{l}\,\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}$ can be computed from the following identity
\begin{eqnarray}
    \hspace{-10pt}\nabla_{\!i}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\! \nabla_{\!j}\!\left(\psi_{l}\, \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}\right) &=& \left(\nabla_{\!i}\psi_{l}\right) \!\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\!\left(\nabla_{\!j}\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}\right)+   (\nabla_{\!i}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\nabla_{\!j}\hspace{1pt}\psi_{l})\,\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}\nonumber\\
    &+& \left(\nabla_{\!j}\psi_{l}\right) \!\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\!\left(\nabla_{\!i}\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}\right)+ \psi_{l}\,(\nabla_{\!i}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\nabla_{\!j}\hspace{1pt}\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}) \label{eq:fgident}
\end{eqnarray}
The terms of the type $\nabla_{\!i}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\nabla_{\!j}\hspace{1pt}\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}$ vanish owing to Eqs.~(\ref{ldoafi0}) and~(\ref{ldoaf30}), while the terms of the type $\nabla_{\!i}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\nabla_{\!j}\hspace{1pt}\psi_{l}$ can be readily expressed using the operators $\hat{t}_{ij}$ defined in Eqs.~(\ref{eq:deldelf}--\ref{eq:diff2r12}). The remaining terms, of the type $\left(\nabla_{\!i}\psi_{l}\right) \!\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\!\left(\nabla_{\!j}\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}\right)$, can be transformed by combining Eq.~(\ref{eq:diff1ri}) with Eqs.~(\ref{eq:nablanp1})--(\ref{eq:nabla2up}) into the following expression
\begin{eqnarray}
   \left(\nabla_{\!i}\psi_{l}\right) \!\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\!\left(\nabla_{\!j}\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}\right) & = & (l_j \hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-3pt} |j\hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-2.5pt}i|\hspace{1pt}d) \left[\left(\!\tfrac{\partial_{r_{i\vphantom{y}}}}{r_i}   \hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-3pt} \tfrac{\partial_{r_{12\vphantom{y}}}}{r_{12}}\!\right) \psi_{l} \right] \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l+j-i} \nonumber \\
   & &  \hspace{-30pt} - (l_j \hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-3pt} |i\hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-2.5pt}j\hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-2.5pt}3|\hspace{1pt}d) \left[\left(\! \tfrac{\partial_{r_{12\vphantom{y}}}}{r_{12}}\!\right) \psi_{l} \right] \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l+i+j-3} \label{eq:ninj}
\end{eqnarray}
where the partial angular momenta $l_1=l$ and $l_2=L-l+d$ have been used for compactness of notation. Note that expressions equivalent to Eq.~(\ref{eq:ninj}) were derived earlier by \'{E}fros \cite{Efros1986} and by Frolov and Smith \cite{Frolov1996} using irreducible tensor algebra. 

The resulting expression for $\hat{\mathcal{T}}\, \psi_{l}\,\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}$ obtained by combining Eqs.~(\ref{eq:deldelf}) and~(\ref{ham})--(\ref{eq:ninj}) can be given by the following compact formula
\begin{eqnarray}
    \hat{\mathcal{T}}\, \psi_{l}\,\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}= - \left[\left(\!\tfrac{\hat{t}_{11_{\vphantom{a}}}}{2\mu_1}\hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{\hat{t}_{22_{\vphantom{a}}}}{2\mu_2}\hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{\hat{t}_{12_{\vphantom{a}}}}{m_3}\!\right) \psi_{l}^{L\uppi} \right] \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi}&&\hspace{20pt}\label{eq:ninj12} \\
    - \left[\left(\!\tfrac{l_1\vphantom{y}}{\mu_1}\tfrac{\partial_{r_{1\vphantom{y}}}}{r_1}   \hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{l_2\vphantom{y}}{\mu_2}\tfrac{\partial_{r_{2\vphantom{y}}}}{r_2}   \hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-3pt}\left(\tfrac{l_1\vphantom{y}}{m_1}\hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{l_2\vphantom{y}}{m_2} \right)\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{\partial_{r_{12\vphantom{y}}}}{r_{12}}\!\right) \psi_{l}^{L\uppi} \right] \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi}&& \nonumber\\
    +  (l_2 - d) \left[ \left(\!\tfrac{1}{m_2}\!\tfrac{\partial_{r_{12\vphantom{y}}}}{r_{12}}\hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{1}{m_3} \tfrac{\partial_{r_{1\vphantom{y}}}}{r_1}   \!\right) \psi_{l}^{L\uppi} \right] \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l+1}^{LL\uppi}&& \nonumber \\
    + (l_1 - d)\left[ \left(\! \tfrac{1}{m_1}\!\tfrac{\partial_{r_{12\vphantom{y}}}}{r_{12}}\hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{1}{m_3} \tfrac{\partial_{r_{2\vphantom{y}}}}{r_2}   \!\right) \psi_{l}^{L\uppi} \right] \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l-1}^{LL\uppi} && \nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
with the operators $\hat{t}_{11}$, $\hat{t}_{22}$,  and $\hat{t}_{12}$ given by Eqs.~(\ref{eq:diff2r1})--(\ref{eq:diff2r12}) and with $l_1=l$ and $l_2=L-l+d$. 
Note that the terms of the type $\nabla_{\!i}\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\nabla_{\!j}\hspace{1pt}\psi_{l}$ are collected in the first line of Eq.~(\ref{eq:ninj12}), while the terms of the type $\left(\nabla_{\!i}\psi_{l}\right) \!\!\boldsymbol{\cdot}\!\!\left(\nabla_{\!j}\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}\right)$, in the last three lines of Eq.~(\ref{eq:ninj12}). Note also that the action of  $\hat{\mathcal{T}}$ is closed with respect to the generator set $\left\{ \overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi}\!: l=d,\ldots,L \right\}$, with the value of $l$ unchanged or modified by $\pm1$, and with the unphysical generators $\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{d-1}^{LL\uppi}$  and $\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{L+1}^{LL\uppi}$ never appearing owing to the preceding zero multipliers that annihiliate them.

Multiplying Eq.~(\ref{eq:ninj12}) by  $N_{l}^{L\uppi}$ from Eq.~(\ref{nlpik}), using Eq.~(\ref{nsbshccnp}) to replace $\overline{\bm{\Omega}}_{l}^{LL\uppi}$ by $\bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi}$, and summing over $l\in \left\{ d,\ldots,L\right\}$ allows us to write down the action of the Hamiltonian operator $\hat{\mathcal{H}}$ on a general wave function  $\Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$ with definite values of $L$, $M$, and $\uppi$ as
\begin{eqnarray}
    \hat{\mathcal{H}}\, \Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1}\hspace{-1pt},\hspace{-1pt}\bm{r}_{2}) = -\sum_{l=d}^{L}\left[\left(\!\tfrac{\hat{t}_{11_{\vphantom{a}}}}{2\mu_1}\hspace{-2pt}+\hspace{-2pt}\tfrac{\hat{t}_{22_{\vphantom{a}}}}{2\mu_2}\hspace{-2pt}+\hspace{-2pt}\tfrac{\hat{t}_{12_{\vphantom{a}}}}{m_3}\hspace{-2pt}+\hspace{-2pt}\hat{\mathcal{V}} \hspace{45pt}\phantom{a} \right.\right.&&\label{eq:Hpsi} \\
    \left.\left.+\tfrac{l_{1\vphantom{y}}}{\mu_1}\tfrac{\partial_{r_{1\vphantom{y}}}}{r_1}\!+\!\tfrac{l_{2\vphantom{y}}}{\mu_2}\tfrac{\partial_{r_{2\vphantom{y}}}}{r_2}   \hspace{-1pt}+\hspace{-2pt}\left(\hspace{-1pt}\tfrac{l_{1\vphantom{y}}}{m_1}\hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{l_{2\vphantom{y}}}{m_2}\hspace{-1pt} \right)\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{\partial_{r_{12\vphantom{y}}}}{r_{12}}  \right) \psi_{l}^{L\uppi}\right]\bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi}&& \nonumber \\
    + \sum_{l=d}^{L\hspace{-1pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1} \tfrac{N_{l+1\vphantom{y}}^{L\uppi}}{N_{l_{\vphantom{1}}}^{L^{\vphantom{1}}\uppi}}(l_1 \hspace{-2pt}+\hspace{-3pt}1\hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-2pt}d)\!\left[ \left(\! \tfrac{1}{m_1}\!\tfrac{\partial_{r_{12\vphantom{y}}}}{r_{12}}\hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{1}{m_3} \tfrac{\partial_{r_{2\vphantom{y}}}}{r_2}   \!\right) \psi_{l\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1}^{L\uppi} \right] \bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi} && \nonumber\\
    + \hspace{-4.5pt} \sum_{l=d+\hspace{-1pt}1}^{L} \hspace{-3.5pt} \tfrac{N_{l-1\vphantom{y}}^{L\uppi}}{N_{l}^{L^{\vphantom{1}}\uppi}} (l_2 \hspace{-2pt}+\hspace{-3pt}1\hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-2pt}d) \!\left[ \left(\!\tfrac{1}{m_2}\!\tfrac{\partial_{r_{12\vphantom{y}}}}{r_{12}}\hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{1}{m_3} \tfrac{\partial_{r_{1\vphantom{y}}}}{r_1}   \!\right) \psi_{l\hspace{-0.5pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1}^{L\uppi} \right] \bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi}&& \nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
with the partial angular momenta $l_1$ and $l_2$ given by the familiar expressions $l_1=l$ and $l_2=L-l+d$ and where the weighted ratio of the normalization phase factors from Eq.~(\ref{nlpik}) can be computed explicitly as
\begin{eqnarray}
   (l_1 \hspace{-2pt}+\hspace{-3pt}1\hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-2pt}d) \tfrac{N_{l+1}^{L^{\vphantom{1}}\uppi}}{N_{l_{\vphantom{y}}}^{L\uppi}} &=&\sqrt{\tfrac{(l_{2}-d)(2l_{1}+3)(l_{1}+1-d)}{(2l_{2}+1)}}\\
   (l_2 \hspace{-2pt}+\hspace{-3pt}1\hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-2pt}d)\tfrac{N_{l-1}^{L^{\vphantom{1}}\uppi}}{N_{l_{\vphantom{y}}}^{L\uppi}} &=&\sqrt{\tfrac{(l_{1}-d)(2l_{2}+3)(l_{2}+1-d)}{(2l_{1}+1)}}\hspace{18pt} 
\end{eqnarray}
Having expressed in Eq.~(\ref{eq:Hpsi}) the action of the Hamiltonian $\hat{\mathcal{H}}$ on the wave function  $\Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$  in a closed form as an expansion over the generator set $\left\{ \bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi}\!: l=d,\ldots,L \right\}$, and having an analogous expansion of $\Psi^{LM\uppi}(\bm{r}_{1},\bm{r}_{2})$ given by Eq.~(\ref{twf}), the Schr\"{o}dinger equation from Eq.~(\ref{secm2}) can be now conveniently rewritten in a particularly transparent and structured tridiagonal matrix operator form 
\begin{widetext}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{hamtme}
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \bm{\Omega}_{d}^{LL\uppi} ~ \bm{\Omega}_{d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1}^{LL\uppi} \cdots ~\bm{\Omega}_{L}^{LL\uppi}
    \end{pmatrix}
    \left[
    \left(\begin{array}{cclcl}
        \!\!\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{d,d} & \!\!\!\!\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{d,d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1} & \;0\;\;\;\; & \cdots\;\;\;\;\; & \;0\vphantom{\vdots}\\
        \hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1,d} & \!\!\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1,d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1} & \ddots & \ddots\;\;\;\;\; & \;\vdots\\
        0 &\;\ddots &\ddots&\ddots\;\;\;\;\;\;& \;0\\ 
        \vdots &\;\ddots&\ddots & \hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{L\hspace{-0.6pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1,L\hspace{-0.6pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1} & \!\!\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{L\hspace{-0.6pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1,L}\\ 
        0\vphantom{\vdots} & \;\cdots\;\; &  \;0& \hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{L,L\hspace{-0.6pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1} & \!\!\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{L,L}
    \end{array} \right)\! -\!E \mathbb{1}\right]
    \left(\!\!\begin{array}{c}
     \psi_{d}^{L\uppi}\vphantom{\vdots}\\
        \psi_{d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1}^{L\uppi}\vphantom{\vdots}\\
        \vdots\\
        \vdots\\
        \psi_{L}^{L\uppi}\vphantom{\vdots}
    \end{array}\!\! \right) = 0
\end{eqnarray}
\end{widetext}
with the operators appearing in this equations having the following explicit form
\begin{eqnarray}
    \hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{l,l\phantom{+1}} &\!=\!& 
    -\tfrac{\partial_{r_{1\vphantom{y}}r_{1}}}{2\mu_{1}}
    \hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{\partial_{r_{2\vphantom{y}}r_{2}}}{2\mu_{2}}
    \hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{\partial_{r_{12\vphantom{y}}r_{12}}}{2\mu_{12}}\hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-3pt} \tfrac{q_{1\vphantom{y}}q_{2}}{r_{12}}\hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{q_{1\vphantom{y}}q_{3}}{r_{1}\vphantom{\mu_2}}\hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{q_{2\vphantom{y}}q_{3}}{r_{2}} \label{hkkgnygp2} \\[2pt]
    &\!-\!& \nonumber (1\hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-1.5pt}l_1)\!\left[\tfrac{\partial_{r_{1\vphantom{y}}}}{\mu_{1}r_{1}}\hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{\partial_{r_{12\vphantom{y}}}}{m_{1}r_{12}}\right]
    \hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-2pt}(1\hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-1.5pt}l_2)\!\left[\tfrac{\partial_{r_{2\vphantom{y}}}}{\mu_{2}r_{2}}\hspace{-3pt}+\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{\partial_{r_{12\vphantom{y}}}}{m_{2}r_{12}}\right]\\[2pt]
    &\!-\!\!&
     \tfrac{r_{1\vphantom{y}}^2+r_{12}^2-r_{2}^2}{2m_{1}r_{1}r_{12}}\partial_{r_{1}r_{12}}\hspace{-4pt}-\hspace{-3pt}  \tfrac{r_{2\vphantom{y}}^2+r_{12}^2-r_{1}^2}{2m_{2}r_{2}r_{12}}\partial_{r_{2}r_{12}} \hspace{-4pt}-\hspace{-3pt} \tfrac{r_{1\vphantom{y}}^2+r_{2}^2-r_{12}^2}{2m_{3}r_{1}r_{2}}\partial_{r_{1}r_{2}} \nonumber\\[5pt]
    \hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{l+1,l} &\!=\!& \sqrt{\tfrac{(l_2-d)(2l_2+1)(l_1-d+1)}{\phantom{(l_2-d)}(2l_1+3)\phantom{(l_1-d+1)}}} \!\left(\!\tfrac{\partial_{r_{12\vphantom{y}}}}{m_{2}\,r_{12}}\hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{\partial_{r_{1\vphantom{y}}}}{m_{3}\,r_{1}}\!\right) \label{hkk+1gnygp2}\\[2pt] \label{hkk-1gnygp2}
    \hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{l-1,l} &\!=\!& \sqrt{\tfrac{(l_1-d)(2l_1+1)(l_2-d+1)}{\phantom{(l_1-d)}(2l_2+3)\phantom{(l_2-d+1)}}}\!\left(\!\tfrac{\partial_{r_{12\vphantom{y}}}}{m_{1}\,r_{12}}\hspace{-3pt}-\hspace{-3pt}\tfrac{\partial_{r_{2\vphantom{y}}}}{m_{3}\,r_{2}}\!\right)
\end{eqnarray}
where $\mu_{12} = \frac{m_{1\vphantom{y}}m_{2}}{m_{1}+m_{2}}$ and where the partial angular momenta $l_1=l$ and $l_2=L-l+d$ have been used again to highlight the underlying symmetry of the resulting operators and equations.

For $L=0$ and natural parity---note that the case of $L=0$ and $\uppi=u$ is unphysical and never occurs---the matrix Hamiltonian in Eq.~(\ref{hamtme}) reduces to a single scalar operator $\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{0n}_{0,0}$ and the off-diagonal operators $\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{l+1,l}$ and $\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{l-1,l}$ do not appear in the formalism. The operator $\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{0n}_{0,0}$ upon the substitutions $l_1=l_2=0$, $q_1=q_2=-1$, $q_3=Z$, $m_1=m_2=1$, and in the limit $m_3\rightarrow\infty$ reduces to the S-state Hamiltonian of the helium-like atom with clamped nucleus of charge $Z$ derived first by Hylleraas (Eq.~(5) of \cite{Hylleraas1929}) and used since numerous times  in the literature in various forms (see Eqs.~(7) and~(15) of Gronwall \cite{Gronwall1932}, Eq.~(1) of Bartlett \cite{Bartlett1937}, Eqs.~(1.10), (2.03), and~(2.04) of Fock \cite{Fock1954,Fock1958}, Eq.~(13) of Pluvinage \cite{Pluvinage1955}, Eq.~(2.3) of Kinoshita \cite{Kinoshita1957}, Eq.~(14) of Pekeris \cite{Pekeris1958},  Eq.~(8)  of Pont and Shakeshaft \cite{Pont1995}, and Eqs.~(5) and~(6) of Nakashima and Nakatsuji \cite{Nakashima2007};  consult also the work of Hylleraas \cite{Hylleraas1960}, Morgan \cite{Morgan1986}, Abbott, Gottschalk, and Maslen \cite{Abbott1987,Gottschalk1987a,Gottschalk1987b}, He and Witek \cite{He2016}, and Liverts and Barnea \cite{Liverts2010,Liverts2013,Liverts2018,LivertS2022}  for a selective review of historical research extensions and applications of this formalism). A generalization of this Hamiltonian to arbitrary masses was presented by
Frolov (see Eq.~(11) of \cite{frolov1987}). 

For $L > 0$, the reduced wave function and RSE  in Eq.~\eqref{hamtme} have multicomponent structure, except for the $\textnormal{P}^{\textnormal{e}}$ states, for which the matrix Hamiltonian reduces to a single scalar operator.  This operator was first derived by Breit \cite{Breit1930} (see Eq.(10) of \cite{Breit1930}) for the helium-like atom with clamped nucleus, together with the analogous operators for the $\textnormal{P}^{\textnormal{o}}$ states (see Eqs.~(12), (18), and~(20) of~\cite{Breit1930}). Schwartz extended these results to $L = 0, 1, 2$ (see Appendix I of \cite{Schwartz1961} for details). Jackson \cite{Jackson1954} used the irreducible tensor approach to derive RSE for helium with clamped nucleus (see Eq.~(5) of \cite{Jackson1954}). \'{E}fros \cite{Efros1986} extended these expressions  (see Eqs.~(22--24) of \cite{Efros1986} corresponding to our Eqs.~(\ref{hkkgnygp2}--\ref{hkk-1gnygp2})) to the finite mass of nucleus (see also Eqs.~(25)--(27) of \cite{Harris2004} in this context). Bottcher, Schultz, and Madison \cite{Bottcher1994} derived analogous form of RSE (see Eq.~(22) of \cite{Bottcher1994}) and reported numerical results for $L=0,1$ computed using the basis-spline collocation method. Pont and Shakeshaft \cite{Pont1995} derived the RSE (see Eq.~(8--12), (79) and (82)) using unnormalized MBHs with $M=L$ for two-electron atom with clamped nucleus (see Eq.~(\ref{eq:pserror}) above for corrections in these results). Frolov and Smith \cite{Frolov1996} revisited the work of \'{E}fros \cite{Efros1986} and reported the general structure and explicit form of the Hamiltonian matrix elements (see Section~II--IV of \cite{Frolov1996}). Ma and co-workers demonstrated how to separate the rotational degrees of freedom for quantum three-body system with clamped nucleus using Jacobi coordinates and reported the RSE (see Eq.~(12) of \cite{Ma1999}); a generalization of this approach to $N$-particle systems was also offered \cite{Ma2000, Gu2001, Gu2001a}. (Note that Jacobi coordinates complicate considerably the form of the potential energy operator, making it mass dependent.) Meremianin and Briggs \cite{MEREMIANIN2003} developed a universal method for separating the angular variables for $N$ particles employing Jacobi vectors and the algebra of irreducible tensors and derived the explicit form of RSE for three particles (see Eq.~(67) of \cite{MEREMIANIN2003}) and four particles (see Eq.~(79) of \cite{MEREMIANIN2003}). (Note that the form of the potential energy operator in Eq.~(62) of \cite{MEREMIANIN2003} is incorrect as $\bm{\mathrm{r}}_1$ and $\bm{\mathrm{r}}_2$ are mass-scaled Jacobi vectors and not interparticle coordinates.) This brief survey highlights three salient features of the existing literature on the RSE: (i)~most formulations pertain to the clamped-nucleus helium atom, (ii)~derivations commonly rely on irreducible tensor algebra, and (iii)~numerical validation of the resulting expressions---particularly for higher angular momentum states ($L>1$)---remains scarce. In contrast, extensive numerical studies are available for $\textnormal{P}^{\textnormal{o}}$ and $\textnormal{P}^{\textnormal{e}}$ states ($L=1$), primarily building on the foundational work of Breit \cite{Breit1930} (for example, consult the work of Breit \cite{Breit1930a}, Traub and Foley \cite{Traub1959}, Pekeris, Schiff, and Lifson \cite{Pekeris1962a}, Perrin and Stewart \cite{Perrin1963}, Machacek, Sanders, and Scherr \cite{Machacek1964}, Schiff, Pekeris, and Lifson \cite{Schiff1965}, Scherr and Machacek \cite{Scherr1965}, Schiff, Lifson, Pekeris, and Rabinowitz \cite{Schiff1965a}, Van Rensbergen \cite{Rensbergen1972}, Drake and Makowski \cite{Drake1988}, Ho \cite{Ho1990} for different numerical estimations related to $\textnormal{P}^{\textnormal{o}}$ states of two-electron systems and the work of Drake and Dalgarno \cite{Drake1970} for numerical estimations related to $\textnormal{P}^{\textnormal{e}}$ states). However, for $L > 1$, systematic numerical treatments are considerably fewer, though noteworthy contributions by Warner and Blinder \cite{Warner1978}, Kar and Ho \cite{Kar2009, Kar2008, Kar2009a, Kar2009b, Kar2009c, kar2010}, Aznabayev \textit{et al}. \cite{Aznabayev2015, Aznabaev2018}, and Drake \cite{Drake1972, Drake2023} provide reliable data for selected higher $L$ states. 

It is important to mention that the discussion above pertains to separation of angular momentum using the angular basis of bipolar harmonics; an alternative approach based on Wigner functions $\mathcal{D}$ as the angular basis, leading to a variational RSE with more intricate differential operators and generally less clear structure of the resulting multicomponent formalism (see for example, the work of Datta Majumdar \cite{DattaMajumdar1952}, Bhatia and Temkin \cite{BHATIA1964, BHATIA1965}, Kalotas \cite{Kalotas1965}, Nikitin and Ostrovsky \cite{Nikitin1985a}, Dmitrieva and Plindov \cite{Dmitrieva1986}, Mukherjee and Mukherjee \cite{Mukherjee1994, Mukherjee1995}, etc.) is not pursued in the current study.

\subsection{Elimination of angular dependency}
We eliminate the angular dependency from Eq.~\eqref{hamtme} in a two-step process. In the first step, we multiply Eq.~\eqref{hamtme} from the left by the Hermitian transpose of the vector of the angular generators, $\begin{pmatrix}\bm{\Omega}_{d}^{LL\uppi} ~ \bm{\Omega}_{d+1}^{LL\uppi} ... ~\bm{\Omega}_{L}^{LL\uppi}\end{pmatrix}^{\dagger}$. In the second step, we integrate the resulting equation over the angular space using Eq.~(\ref{dtau}) with the Euler angles as the integration variables. It is obvious that the dependence on the angular variables will be eliminated in this way, leaving us only with equations that depend on the internal shape variables $r_1$, $r_2$, and $r_{12}$ (or equivalently: $r_1$, $r_2$, and $\theta$).  This procedure produces the following explicit matrix representation of RSE corresponding formally to a set of coupled PDEs for the partial wave components $\psi_{l}^{L\uppi} \equiv \psi_{l}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12})$ with $l=d,\ldots,L$
\begin{widetext}
\begin{eqnarray}\label{rrse}
    \left(\!\begin{array}{cllc}
        \mathscr{W}_{dd\phantom{\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1}}^{L\uppi} & \mathscr{W}_{d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1\hspace{-0.7pt},d}^{L\uppi} & \!\!\!\!\!\cdots & \mathscr{W}_{Ld}^{L\uppi}\vphantom{\vdots}\\
        \mathscr{W}_{d,d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1}^{L\uppi} & \mathscr{W}_{d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1\hspace{-0.7pt},d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1}^{L\uppi} &\!\!\!\!\!&\vdots\\ 
        \vdots && \!\!\!\!\!\ddots & \vdots\vphantom{\mathscr{W}_{d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1\hspace{-0.7pt},d}^{L\uppi}}\\ 
        \mathscr{W}_{dL}^{L\uppi} &\hspace{9pt}\cdots &  \!\!\!\!\!\cdots  &\mathscr{W}_{LL}^{L\uppi}\vphantom{\vdots}
    \end{array} \right)\!\! \left[\!
    \left(\begin{array}{cclcl}
        \!\!\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{d,d} & \!\!\!\!\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{d,d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1} & \;0\;\;\;\; & \cdots\;\;\;\;\; & \;0\vphantom{\vdots}\\
        \hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1,d} & \!\!\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1,d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1} & \ddots & \ddots\;\;\;\;\; & \;\vdots\\
        0 &\;\ddots &\ddots&\ddots\;\;\;\;\;\;& \;0\\ 
        \vdots &\;\ddots&\ddots & \hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{L\hspace{-0.6pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1,L\hspace{-0.6pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1} & \!\!\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{L\hspace{-0.6pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1,L}\\ 
        0\vphantom{\vdots} & \;\cdots\;\; &  \;0& \hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{L,L\hspace{-0.6pt}-\hspace{-1pt}1} & \!\!\hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{L,L}
    \end{array} \right)\! -\!E \mathbb{1}\right]\!\!
    \left(\!\!\begin{array}{c}
     \psi_{d}^{L\uppi}\vphantom{\vdots}\\
        \psi_{d\hspace{-0.5pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1}^{L\uppi}\vphantom{\vdots}\\
        \vdots\\
        \vdots\\
        \psi_{L}^{L\uppi}\vphantom{\vdots}
    \end{array}\!\! \right)\!\! =\!\! \left(\!\begin{array}{c}
     0\vphantom{\vdots}\\
        0\vphantom{\vdots}\\
        \vdots\\
        \vdots\\
        0\vphantom{\vdots}
    \end{array}\! \right)
\end{eqnarray}
\end{widetext}
The angular integrals $\mathscr{W}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{L\uppi} \equiv \mathscr{W}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},\theta)$ can be computed using the inner product  $\langle \cdot| \cdot \rangle$ defined in Eq.~\eqref{dtau} as
\begin{equation}\label{angularint1}
   \mathscr{W}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{L\uppi}  = \left\langle \left. \bm{\Omega}_{l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{LL\uppi}\right| \bm{\Omega}_{l}^{LL\uppi} \right\rangle
\end{equation}
The computation of these integrals can be simplified by separating the $r_1$ and $r_2$ dependence; we have
\begin{equation}\label{wlpikk}
    \mathscr{W}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{L\uppi} = r_{1}^{l+l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}r_{2}^{2L-l-l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}\!+2d}\left\langle\left.\Omega_{l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{LL\uppi}\right|\Omega_{l}^{LL\uppi}\right\rangle
\end{equation}
Considering the representation of the MBH $\Omega_{l}^{LL\uppi}$ as the linear combination of the Wigner functions $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{LK}$ (see Eq.~\eqref{mbhwdr}) and using the othonormalization condition of $\mathcal{D}_{L}^{LK}$ (see Eq.~\eqref{oncwd}),  it is straightforward to show that
\begin{eqnarray}\label{alpikk1}
    \left\langle\left.\Omega_{l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{LL\uppi}\right|\Omega_{l}^{LL\uppi}\right\rangle = \sum_{K=-L}^{L}\left(\Lambda_{Kl\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{L\uppi}\right)^\ast\Lambda_{Kl}^{L\uppi}
\end{eqnarray}
where the closed-form expressions for $\Lambda_{Kl}^{L\uppi}$ have been given by Eqs.~(\ref{Lambdapi}--\ref{Lambdau}). After a series of transformations and simplifications, the inner product $\langle\Omega_{l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{LL\uppi}|\Omega_{l}^{LL\uppi}\rangle$ in Eq.~\eqref{alpikk1} can be most conveniently expressed as a linear combination of Legendre polynomials
\begin{equation}\label{lpexp}
    P_{\lambda}(\cos{\theta}) = \sum_{q=0}^{\lfloor\frac{\lambda}{2}\rfloor} \tfrac{\left(-1\right)^{q}}{2^{\lambda^{\vphantom{l}}}}\!\! \left(\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle \lambda  \\
         \scriptstyle q
    \end{array}\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2\lambda-2q  \\
         \scriptstyle \lambda
    \end{array}\!\!\right)\left(\cos{\theta}\right)^{\lambda-2q}
\end{equation}
where the floor symbol $\lfloor\frac{\lambda}{2}\rfloor$ stands for the largest integer not exceeding $\frac{\lambda}{2}$. We have
\begin{equation}\label{alpikk2}
    \left\langle\left.\Omega_{l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{LL\uppi}\right|\Omega_{l}^{LL\uppi}\right\rangle= \hspace{-4pt}\sum_{p=0}^{\text{min}\left(l,l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}\right)}\hspace{-4pt}\mathcal{C}^{L\uppi}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.0pt}p}\hspace{4pt}P_{l+l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}-2p}(\cos{\theta})
\end{equation}
where the coefficients $\mathcal{C}^{L\uppi}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.0pt}p}$ for the natural parity states ($\uppi=n$) are given by
\begin{eqnarray}\label{clpikkp}
\mathcal{C}^{Ln}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.0pt}p}\! &=&\! \tfrac{(-1)^{\hspace{-0.5pt}p}}{2\,p!}\!\sqrt{\tfrac{\left(\!\frac{3}{2}\!\right)_{\hspace{-1pt}l}\hspace{3pt}\left(\!\frac{3}{2}\!\right)_{\hspace{-0.8pt}l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}\hspace{1pt}\left(\!l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}-L\hspace{-1.5pt}\right)_{\hspace{-1.5pt}l\!-\hspace{-0.5pt}p}\left(\!l\phantom{\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}}-L\!\right)_{\hspace{-1.5pt}l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}-\hspace{-0.5pt}p}}{l!\hspace{3pt}l^{'\!}!\hspace{3pt}\left(\!p-L-{\frac{1}{2}}\!\right)_{\hspace{-1.0pt}l\!-\hspace{-0.5pt}p}\hspace{2pt}\left(\!p-L-{\frac{1}{2}}\!\right)_{\hspace{-1.0pt}l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}-\hspace{-0.5pt}p}}}\\
&&\cdot \tfrac{\left(\!-l\phantom{\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}\!\!\right)_{\hspace{-1.5pt}p}\!\left(\!-l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}\!\right)_{\hspace{-1.5pt}p}\!\left(-l-l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}+\frac{1}{2}\right)_{\hspace{-1.5pt}p}\!\left(l+l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}+\frac{1}{2}-2p\right)\!\left({\frac{1}{2}}\right)_{\hspace{-1.0pt}l\!-\hspace{-0.5pt}p}\!\left({\frac{1}{2}}\right)_{\hspace{-1.0pt}l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}-\hspace{-0.5pt}p}}{\left(l+l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}+{\frac{1}{2}}-p\right)\left({\frac{1}{2}}\right)_{\hspace{-1.0pt}l+l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}}\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
while for the unnatural parity states ($\uppi=u$), by
\begin{eqnarray}\label{clpikkp1}
\mathcal{C}^{Lu}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.0pt}p}\! &=&\! \tfrac{(-1)^{\hspace{-0.5pt}p+\hspace{-1pt}1}}{2\,p!}\!\sqrt{\tfrac{\left(\!\frac{3}{2}\!\right)_{\hspace{-1pt}l}\left(\!\frac{3}{2}\!\right)_{\hspace{-0.8pt}l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}\left(\!l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}-L-1\hspace{-1.5pt}\right)_{\hspace{-1.5pt}l\!-\hspace{-0.5pt}p}\!\left(\!l\phantom{\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}}-L-1\!\right)_{\hspace{-1.5pt}l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}-\hspace{-0.5pt}p}}{l!\hspace{3pt}l^{'\!}!\hspace{3pt}\left(\!p-L-{\frac{3}{2}}\!\right)_{\hspace{-1.0pt}l\!-\hspace{-0.5pt}p}\left(\!p-L-{\frac{3}{2}}\!\right)_{\hspace{-1.0pt}l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}-\hspace{-0.5pt}p}}}\\
&&\cdot\!\tfrac{\left(\!-l\phantom{\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}\!\right)_{\hspace{-1.5pt}p}\!\left(\!-l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}\!\right)_{\hspace{-1.5pt}p}\!\left(-l-l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}+\frac{1}{2}\right)_{\hspace{-1.5pt}p}\!\left(l+l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}+\frac{1}{2}-2p\right)\!\left({\frac{1}{2}}\right)_{\hspace{-1.0pt}l\!-\hspace{-0.5pt}p}\!\left({\frac{1}{2}}\right)_{\hspace{-1.0pt}l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}-\hspace{-0.5pt}p}}{\left(l+l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}+{\frac{1}{2}}-p\right)\left({\frac{1}{2}}\right)_{\hspace{-1.0pt}l+l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}}\nonumber\\
&& \cdot\!\tfrac{\left(\!2\left(\!l\phantom{'}\!\!-p\!\right)\!\left(\!l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}-\hspace{-0.5pt}p\!\right)-p\!\right)\!\left(\!L-l-l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}+\hspace{-0.5pt}p+1\!\right)+\left(\!l\phantom{'}\!\!-p\!\right)\!\left(\!l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}-\hspace{-0.5pt}p\right)\!\left(\!2l+2l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}+1-2p\right)}{\sqrt{l\,l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}\left(\!L-l\phantom{\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}}+1\!\right)\left(\!L-l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}+1\phantom{'}\hspace{-4pt}\right)}}\nonumber
\end{eqnarray}
For practical purposes, it is also useful to give an explicit expression for $\mathscr{W}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{L\uppi}$ as a function of $r_1$, $r_2$, and $r_{12}=\sqrt{r_1^2+r_2^2-2r_1r_2\cos\theta}$, which can be useful for coding the usual Hylleraas-like scheme for non-vanishing values of $L$. The formula for  $\mathscr{W}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{L\uppi} \equiv \mathscr{W}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12})$ reads
\begin{eqnarray}\label{wlpikk1}
    \mathscr{W}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt}}^{L\uppi}\hspace{-2pt} &=&\hspace{-10pt}\sum_{p=0}^{\text{min}(l,l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-0.3pt})}\!\sum_{q=0}^{\lfloor\frac{\lambda}{2}\rfloor}\sum_{t=0}^{\lambda-2q}\sum_{s=0}^{t} \tfrac{\left(-1\right)^{q}}{4^{\lambda^{\vphantom{l}}\!-\hspace{-0.5pt}q}}\! \left(\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle \lambda  \\
         \scriptstyle q
    \end{array}\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle 2\lambda-2q  \\
         \scriptstyle \lambda
    \end{array}\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle \lambda-2q  \\
         \scriptstyle t
    \end{array}\!\!\right)\!\!\left(\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle t  \\
         \scriptstyle s
    \end{array}\!\!\right) \nonumber\\
    && \hspace{-15pt}\! \cdot\mathcal{C}^{L\uppi}_{ll\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.0pt}p}~ r_{1}^{2(\lambda+p-q-t)}~r_{2}^{2(L+d-\lambda-p+q+t-s)}~ r_{12}^{2s}
\end{eqnarray}
where $\lambda=l+l\hspace{-0.3pt}'\hspace{-1.5pt}-2p$.
\subsection{Explicit form of the \text{RSE}s for \texorpdfstring{$\text{S}$, $\text{P}$, $\text{D}$ and $\text{F}$}~ states}

The formulas presented in the previous Sections of this paper might seem complicated. This impression may originate from the fact that the presented expressions are universal, being valid for every angular momentum $L$ and every parity $\uppi$. In fact, the resulting formulas for particular small values of $L$, i.e., those most useful in practical applications of the presented theory are rather simple. In this Section, we present a number of such formulas together with the explicit form of the resulting block Hamiltonians in order to demonstrate to the reader how to use the derived here theory in practice.
