\section{Numerical results}\label{numerical}
\subsection{Algebraic eigenequation}

By expanding each of the partial wave components $\psi_{l}^{L\uppi}(r_{1},r_{2},r_{12})$ in terms of a suitably chosen basis set consisting of $\texttt{N}_{ab}$ basis functions (for details, see Section~\ref{s:basis} below), and applying the Rayleigh-Ritz variational principle, it is straightforward to show that Eq.~\eqref{rrse} reduces to a generalized eigenvalue problem
\begin{eqnarray}\label{gep}
\mathbb{H}~ \mathrm{c} = E~\mathbb{S} ~\mathrm{c}
\end{eqnarray}
where $\mathbb{H}$ and $\mathbb{S}$ are square matrices, both of dimension $\texttt{N}_{ab}(L\!-\!d\!+\!1) \times \texttt{N}_{ab}(L\!-\!d\!+\!1)$, $\mathrm{c}$ is the column vector of dimension $\texttt{N}_{ab}(L\!-\!d\!+\!1)$ containing the linear variational coefficients, and $E$ represents the numerical estimate of the corresponding energy eigenvalue. The matrices $\mathbb{H}$ and $\mathbb{S}$ have block structure with $(L-d+1)^2$ blocks, each of size $\texttt{N}_{ab} \times \texttt{N}_{ab}$. Let us denote the blocks of the $\mathbb{H}$ and $\mathbb{S}$ matrices by $\mathbb{H}^{mn}$ and $\mathbb{S}^{mn}$, respectively, with $m$ and $n$ ranging from $d$ to $L$. The matrix elements of the block $\mathbb{H}^{mn}$ are given by
\begin{eqnarray}\label{bmh}
    \mathbb{H}^{mn}_{ij} = \left\langle b_{i}\left\vert \sum_{k=d}^{L} \mathscr{W}_{km}^{L\uppi}\hspace{2pt}\hat{\mathcal{H}}_{kn}\right\vert b_{j}\right\rangle
\end{eqnarray}
with $b_{k}$ representing the $k^{\text{th}}$ basis function and 
\begin{equation}
    \hat{\mathcal{H}}_{kn} = \left\{\begin{array}{ll}
       0  &  \text{~~when $\vert k-n\vert > 1$}\\
       \hat{\mathcal{H}}^{L\uppi}_{kn}  & \text{~~otherwise}
    \end{array}\right.
\end{equation}
with the operators $\mathcal{H}^{L\uppi}_{kn}$ defined in Eqs.~(\ref{hkkgnygp2}--\ref{hkk-1gnygp2}). Analogously, the matrix elements of the block $\mathbb{S}^{mn}$ are given by
\begin{eqnarray}\label{bms}
    \mathbb{S}^{mn}_{ij} = \left\langle b_{i}\left\vert  \mathscr{W}_{nm}^{L\uppi}\right\vert b_{j}\right\rangle
\end{eqnarray}
The angular integrals $\mathscr{W}_{ij}^{L\uppi}$ in Eqs.~\eqref{bmh} and~\eqref{bms} are explicitly defined in Eq.~\eqref{wlpikk1}.

The generalized eigenvalue problem in Eq.~(\ref{gep}) can be transformed to the usual eigenvalue problem by the standard procedure. Using the eigendecoposition of the positive-definite matrix $\mathbb{S}$
\begin{eqnarray}\label{evds}
    \mathbb{S} = \mathbb{X}~\mathbb{\Lambda}~\mathbb{X}^{-1}
\end{eqnarray}
and the resulting associated square-root matrices
\begin{eqnarray}\label{evdshalf}
     \mathbb{S}^{\pm \frac{1}{2}} = \mathbb{X}~\mathbb{\Lambda}^{\pm \frac{1}{2}}~\mathbb{X}^{-1}
\end{eqnarray}
it can be shown that the associated eigenvalue problem
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eve}
    \widetilde{\mathbb{H}}~\widetilde{\mathrm{c}} = E~\widetilde{\mathrm{c}}
\end{eqnarray}
with $\widetilde{\mathbb{H}} = \mathbb{S}^{-\frac{1}{2}}\mathbb{H}\mathbb{S}^{-\frac{1}{2}}$ and $\widetilde{\mathrm{c}}= \mathbb{S}^{\frac{1}{2}}\mathrm{c}$ is isospectral with the generalized eigenvalue problem given by Eq.~(\ref{gep}).

\subsection{Construction of basis functions} \label{s:basis}
The partial wave components $\psi_{l}^{L\uppi}\left(r_{1}, r_{2}, r_{12}\right)$ are constructed as linear combinations of suitable basis functions
\begin{eqnarray}\label{basis0}
    \psi_{l}^{L\uppi}(r_{1}\hspace{-1pt},\hspace{-1pt}r_{2},\hspace{-1pt}r_{12}) = \sum_{i=1}^{\texttt{N}_{a}}\sum_{j=1}^{\texttt{N}_{b}} \mathscr{C}_{lij}^{L\uppi}\upphi_{ij}^l (r_{1}\hspace{-1pt},\hspace{-1pt}r_{2},\hspace{-1pt}r_{12})
\end{eqnarray}
with variational coefficients  $\mathscr{C}_{lij}^{L\uppi}$. The basis functions $\upphi_{ij}^{l}\equiv \upphi_{ij}^{l}(r_{1}\hspace{-1pt},\hspace{-1pt}r_{2},\hspace{-1pt}r_{12})$ are selected here in the following form
 \begin{eqnarray}\label{rwf}
    \upphi_{ij}^l = r_{1}^{\texttt{l}_{j}} r_{2}^{\texttt{m}_{j}} r_{12}^{\texttt{n}_{j}}~e^{-\zeta_{i} r_{1}-\eta_{i} r_{2} - \xi_{i} r_{12}}
\end{eqnarray}
where ($\texttt{l}_{j}$,$\texttt{m}_{j}$,$\texttt{n}_{j}$) is a triple of non-negative integers, and ($\zeta_{i}, \eta_{i}, \xi_{i}$) is a triple of exponents satisfying the condition that the sum of any two of them is always positive. The constants $\texttt{N}_{a}$ and $\texttt{N}_{b}$ in Eq.~\eqref{rwf} have the following meaning: $\texttt{N}_{b}$ represents the number of distinct triples of integers ($\texttt{l}_{j}$,$\texttt{m}_{j}$,$\texttt{n}_{j}$) and $\texttt{N}_{a}$ represents the number of distinct triples of exponents ($\zeta_{i}, \eta_{i}, \xi_{i}$) used in the expansion. The exponents ($\zeta_{i}, \eta_{i}, \xi_{i}$) constitute non-linear optimization parameters to be determined together with the optimal linear expansion coefficients $\mathscr{C}_{lij}^{L\uppi}$ during the solution of the variational problem.  Different sets of triples ($\texttt{l}_{j}$,$\texttt{m}_{j}$,$\texttt{n}_{j}$) are generated maintaining the condition $\texttt{l}_{j}\!+\!\texttt{m}_{j}\!+\!\texttt{n}_{j}\leq \texttt{w}$, where \texttt{w} is a positive integer related to $\texttt{N}_{b}$ by a simple relation
\begin{eqnarray}
    \texttt{N}_{b}=\left(\!\!\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle \texttt{w}+3  \\
         \scriptstyle 3
    \end{array}\!\!\right)
\end{eqnarray} 
It is to be remembered that for the given values of $\texttt{N}_{a}$ and $\texttt{N}_{b}$ the number of basis functions, $\texttt{N}_{ab}=\texttt{N}_{a}\!\cdot \texttt{N}_{b}$, determines the dimensions ($\texttt{N}_{ab}\times \texttt{N}_{ab}$) of the block matrices $\mathbb{H}^{mn}$ and $\mathbb{S}^{mn}$ appearing in Eqs.~\eqref{bmh} and~\eqref{bms}, respectively. 

Taking into account the structure of the basis function $\upphi_{ij}^{l}$ in Eq.~\eqref{rwf}, a straightforward calculation reveals that the integrals arising from the matrix elements of the eigenvalue problem in Eq.~\eqref{eve} can be expressed in the following closed form
\begin{eqnarray}
    &&\int\limits_{0}^{\infty}\hspace{-3pt}dr_{1}\hspace{-3pt}\int\limits_{0}^{\infty}\hspace{-3pt}dr_{2}\hspace{-10pt}\int\limits_{\vert \hspace{-0.5pt}r_{1}\hspace{-1pt}-r_{2}\hspace{-1pt}\vert}^{r_{1}\hspace{-1pt}+r_{2}}\hspace{-10pt}dr_{12} \, r_{1}^{\texttt{k}_{1}}r_{2}^{\texttt{k}_{2}}r_{12}^{\texttt{k}_{3}}\,e^{-\left(\zeta r_{1}+\eta r_{2}+\xi r_{12}\right)}\\[2pt] &=&\frac{\partial^{\texttt{k}_{1}}}{\partial \zeta^{\texttt{k}_{1}}} \frac{\partial^{\texttt{k}_{2}}}{\partial \eta^{\texttt{k}_{2}}} \frac{\partial^{\texttt{k}_{3}}}{\partial \xi^{\texttt{k}_{3}}} \frac{2}{(\zeta+\eta)(\eta+\xi)(\zeta+\xi)} \nonumber\\[3pt] \nonumber
    &=& \sum_{\texttt{j}_{1}=0}^{\texttt{k}_{1}}\sum_{\texttt{j}_{2}=0}^{\texttt{k}_{2}}\sum_{\texttt{j}_{3}=0}^{\texttt{k}_{3}}\!\tfrac{2\,\texttt{k}_{1}\hspace{-1pt}!\hspace{1pt}\texttt{k}_{2}\hspace{-1pt}!\hspace{1pt}\texttt{k}_{3}\hspace{-1pt}!\hspace{1pt}\!\left(\hspace{-4pt}\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle \texttt{k}_{1}\hspace{-1pt}-\hspace{-1pt}\texttt{j}_{1}\hspace{-1pt}+\hspace{-1pt}\texttt{j}_{2}  \\
         \scriptstyle \texttt{j}_{2}
    \end{array}\hspace{-5pt}\right)\!\left(\hspace{-4pt}\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle \texttt{k}_{2}\hspace{-1pt}-\hspace{-1pt}\texttt{j}_{2}\hspace{-1pt}+\hspace{-1pt}\texttt{j}_{3}  \\
         \scriptstyle \texttt{j}_{3}
    \end{array}\hspace{-5pt}\right)\!\left(\hspace{-4pt}\begin{array}{c}
         \scriptstyle \texttt{k}_{3}\hspace{-1pt}-\hspace{-1pt}\texttt{j}_{3}\hspace{-1pt}+\hspace{-1pt}\texttt{j}_{1}  \\
         \scriptstyle \texttt{j}_{1}
    \end{array}\hspace{-5pt}\right)}{(\!\zeta\hspace{-1pt}+\eta\!)^{\texttt{k}_{1}\hspace{-1pt}-\hspace{-1pt}\texttt{j}_{1}\hspace{-1pt}+\hspace{-1pt}\texttt{j}_{2}\hspace{-1pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1^{\vphantom{l}}}(\!\eta+\xi\hspace{-1pt})^{\texttt{k}_{2}\hspace{-1pt}-\hspace{-1pt}\texttt{j}_{2}\hspace{-1pt}+\hspace{-1pt}\texttt{j}_{3}\hspace{-1pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1}(\!\zeta\hspace{-1pt}+\hspace{-1pt}\xi\hspace{-1pt})^{\texttt{k}_{3}\hspace{-1pt}-\hspace{-1pt}\texttt{j}_{3}\hspace{-1pt}+\hspace{-1pt}\texttt{j}_{1}\hspace{-1pt}+\hspace{-1pt}1}}
\end{eqnarray}
where $\texttt{k}_1$, $\texttt{k}_2$, and $\texttt{k}_3$ are nonnegative integers, and where the exponents $(\zeta, \eta, \xi)$ are real numbers, subject to the condition that the sum of any two must be positive to ensure the convergence of the triple integral (see Section~III of \cite{Calais1962} and text around Eq.~(38) of \cite{Harris2004} for details).

For a system containing two identical fermion particles with spin $s=\sfrac{1}{2}$, a proper spin-adaptation of the formalism---originating from the permutational symmetry of the system under the exchange of identical fermions---can reduce the size of the resulting eigenproblem by about half, simultaneously separating the eigensolutions for the singlet and triplet states. For states with vanishing angular momentum, $L = 0$, the spin-adaptation can be performed at the level of constructing a symmetry-adapted basis set for the singlet and triplet spin states. For higher angular momentum, $L > 0$, where the angular degrees of freedom have been eliminated at the cost of introducing multiple partial components $\psi_{l}^{L\uppi}\left(r_{1}, r_{2}, r_{12}\right)$, the situation is more intricate and requires more detailed considerations going beyond a simple spin-adaptation of the basis functions. We are planning to address this issue in detail in a forthcoming paper.

\subsection{Results and discussion}
Numerical verification of the described here formalism is performed by estimating the nonrelativistic energies for several low-angular-momentum natural ($d=0$) and unnatural ($d=1$) parity states of the helium atom. An explicitly correlated Hylleraas-type multi-exponent basis is employed within the framework of the Rayleigh-Ritz variational principle. The optimized energy eigenvalues for all states under consideration, along with the corresponding optimal variational coefficients $\mathscr{C}_{lij}^{L\uppi}$ and exponents ($\zeta_{i}, \eta_{i}, \xi_{i}$), are obtained by diagonalizing the eigenvalue Eq.~\eqref{eve}. The exponents are optimized using the Nelder-Mead optimization algorithm \cite{Nelder1965, Gao2012}. All numerical calculations are performed using a code written in the Julia programming language \cite{julia_bezanson_2017}, capable of performing the calculations for arbitrary angular momentum $L$ and parity $\uppi$.

The estimated energy eigenvalues for singlet and triplet states of helium with natural parity are presented in Table~\ref{tab_eng_np}. Two lowest energy eigenvalues for each $L \leq 7$ and $S=0,1$ are computed with a single, double, and triple set of exponents. The mass ratio of the alpha particle to the electron has been taken as $7294.299\,541\,71$, based on CODATA 2022 \cite{codata2022}. The accuracy of the estimated numerical values is verified by comparing the results with the accurate results of Drake \cite{Drake1993} (last column of Table~\ref{tab_eng_np}). The reference values are estimated using Eq.~(3.3.2) of~\cite{Drake1993} considering the same alpha particle-to-electron mass ratio as in the present work.

Much less information is available in the literature on accurate energetics of the singlet and triplet states of helium with unnatural parity. In Table~\ref{tab_eng_up} we have compiled the most accurate energy estimates of the low-lying states with unnatural parity reported in the literature; these results correspond either to clamped nucleus results (i.e., results computed within the Born-Oppenheimer approximation) or to results computed with a largely inaccurate mass of the alpha particle ($m_{3}=7294.261\,824\,1$) used before by Hesse and Baye \cite{Hesse2001}.  The reported here formalism reproduces these results well. No energy values are available in the literature for the accurate finite nuclear mass of helium ($m_{3}=7294.299\,541\,71$) \cite{codata2022}; our results seem to be the first set of accurate energy values for these states.

