\begin{abstract}
Watermarking is a promising defense against the misuse of large language models (LLMs), yet it remains vulnerable to scrubbing and spoofing attacks.
This vulnerability stems from an inherent trade-off governed by watermark window size: 
smaller windows resist scrubbing better but are easier to reverse-engineer, enabling low-cost statistics-based spoofing attacks. 
This work breaks this trade-off by introducing a novel mechanism, equivalent texture keys, where multiple tokens within a watermark window can independently support the detection. 
Based on the redundancy, we propose a novel watermark scheme with \textbf{S}ub-vocabulary decomposed \textbf{E}quivalent t\textbf{E}xture \textbf{K}ey (\textbf{SEEK}). 
It achieves a Pareto improvement, increasing the resilience against scrubbing attacks without compromising robustness to spoofing. 
% Our experimental results show that our method outperforms previously considered secure methods and achieves a Pareto-optimal trade-off, with spoofing robustness gains of +88.2\%/+92.3\%/+82.0\% and scrubbing robustness gains of +10.2\%/+6.4\%/+24.6\% across diverse datasets. 
Experiments demonstrate SEEK's superiority over prior method, yielding spoofing robustness gains of +88.2\%/+92.3\%/+82.0\% and scrubbing robustness gains of +10.2\%/+6.4\%/+24.6\% across diverse dataset settings.
% Our anonymous code submission accompanies this paper and will be released after anonymous reviews.
% Our anonymous codes are submitted with the paper and will be publicly available.
\end{abstract}
% Watermarking is a promising technique for mitigating the misuse of large language models, but remains vulnerable to two major types of attacks: scrubbing and spoofing. Recent studies reveal that adversaries can perform statistical watermark stealing to reverse-engineer watermark rules, enabling low-cost spoofing attacks that compromise previously secure schemes. Existing methods face an inherent trade-off between robustness to scrubbing and spoofing. In this work, we enhance watermark robustness by introducing an equivalent texture key mechanism that enables watermark deployment over a larger token window. This design increases the difficulty of watermark extraction while preserving resilience against scrubbing attacks. Our experimental results show that our method outperforms existing watermarking frameworks and reaches a Pareto-optimal trade-off between robustness to scrubbing and resistance to watermark-stealing-based spoofing attacks.

