One proactive approach involves watermarking, wherein the LLM's output distribution is subtly altered to embed an imperceptible signature within the generated text~\citep{KWG1,kuditipudi2023robust,fairoze2023publicly,zhao2023provable}. This signature can subsequently be verified by a detector equipped with a secret key held by the LLM provider. Compared to post-hoc detection methods~\citep{gehrmann2019gltr,mitchell2023detectgpt,hovy2016enemy, zellers2019defending}, watermarking offers notable advantages, including the preservation of output quality and a provably low false positive rate (below $10^{-3}$)~\citep{hans2401spotting}. However, watermarking remains vulnerable to two types of adversarial attacks: 1)~\textbf{scrubbing attacks} which generate a semantically equivalent paraphrase to disturb watermark patterns, thereby bypassing the watermark detection and  2)~\textbf{spoofing attacks} which mimic the watermark patterns to inject target watermarks into harmful text, fabricating content that appears to be generated by the victim LLM.

