\textit{How can watermarking schemes be enhanced to simultaneously ensure robustness against scrubbing and resistance to spoofing attacks?}

% Previous researches~\citep{kuditipudi2023robust,pang2024strength, steal,KWG2} are limited by employing this predefined watermark window (typically the preceding $h$ tokens of protected text) to optimize the trade-off between robustness against scrubbing and spoofing attacks by adjusting the watermark window size. In our work, we argue that the position and size of the watermark window are equally critical. The position of watermarks constitutes critical information. Token frequency attacks inherently exploit knowledge of the predefined watermark window positions. Thus, we propose that watermark placements should not be explicitly disclosed or predefined. Instead, the elements of the watermark window should be dispersed across a substantially larger context, or even full-context, to significantly increase the statistical computational complexity, thereby making spoofing attacks computationally prohibitive or infeasible. Additionally, we enhance existing red-green vocabulary partitioning strategies by proposing the watermark window with a dynamically adjustable size. Under this improved scheme, adversaries must remove every element within the watermark window to infer the watermark, thereby significantly increasing robustness against scrubbing attacks. Our contributions are the following:

% Previous researches~\citep{kuditipudi2023robust,pang2024strength, steal,KWG2} are limited by employing a small predefined watermark window (typically the preceding $h$ tokens of protected text) to optimize the trade-off between robustness against scrubbing and spoofing attacks by adjusting the watermark window size. 

% In our work, we argue that the position and size of the watermark window are equally critical. The position of watermarks constitutes critical information. Token frequency attacks inherently exploit knowledge of the predefined watermark window positions. Thus, we propose that watermark placements should not be disclosed or predefined. Instead, the elements of the watermark window should be dispersed across a substantially larger context, or even full-context, to significantly increase the statistical computational complexity, thereby making spoofing attacks computationally prohibitive or infeasible. Additionally, we enhance existing red-green vocabulary partitioning strategies by proposing the watermark window with a dynamically adjustable size. Under this improved scheme, adversaries must remove every element within the watermark window to infer the watermark, thereby significantly increasing robustness against scrubbing attacks. Our contributions are the following:
% Existing watermarking techniques typically fail to achieve strong defenses against both scrubbing and spoofing attacks simultaneously.
To address this question, we investigate an overlooked factor influencing robustness against scrubbing attacks beyond the watermark window size. We identify a phenomenon of equivalent texture keys, where multiple tokens within a watermark window can independently support the detection of the watermark pattern at the subsequent timestep. This redundancy enables a path towards Pareto improvements: by increasing the number of equivalent texture keys, scrubbing robustness can be significantly enhanced even when employing large watermark windows, without compromising resistance to spoofing attacks. 

Building on this insight, our initial approach increases the prevalence of equivalent texture keys by reducing the output space of the hash function typically used in watermarking. However, recognizing that a smaller hash output space could potentially degrade text quality, we introduce our final watermark scheme, \textbf{SEEK} (\textbf{S}ub-vocabulary decomposed \textbf{E}quivalent t\textbf{E}xture \textbf{K}ey). SEEK ingeniously mitigates this quality concern by decoupling the watermark construction process across disjoint sub-vocabularies. This allows for a high density of equivalent texture keys, bolstering robustness, while simultaneously preserving the quality of the generated text.
