\section{Related work}

\textbf{LLM Watermarking technology.} Watermarking natural language faces challenges due to the discrete and symbolic nature of text~\citep{stefan2000information}. Early methods employed rule-based techniques such as syntactic restructuring~\citep{atallah2001natural}, synonym substitution~\citep{topkara2006hiding}, and paraphrasing~\citep{atallah2002natural}. Foundational pioneering work in LLM watermarking includes KGW~\citep{KWG1,KWG2} and AAR~\citep{arr} builds watermarks on statistics for a low and provable false positive rate. Specifically, watermark detection was framed as a hypothesis testing problem, where the alternative hypothesis models text generation from a modified distribution. The KGW method partitions the vocabulary into a "green" list and a "red" list to modify the next token's distribution,  which significantly influenced numerous follow-up watermark works~\citep{kuditipudi2023robust,mao2024watermark, sweet,zhao2023provable,liu2023unforgeable,lu2024entropy}. Our work focuses on the security properties of KGW-family watermarking schemes.

% The UNIGRAM~\citep{kuditipudi2023robust} applies fixed green lists to remove contextual dependency, emphasizing robustness to scrubbing over the comparatively minor threat of spoofing.  This line of research has further evolved to incorporate semantic information~\citep{hou2023semstamp,ren2023robust}, embedding multi-bit information~\citep{CWTL,yoo2023advancing}, and exploring watermarking in low-entropy settings such as code generation~\citep{sweet,EWD}.



\textbf{Scrubbing attacks.} Scrubbing attacks fundamentally stem from text editing operations. Early studies~\cite{zhao2023provable,kuditipudi2023robust, KWG1} evaluated the robustness of watermarking schemes using simple perturbations such as random word deletion or substitution. In addition, some work~\citep{KWG2,zhao2023provable} has explored manual paraphrasing by human annotators to assess watermark removal under high-quality rewriting. Another line of work aims to develop black-box scrubbing attacks, like ~\citet{zhang2023watermarks} and \citet{sadasivan2023can} propose recursive paraphrasing pipelines, while \citet{huang2024b} formulate the scrubbing task as a constrained optimization problem. \citet{dipper} propose a systematic approach to evade AI-generated text detection by training a paraphrasing model.

\textbf{Spoofing attacks.} Early work~\citep{sadasivan2023can,hou2023semstamp,ghosal2023towards,gu2023learnability} has demonstrated proof-of-concept spoofing against simple $h=1$ watermark schemes known as KGW-Left~\citep{KWG1}. \citet{gu2023learnability} propose a distillation-based spoofing method, which requires considerable training overhead. \citet{zhang2024large} formalize spoofing as a mixed-integer programming problem; however, their method depends on access to the model's full vocabulary and tokenizer. In contrast, \citet{steal} proposes a highly effective statistics-based spoofing attack that approximates the watermark distribution via token frequency analysis, without any model-specific access. By learning watermark prefix \( p_w(x_n|x_{n-h:n-1}) \) and non-watermark prefix \( p_m(x_n|x_{n-h:n-1}) \), they successfully spoof KGW-Min~\cite{KWG2} for under \$50. 


