\textbf{Derivation of the cipher $\theta_n$.}
The cipher $\theta_n=\xi\cdot\zeta$ consists of two components: a \textit{secret key} $\xi$ held by the service provider and a \textit{texture key}~\citep{wu2023resilient} $\zeta$ derived from the watermark window. Specifically, given a hash function $H$ with a hash space of $\{1,2,3,...,d\}$, a hash signature $I=\{H(x_{n-k})|1\leq k\leq h\}$ of current watermark window is firstly calculated. Then an aggregation function $f: \mathbb{N}^{h} \to \mathbb{N}$ is applied on the signature to generate the texture key $\zeta$. Among several variants of the aggregation function, the $f=$\textit{Min} variant exhibits superior robustness~\citep{ghosal2023towards,liu2024survey}, which is formulated as:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:seed}
\theta_n =\zeta\cdot\xi=f(I)\cdot \xi=\operatorname{Min} I \cdot\xi =  \operatorname{Min}\left\{ H(x_{n-h})\cdot\xi, \dots, H(x_{{n-1}})\cdot\xi)  \right\}
\end{equation}
We also present other aggregation function $f$ variants in detail in Appendix~\ref{sec:KGW_Variants}. 

\subsection{Threat Models}
Following previous works \citep{steal,kuditipudi2023robust,pang2024strength,huang2024b}, the threat models involve two primary entities: \textit{victim} and \textit{attacker}. The victim operates a special language model $\mathcal{M}$ protected by watermarking and provides services externally via an API. The attacker aims to compromise the watermark detection algorithm through either scrubbing attacks or spoofing attacks. Under standard security assumptions (i.e., Kerckhoffs's principle \citep{kerckhoffs1883cryptographie}), we follow \cite{wu2024bypassing, steal} to assume the attacker has complete knowledge of the watermarking scheme, and can query the victim model via an API interface. 


\textbf{Scrubbing’s objective.}
Scrubbing attacks refer to adversarial attempts to remove watermarks from protected text. Formally, given a watermarked response, an attacker aims at producing a semantically equivalent paraphrased output classified as non-watermarked text by the victim's detector. 

\textbf{Spoofing’s objective.} The objective of a spoofing attack is to construct a watermarking model \( \mathcal{M}' \) that mimics the text distribution of the victim model \( \mathcal{M} \). Subsequently, the attacker is able to generate a malicious message that is classified as watermarked text by the victim's detector.




