\label{sec:motivation}
\textit{\textbf{Finding I}: Statistical spoofing attacks suffer from sparsity: as the watermark window increases, the sample complexity grows exponentially, thereby reducing spoofing success rates.}

The efficacy of statistics-based spoofing~\citep{steal} stems from the statistical analysis of the watermark window, enabling the attacker to estimate the mapping between watermark windows and corresponding watermarked tokens. Intuitively, the complexity of such attacks is determined by the diversity of watermark windows. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:toutu} and~\ref{fig:sparsity}, increasing the size of the watermark window leads to a substantial decline in spoofing success rates. This observation aligns with the sparsity challenge highlighted in~\citep{steal}, where sample complexity in statistical spoofing attacks scales as \(\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{V}|^h)\). 

\textit{\textbf{Finding II}: For watermarking schemes with the same window size, reducing the cardinality of the hash space improves robustness against scrubbing attacks without compromising spoofing resistance.}
%the equivalent texture key of the watermark increases

% \red{Huanming} Traditional watermarking methods often use collision-free hash functions, resulting in a single texture key within the watermark window. Under such settings, altering this single key suffices to eliminate the corresponding watermark token. In contrast, our experiments in Table~\ref{tab:multi-texture-key} show that employing a watermark scheme with a collision-prone hash function enhances the watermark's robustness against scrubbing. The improved robustness arises from hash collisions among tokens in the watermark window, which produce multiple equivalent texture keys that must all be removed to eliminate the watermark. This redundancy significantly enhances robustness: an attacker must now modify all colliding tokens to remove the watermark, substantially increasing the cost of scrubbing. Viewed from this perspective, UNIGRAM~\citep{ren2023robust} can be interpreted as a special case with the minimal hash space dimension $d=1$.
