We investigate a variant of KGW that operates over a reduced hash space, thereby increasing the likelihood of hash collisions\footnote{A hash collision occurs when two tokens in a watermark window yield same hash value under $H(\cdot)$.} when deriving texture keys in Eq \ref{eq:seed}. 
As shown in Table~\ref{tab:multi-texture-key}, this variant achieves markedly gains in robustness against scrubbing attacks while maintaining watermark window size. We attribute the improvement to the increased frequency of hash collisions in the watermark window, which enables multiple tokens to independently reconstruct texture key $\zeta$ for valid detection. 

Consider KGW-MIN as an example, where texture key $\zeta=\operatorname{Min}\left\{ H(x_{n-h}), \dots, H(x_{{n-1}}))\right\}$. In the presence of frequent hash collisions, multiple tokens $x_j$ in the watermark window may satisfy $H(x_j)=\operatorname{Min} I$, enabling each to independently support watermark detection. We refer to this phenomenon as the emergence of \textit{Equivalent Texture Keys}. This redundancy enhances robustness: an attacker now needs to disturb all equivalent texture key tokens to remove the watermark. The challenge of scrubbing attacks towards our proposed variant can be formulated as follows:
\begin{proposition}
Given a hash function with space dimension $d$ and a watermark window size $h$, the probability of a hash collision occurring can be approximated by $p(h, d) \geq 1 - e^{\frac{-h(h-1)}{2d}}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proposition}
The expected number of tokens that must be erased to eliminate the watermark is expressed as $\sum_{m=1}^d \frac{h}{d} \left( \frac{d - m + 1}{d} \right)^{h-1}$, which is monotonically non-increasing in $d$ (strictly decreasing for $h\geq2$).
\end{proposition}

Formal proofs and corresponding visualizations for these propositions are presented in Appendix~\ref{sec:method}. From this perspective, UNIGRAM~\citep{ren2023robust}, a scheme known for its exceptional resilience to scrubbing, can also be interpreted as a special case of our proposed variant with the minimal hash space of cardinality $d=1$. On the other hand, reducing the hash space size does not influence the watermark window size, thereby maintaining similar spoofing resistance. Experimental results in Figure~\ref{fig:sparsity} indicate that the success rate of spoofing attacks is not affected by $d$. 

% These findings suggest that introducing more equivalent texture keys offers a form of pareto improvement. It can strengthen resistance against scrubbing-based attacks without compromising resistance to spoofing, which breaks the dilemma of watermarking.
Taken together, these two findings underscore a key insight: the introduction of equivalent texture keys yields a form of Pareto improvement, enhancing robustness against scrubbing without sacrificing spoofing resistance, offering a promising pathway toward more practical watermark designs.



