% The vulnerability of the watermark to text perturbations arises from the fact that modifications to a single token can propagate and impact subsequent watermark windows. As $h$ increases, the cascading impact extends over a broader range. Existing studies~\citep{kuditipudi2023robust} have shown that, in theory, watermarking schemes achieve optimal robustness against editing operations when $h=0$ (i.e., with no window dependency). However, such schemes alone offer insufficient resistance against scrubbing attacks, making them impractical for real-world deployment. To address this, we propose distributing the green set construction task across multiple tokens. Each token determines an independent subword table for the green set, and different numbers of tokens are dynamically assigned for protection. This approach enhances the watermark's robustness against scrubbing attacks.

