\textbf{Evaluation metrics.} 
% text quality
We evaluate watermarking methods by assessing both the quality of generated texts and the robustness to both spoofing and scrubbing. To assess the text quality, we employ the perplexity (PPL) using OPT-2.7B~\citep{zhang2022opt} as an oracle, employ the P-SP score \citep{wieting2021paraphrastic} between watermarked and non-watermarked texts, and the log-diversity metric \citep{KWG2,welleck2019neural}, following the common setup~\cite{zhao2023provable,KWG1,KWG2}. 
% scrubbing robustness
For scrubbing robustness, we report AUROC and the true positive rate at false positive rates of $f$ (TP@f). We consider two paraphrase-based scrubbing attacks using prompted ChatGPT-3.5~\citep{gpt} and DeepSeek-V3~\citep{liu2024deepseek}, as well as two configurations of DIPPER~\citep{dipper}, denoted as DIPPER-I $(O = 60, L = 60)$ and DIPPER-II $(O = 20, L = 20)$. 
% spoofing robustness
Regarding spoofing robustness, we use FPR@$f$~\citep{steal} to denote the fraction of attacker’s texts detected as watermarked by a victim's detector calibrated to a false-positive rate of $f$ on non-adversarial texts. 
We evaluate against two types of spoofing: a statistics-based attack~\citep{steal} and a distillation-based attack~\citep{gu2023learnability}. For training spoofing methods, we collected 30,000 watermarked responses, each with a maximum length of 800 tokens. We utilize Qwen2-0.5B-Instruct~\citep{qwen2} as the initial checkpoint for distillation-based attack.
% Specifically, we fine-tune a Qwen2-0.5B-Instruct~\citep{qwen2} model using watermarked queries from the C4-RealNewLike dataset, thereby constructing a surrogate model \( \mathcal{M}' \) that approximates the behavior of the original watermarked model.
% . The victim detector is calibrated on the same subset using over 2,000 labeled watermarked and non-watermarked samples.

\textbf{Baseline and Language Model.} Among $h$-gram watermarking schemes, KGW-Min (i.e. SelfHash~\cite{KWG2}) demonstrates the strongest balance between scrubbing and spoofing robustness, making it our primary baseline. We denote watermarking schemes with a window size of $h$ as \textsc{Scheme}$^h$. 
We set $h=6, d=6$ for SEEK by default. We also implement a version with larger watermark window $h=16, d=16$, denoted as \model$_L$.
For our experiments, we utilize LLaMA2-7B/13B~\citep{llama2}, Mistral-7B~\citep{jiang2023mistral7b} and OPT-6.7B~\citep{zhang2022opt} language models.
