\textbf{Robustness Against Spoofing Attacks.}
Table~\ref{table:spoofing} demonstrates that our method consistently achieves lower FPR@$f$ than all baselines across different settings. 
Notably, KGW-Left$\textsuperscript{1}$ exhibits better robustness than KGW-MIN$\textsuperscript{3}$, even with a smaller watermark window. We attribute this pheonomenon to the higher z-score of self-seeding, leading to a more informative training corpus for the attacker.
To further assess the spoofing robustness of \model, we also adopt the distillation-based spoofing~\citep{gu2023learnability}. Results in Table~\ref{table:spoofing2} consistently show our method's superior robustness across all datasets. Interestingly, we observed a monotonic non-increasing relationship between the watermark window size and resistance to distillation-based spoofing, similar to statistics-based spoofing.


% As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:sparsity}, although KGW-3 provides strong scrubbing robustness, it suffers from high false positive rates under spoofing attacks, reaching nearly 1.00 with just 10{,}000 queries. When the number of spoofed inputs increases to 30{,}000, even KGW-4 exhibits spoofing success rates above 80 percent across all datasets.  As shown in Table~\ref{table:spoofing}, our method consistently achieves lower false positive rates than all KGW variants under spoofing attacks. Compared to KGW-4, our approach reduces FPR@$10^{-3}$ by 88.2\%, 92.3\%, and 82.0\% on the Dolly CW, BookReports, and FakeNews datasets, respectively. This highlights our method’s enhanced robustness against large-scale spoofing. Furthermore, our method exhibits spoofing resistance comparable to KGW-6, and even achieves a lower FPR on the FakeNews dataset. 

\textbf{Watermark text generation quality.} Figure~\ref{fig:quality} compares the generation quality across different datasets. The results demonstrate that our method maintains linguistic fidelity comparable to KGW, with negligible impact on the text quality. 
% These results support the effectiveness of our approach in preserving linguistic characteristics, enabling seamless watermark embedding without introducing noticeable degradation.
% We perform a comprehensive comparison of generation quality between our method and KGW. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:quality}, our method achieves slightly higher P-SP scores on WikiText and LFQA, indicating better semantic preservation. In terms of perplexity, our method demonstrates greater stability and consistently lower values across datasets and model types. These results support the effectiveness of our approach in preserving linguistic characteristics and overall text quality, enabling seamless watermark integration comparable to KGW without introducing noticeable degradation.

\textbf{Visualization.} Table~\ref{tab:examples} visualizes the responses of watermarking schemes to scrubbing and spoofing attacks. The scrubbing attack is performed by ChatGPT-3.5, with the $z$-score in parentheses indicating the decrease caused by the attack, while spoofing robustness is
evaluated using statistics-based attacks. Under scrubbing, KGW results in greater loss of watermark evidence and lower $z$-scores than \model, increasing the risk of false negatives. Under spoofing attacks, adversaries can easily mimic KGW watermark patterns, leading to abnormally high $z$-scores than \model. 


%We provide qualitative examples in Table~\ref{tab:examples} to illustrate how different watermarking schemes respond to scrubbing and spoofing attacks. In the unwatermarked case, the $z$-score remains close to zero, indicating no detectable watermark signal. Under scrubbing attacks, both KGW and our method exhibit decreased $z$-scores due to partial watermark retention. However, KGW shows a larger shift compared to our method, suggesting that KGW's watermark is more easily disrupted, leading to higher variability. In spoofing attacks, KGW-generated texts exhibit a higher frequency of green-list tokens, closely imitating authentic watermark distributions. Consequently, these texts yield elevated $z$-scores, increasing the likelihood of being misclassified as genuine watermarked outputs. This suggests that the watermarking mechanism is more easily subverted under such attacks. In contrast, our approach effectively suppresses such token-level artifacts, leading to lower $z$-scores and enhanced robustness against spoofing attempts.


