\section{Full Related Work}
\label{Full-Related-Work}
\textbf{Machine-human generation detection.} The task of distinguishing between machine-generated and human-written text can be addressed through two primary approaches: post-hoc detection and proactive detection.  Post-hoc detection operates independently of the text generation process and generally leverages a priori knowledge about the statistics of texts generated by a given class of LLMs. For instance, GLTR~\citep{gehrmann2019gltr} estimates the likelihood of individual tokens and applies thresholding techniques to detect AI-generated text. DetectGPT~\citep{Detectgpt} posits that such text typically resides in regions of negative curvature within the log-likelihood landscape. As language models grow more advanced, their outputs closely mimic human writing, making detection increasingly difficult. Existing statistical detectors often require white-box access to know the language model statistics, such as perplexity or curvature~\citep{KWG2}. Despite their flexibility, these methods exhibit limited performance, with error rates empirically validated on limited datasets and rarely falling below $10^{-3}$~\citep{hans2401spotting,giboulot2024watermax}. Proactive detection must be actively deployed by model owners, yet it consistently outperforms passive detection in both effectiveness and reliability. A prominent example is watermarking, which enables the embedding of imperceptible signals into text while preserving its overall quality.

\textbf{LLM Watermarking technology.} Watermarking embeds identifying information within data, but its application to natural language faces challenges due to its discrete and symbolic nature ~\citep{stefan2000information}. Early methods employed rule-based techniques such as syntactic restructuring~\citep{atallah2001natural}, synonym substitution~\citep{topkara2006hiding}, and paraphrasing~\citep{atallah2002natural}. More recent neural network watermarking approaches~\citep{he2022cater,ziegler2019neural, dai2019towards,abdelnabi2021adversarial,he2022protecting} are introduced to develop a black-box watermark that adopts end-to-end learning frameworks during the generation and detection process. However, their limited theoretical guarantees and lack of interpretability hinder broader adoption in practice. Early foundational pioneering work
in LLM watermarking includes KGW~\citep{KWG1,KWG2} and AAR~\citep{arr} place watermarks on a robust mathematical foundation with a low and provable false positive rate. KGW introduces the previous context as the watermark window to modify the next token's distribution,  which significantly influenced numerous follow-up watermark works~\citep{kuditipudi2023robust,mao2024watermark, sweet,zhao2023provable,liu2023unforgeable,lu2024entropy}.
Building on this, watermark detection is later formulated as a hypothesis testing problem, where the alternative hypothesis assumes that the text is drawn from the modified distribution. Subsequent work has extended this line of research by incorporating semantic information~\citep{hou2023semstamp,ren2023robust}, embedding multi-bit information~\citep{CWTL,yoo2023advancing}, and exploring watermarking in low-entropy settings such as code generation~\citep{sweet,EWD}. The UNIGRAM~\citep{kuditipudi2023robust} applies fixed green lists to remove contextual dependency, emphasizing robustness to scrubbing over the comparatively minor threat of spoofing.  This line of research has further evolved to incorporate semantic information~\citep{hou2023semstamp,ren2023robust}, embedding multi-bit information~\citep{CWTL,yoo2023advancing}, and exploring watermarking in low-entropy settings such as code generation~\citep{sweet,EWD}.  Our work focuses on analyzing the security properties and robustness of KGW-based watermarking frameworks


% \textbf{Scrubbing attacks.} Scrubbing attacks fundamentally stem from text editing operations. Early studies~\cite{zhao2023provable,kuditipudi2023robust, KWG1} evaluated the robustness of watermarking schemes using simple perturbations such as random word deletion or substitution. In addition, some work~\citep{KWG2,zhao2023provable} has explored manual paraphrasing by human annotators to assess watermark removal under high-quality rewriting. Another line of work aims to develop black-box scrubbing attacks, like Zhang et al.~\citep{zhang2023watermarks} and Sadasivan et al.~\citep{sadasivan2023can} propose recursive paraphrasing pipelines, while Huang et al.~\citep{huang2024b} formulate the scrubbing task as a constrained optimization problem.  Krishna et al.~\citep{dipper} has proposed a systematic approach to evade AI-generated text detection by training a paraphrasing model, referred to as DIPPER, which has emerged as a standard tool for evaluating the robustness of watermarking techniques.

% \textbf{Spoofing attacks.} Early work~\citep{sadasivan2023can,hou2023semstamp,ghosal2023towards,gu2023learnability} has demonstrated proof-of-concept spoofing against simple $h=1$ watermark schemes~\citep{KWG1}. Gu et al.\citep{gu2023learnability} propose a distillation-based spoofing method, which requires access to watermarked data and incurs considerable training overhead. Zhang et al.\citep{zhang2024large} formalize spoofing as a mixed-integer programming problem; however, their method depends on access to the model's full vocabulary and tokenizer. In contrast, recent work~\citep{steal} demonstrates a highly effective spoofing attack against the KGW-Min watermarking scheme~\citep{KWG2} with $h=4$. Their method leverages statistical token frequency analysis to approximate the watermark generation distribution without any model-specific knowledge, achieving a successful spoofing attack at a cost of less than \$50. This is achieved by learning the statistical probabilities \( p_w(x_n | x_{n-h:n-1}) \) of the watermark and the non-watermark prefix \( p_m(x_n | x_{n-h:n-1}) \), thereby covertly stealing the watermarking scheme. This approach significantly advances the state of spoofing attacks, exposing critical vulnerabilities in watermark schemes previously considered secure.


% \textbf{Scrubbing attacks.} Scrubbing attacks fundamentally stem from text editing operations. Early studies~\cite{zhao2023provable,kuditipudi2023robust, KWG1} evaluated the robustness of watermarking schemes using simple perturbations such as random word deletion or substitution. A more effective strategy involves paraphrasing the text with other LLMs, aiming to preserve semantic content while obfuscating the embedded watermark signal. In addition, some work~\citep{KWG2,zhao2023provable} has explored manual paraphrasing by human annotators to assess watermark removal under high-quality rewriting. Study~\citep{zhang2024large} formalizes the task of stealing the green-list vocabulary as a mixed-integer programming problem. However, the method requires access to the full vocabulary and tokenizer, limiting its applicability in real-world settings. Another line of work aims to develop black-box scrubbing attacks, like \citep{zhang2023watermarks,sadasivan2023can} propose recursive paraphrasing pipelines.  Study~\citep{dipper} has proposed a systematic approach to evade AI-generated text detection by training a paraphrasing model with 11 billion parameters, referred to as DIPPER, which has emerged as a standard tool for evaluating the robustness of watermarking techniques.

%  \textbf{Spoofing attacks.} Research~\citep{sadasivan2023can,hou2023semstamp,ghosal2023towards,gu2023learnability} demonstrated proof-of-concept examples of spoofed text that successfully imitate the $h=1$ watermark scheme, hereafter referred to as LeftHash~\citep{KWG1}. However, these works have yet to present successful attacks targeting the more complex $h>1$ watermark scheme with self-seeding strategy, termed SelfHash~\citep{KWG2}, under realistic conditions characterized by interactive chat-based language models, low false-positive rates (FPR), high-quality generated text, automated adversaries, and minimal query overhead~\citep{ghosal2023towards,liu2024survey,KWG2}.  Recent work~\citep{steal} has significantly advanced the success rates of spoofing attacks and identified vulnerabilities in the SelfHash watermark scheme concerning statistical token frequency attacks, successfully demonstrating effective spoofing attacks. Their work shows that an attacker can infer and statistically approximate the underlying watermarking rules of the SelfHash scheme at a query cost below \$50. This is achieved by learning the statistical probabilities \( P_W(x_n | \{ x_{n-i}|i<h \}) \) of the watermark and the non-watermark prefix \( P_M(x_n | \{ x_{n-i}|i<h \}) \), thereby covertly stealing the green list $G$ used in the watermarking scheme. Their findings indicate that, through their statistical attacks, the anti-spoofing and anti-eraser robustness of text watermarking can be significantly weaker than previously believed.



