Again, the deterministic case is easier because we can exploit the graph representation  of the discounted value functions mentioned above, whereas for the case of non-deterministic SGs we require a unichain assumption.

\section{Results for perfect-information deterministic stochastic games}\label{SectionDeterm}
We start by analyzing the structure of the discounted value functions in deterministic perfect information SGs. A deterministic game can be represented by a weighted digraph: its set of nodes is the set of states, %controlled by player $\Min$ (if we assume that $\Min$ plays first), 
there is an edge from state $i$ to state $j$ if some actions $a$ and $b$ of the players realize this transition,
