\section{A Smith--Thom inequality for orbifold cohomology} \label{sec:smith-thom-orbifold}

Let $\ca X$ be a real smooth Deligne--Mumford stack. In this case the space $\va{\ca X(\RR)}$ can be naturally enriched with the structure of a real analytic orbifold, see \cite[Section 2.2.3]{thesis-degaayfortman}, in analogy with the natural complex analytic orbifold structure on $\va{\ca X(\CC)}$.
It is natural to ask whether the classical Smith–Thom inequality \eqref{align:inequality-ST} admits an analogue for the orbifold cohomology groups  
\[
\mathrm{H}^{i}_{\mathrm{orb}}(\lvert \mathcal{X}(\mathbb{R}) \rvert, \mathbb{Z}/2) 
\quad \text{and} \quad 
\mathrm{H}^{i}_{\mathrm{orb}}(\lvert \mathcal{X}(\mathbb{C}) \rvert, \mathbb{Z}/2)
\]  
of \( \lvert \mathcal{X}(\mathbb{R}) \rvert \) and \( \lvert \mathcal{X}(\mathbb{C}) \rvert \).  
We refer the reader to \cite{MR1816220} and \cite{crainic-moerdijk} for general background on orbifold cohomology.  

For the following discussion, it will be useful to recall the definition of orbifold cohomology for quotient stacks.

\begin{remark}\label{rmq:orbifoldisequivariant}
    If a topological orbifold $\mr X$ is obtained as a quotient of a topological space $X$ by the action of a finite group $\Gamma$, by \cite[Section 1.3]{MR1816220}, one has 
    $$\rm H^{i}_{\rm{orb}}(\mr X ,\ZZ/2)\simeq \rm H^{i}_{\Gamma}(X ,\ZZ/2),$$
    where $\rm H^{i}_{\Gamma}(X ,\ZZ/2)$ is the $\Gamma$-equivariant cohomology of $X$. 
\end{remark}
As already mentioned, in general, the groups $\rm H^{i}_{\rm{orb}}(\vert \ca X(\RR)\vert ,\ZZ/2)$ and $\rm H^{i}_{\rm{orb}}(\vert \ca X(\RR)\vert ,\ZZ/2)$ can be non-zero for infinitely many $i$.
\begin{example}\label{ex:equivariantZ2}
    Let $X\coloneqq\Spec(\mathbb R)$ and $\Gamma\coloneqq\ZZ/2$ viewed as a constant group scheme over $\mathbb R$. 
    By Remark \ref{rmq:orbifoldisequivariant}, one has $$\rm H^i_{\rm{orb}}(\vert[X/\ZZ/2](\mathbb C)\vert,\ZZ/2)\simeq \rm H^i(\Gamma, \ZZ/2) \quad\text{and}\quad \rm H^i_{\rm{orb}}(\vert[X/\ZZ/2](\mathbb R)\vert,\ZZ/2)\simeq \rm H^i(\Gamma, \ZZ/2)\oplus \rm H^i(\Gamma, \ZZ/2).$$
 In particular, they are both non zero every integer $i\geq 0$.
\end{example}
Even if it does not make sense to compare the sum of all the dimensions of all the cohomology groups, one can ask the following vague question. 
\begin{question}\label{que:orbifold}
    Let $\ca X$ be a smooth separated Deligne--Mumford stack over $\RR$. Is there a uniform natural bound on the growth rate of $\rm H^{\leq i}_{\rm{orb}}(\vert \ca X(\RR)\vert,\ZZ/2)$ when $i \to \infty$ in terms of the growth rate of $\rm H^{\leq i}_{\rm{orb}}(\ca X(\CC),\ZZ/2)$, that does not depend on the real model $\ca X$ of $\ca X_\CC$?
\end{question}
In this section we do it for quotient stacks, where the orbifold cohomology can be identified with equivariant cohomology by Remark \ref{rmq:orbifoldisequivariant}.
\subsection{Quillen's theorem on Poincaré series.} In order to do so, we need to recall a result of Quillen on the structure of the Poincaré series of equivariant cohomology. 
Let $\Gamma$ be a finite group and let $X$ be a topological $\Gamma$-space such that $\rm H^\bullet(X, \ZZ/2)$ is a finite dimensional $\FF_2$-vector space. By \cite[Corollary 2.2]{quillen}, the equivariant cohomology ring $\rm H^\ast_\Gamma(X,\ZZ)$ is a finitely generated graded $\bb F_2$-algebra. Let
\[
P_\Gamma(X)(t) \coloneqq \sum_{i = 0}^\infty \dim_{\FF_2}\left( \rm H^i_\Gamma(X, \ZZ/2) \right) \cdot t^i  \in \ZZ[[t]], 
\]
the associated Poincar\'e series. % of the equivariant cohomology of $X$. 
If $X$ is just a point, we write $P_\Gamma(t)\coloneqq P_\Gamma(X)(t)$. 

Recall from \cite[Proposition 2.5, Theorem 7.7]{quillen} the  following theorem. 

\begin{theorem}[Quillen]\label{thm:quillen}  Let $e_\Gamma(X) \coloneqq \max_{n \in \NN}\left( \exists A \subset \Gamma \mid A \cong (\ZZ/2)^n \tn{ and } X^A \neq \emptyset \right). $
 Then there exists a polynomial $Q_\Gamma(X)(t) \in \ZZ[t]$ with $Q_\Gamma(X)(1) \neq 0$ such that 
\[
P_\Gamma(X)(t) = \frac{Q_\Gamma(X)(t)}{\prod_{i  = 1}^{e_\Gamma(X)} \left( 1 - t^{2i} \right)} \in \QQ(t).
%\ZZ[[t]]. 
\]
\end{theorem}
In particular, if a subspace $Y\subset X$ is stable under the action of a subgroup $H\subset \Gamma$, then the rational function 
$$
%P_{H,\Gamma}(Y,X)(t) \coloneqq
%\frac{h^\ast([Y/H])}{h^\ast([X/\Gamma])}\coloneqq 
f(t) = \frac{P_H(Y)(t)}{P_\Gamma(X)(t)} \in \QQ(t)$$ has no pole at $1$. %so that it make sense to evaluate it at $1$. 
Consequently, one obtains a rational number $f(1) \in \QQ$, and $f(1) = 0$ is zero if and only if $e_H(Y) < e_\Gamma(X)$.   
Morally, the rational number $f(1)$ can be thought as the ratio between the total $H$-equivariant Betti number of $Y$ and the total $\Gamma$-equivariant Betti number of $X$. 
%In fact, if $\Gamma$ is the trivial group and $Y = X^\sigma$ for an involution $\sigma \colon X \to X$, then $P_{H,\Gamma}(Y,X)(1)$ is precisely the ratio of the total Betti numbers of $X^\sigma$ and $X$, hence $P_{H,\Gamma}(Y,X)(1)\leq 1$ in that case by \eqref{align:inequality-ST}. 
