\subsection{Frames in Hilbert spaces}\label{sec:Tn_frames}
Frames are a generalization of Schauder bases, as they relax the uniqueness constraint on the basis coefficients $c_i$. For the sake of a simpler notation, we consider in what follows the classical case of an infinite-dimensional, separable Hilbert space $\X$ with inner product $\langle \cdot, \cdot\rangle.$ However, note that finite-dimensional spaces can be handled in a similar way \cite[Chapter 1]{Christensen_Frames}. Furthermore, frames can be generalized to non-separable Hilbert spaces \cite{Bilalov_Frames_nonSeparableSpace} as well as to a Banach space setting \cite[Chapter 24]{Christensen_Frames}.
\begin{definition}[\textbf{Frame}]\label{def:frame}
     A frame of an infinite-dimensional, separable Hilbert space $\X$ is a sequence $(f_i)_{i\in \N}$ in $\X$, if there exist constants $A,B>0$ such that for all $f\in \X$ it holds that 
    \begin{align*}
        A\Vert f \Vert^2 \leq \sum_{i=1}^\infty \vert \langle f, f_i\rangle\vert^2 \leq B \Vert f\Vert ^2.
    \end{align*}
    The values $A$ and $B$ are called frame bounds.
\end{definition}
For a given a frame $(f_i)_{i\in \N}$ in $\X$ with frame bounds $A, B>0$, the so-called \textit{frame operator} is defined as
\begin{align*}
    S: \X &\longrightarrow\X\\
    f &\longmapsto \sum_{i=1}^\infty\langle f, f_i\rangle f_i.
\end{align*}
It is well-known that $S$ is linear, bounded and has a continuous inverse $S^{-1}$. Using the inverse of the frame operator, the \textit{canonical dual} of $(f_i)_{i\in \N}$ is defined as $\big(S^{-1}(f_i)\big)_{i\in \N}$, which is also a frame with frame bounds $B^{-1}, A^{-1}$. The canonical dual leads to the \textit{frame decomposition}, which is one of the major results in classical frame theory and states that every element in $\X$ can be written as an infinite linear combination of the frame elements $f_i.$ More precisely, for a given frame $(f_i)_{i\in \N}$ with corresponding frame operator $S,$ every $f\in \X$ can be expressed as
\begin{equation*}
    f = \sum_{i=1}^\infty \big\langle f,\, S^{-1} (f_i)\big\rangle f_i.
\end{equation*}
As the name of the sequence $\big(S^{-1}(f_i)\big)_{i\in \N}$ suggests, this result can be generalized to so-called \textit{dual frames} of a given frame $(f_i)_{i\in \N}$.
\begin{definition}[\textbf{Dual Frame}]\label{def:dualFrame}
    Let $\X$ be an infinite-dimensional, separable Hilbert space and $(f_i)_{i\in \N} \subset \X$ a given frame of $\X.$ A frame $(f^*_i)_{i\in \N} \subset \X$ of $\X$ is called a dual frame of $(f_i)_{i\in \N},$ if for all $f\in \X$ it holds that
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:def:dualFrame}
        f = \sum_{i=1}^\infty \langle f, f^*_i\rangle f_i.
    \end{equation}
\end{definition}
By the result of the frame decomposition, the canonical dual of a given frame $(f_i)_{i\in \N}$ is also a dual of this frame. Naturally, the question arises under which conditions one can find a frame $(f^*_i)_{i\in \N} \neq \big(S^{-1}(f_i)\big)_{i\in \N}$ of $\X$ such that \eqref{eq:def:dualFrame} holds for all $f\in \X.$ In order to give an answer to this question, we need the notion of a Riesz basis of a Hilbert space $\X.$
\begin{definition}[\textbf{Riesz Basis}]\label{def:RieszBasis}
    Let $\X$ be an infinite-dimensional, separable Hilbert space and $(e_i)_{i\in \N}$ an orthonormal basis of $\X.$ For a given linear, bounded and bijective operator $U:\X\to\X,$ the sequence $\big(U(e_i)\big)_{i\in\N}$ is called a Riesz basis.
\end{definition}
It is well-known, that every Riesz basis of $\X$ is also a Schauder basis as well as a frame of $\X$. Conversely, not every frame is a Riesz basis and frames that do not fulfill the properties of a Riesz basis are typically said to be \textit{overcomplete}. It turns out that this property guarantees the existence of dual frames different from the canonical dual, which we state below and refer to \cite[Lemma 6.3.1]{Christensen_Frames}.
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:dualFrame:Overcomplete}
    Let $\X$ be an infinite-dimensional, separable Hilbert space and $(f_i)_{i\in \N} \subset \X$ a given overcomplete frame of $\X.$ Then there exist frames $(f^*_i)_{i\in \N} \neq \big(S^{-1}(f_i)\big)_{i\in \N}$ such that for all $f\in \X$ it holds that
    \begin{equation*}
        f = \sum_{i=1}^\infty \langle f, f^*_i\rangle f_i.
    \end{equation*}
\end{lemma}
In the following, we state the main result of this section, which will give rise to the construction of encoders and decoders based on frames.
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:frames}
    Let $\X$ be an infinite-dimensional, separable Hilbert space and $(f_i)_{i\in \N} \subset \X$ a given frame of $\X$ along with a dual frame $(f^*_i)_{i\in \N} \subset \X$ of $(f_i)_{i\in \N}.$ Then $\X$ has the $\lambda$-BAP with $T_n$ being the projections
    \begin{align*}
        T_n:\X &\longrightarrow \X\\
        f &\longmapsto \sum_{i=1}^n \langle f, f^*_i \rangle f_i,
    \end{align*}
    which are linear, bounded, have finite rank and converge uniformly to the identity operator on every compact $K\subset \X$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    The statement follows immediately by the definition of the projections $T_n$ and by applying Lemma \ref{lem:BAP_pointwise_convergence_suffices}.
\end{proof}
In particular, we obtain the following result by choosing the dual frame to be the canonical dual of $(f_i)_{i\in \N}$.
\begin{corollary}
    Within the framework of Theorem \ref{thm:frames}: Choosing $(f^*_i)_{i\in \N} \coloneqq \big(S^{-1}(f_i)\big)_{i\in \N}$ leads to the $\lambda$-BAP of $\X$ together with the projections
    \begin{align*}
        T_n:\X &\longrightarrow \X\\
        f &\longmapsto \sum_{i=1}^n \big\langle f,\, S^{-1}(f_i) \big\rangle f_i.
    \end{align*}
\end{corollary}

As in the previous section, the mappings $T_n$ admit a canonical representation of the form $T_n = D_n\circ E_n$ where $D_n$ and $E_n$ are decoders and encoders, respectively. In the following, we refer to $E_n$ as a frame encoder and to $D_n$ as a frame decoder, which are shown in Table \ref{tab:frame_encoder} and satisfy the required conditions in Definition \ref{def:EDAP_normed_space} of the EDAP for $\X$.

