                \mu &\longmapsto \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mu_i v_i^{(n)}\\[2pt]
            \end{aligned}
            $}\\
        }
    }
\end{table}


\section{New universal operator approximation theorem}\label{sec:approx_theorem}
We recall that the overall goal of this work is to approximate any continuous operator $G:\X\to\Y$ between suitable separable metric spaces $\X$ and $\Y$ by a sequence of encoder-decoder architectures $G_n=D_n^\Y\circ \varphi_n \circ E_n^\X:\X\to \Y$ that converges uniformly to $G$ on every compact set $K\subset \X$, see statement \ref{fig:approximation_types:(B)} of Figure \ref{fig:approximation_types}. In this section, we prove in Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed} that this is possible if encoders and decoders from the EDAP are used for the construction of $G_n$, see Definition \ref{def:EDAP_normed_space}.
If only statement \ref{fig:approximation_types:(A)} is desired, so when $G_n$ is allowed to depend on $K$, we further present a corresponding universal approximation theorem at the end of this section. For this weaker type of approximation, the definition of EDAP can be relaxed, which would then also allow for the consideration of non-separable spaces.

Due to the encoding and decoding, the operator approximation task can be reduced to a function approximation task, that is, approximating functions between euclidean spaces $\K^a$ by $\varphi_n$ used in the encoder-decoder architecture $G_n=D_n^\Y\circ \varphi_n \circ E_n$. Therefore, the functions $\varphi_n$ must be chosen from a sufficiently rich set of functions, which leads to the term of universal function approximators. A similar terminology and definition has been considered, for example, within the work of Kratsios et al. \cite[Definition 2.11]{kratsios2023}.

\begin{definition}[\textbf{Universal function approximator}]\label{def:function_approximator}
    A set $\FF$ of continuous functions $\K^a\to \K^b$ is called a \emph{universal function approximator} if for any $a,b\in \N$, compact set $K\subset \K^a$ and continuous function $f:K\to \K^b$  there exists a sequence $(f_n)_{n\in \N}$ in $\FF$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{x\in K} \big \vert f(x) - f_n(x) \big \vert \xrightarrow[]{n\to\infty} 0.
    \end{align*}
    In other words, $\FF\cap \CC(\K^{a}, \K^b)$ is (sequentially) dense in $\CC(\K^a, \K^b)$ with respect to the compact-open topology for all $a,b\in \N$.
\end{definition} 

There are different possible choices for universal function approximators. For example, one can consider different types of neural network architectures such as fully connected or convolutional neural networks \cite{Cybenko1989, Pinkus_1999, Petersen2018}. 
Moreover, polynomials build another type of universal function approximators according to the Stone-Weierstraß theorem \cite[Section XIII.3]{Dugundji_Topologie}.

\begin{remark}
    Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a universal function approximator. Since $\K^a$ is hemicompact, it is due to Theorem \ref{thm:dense_Frechet_hemicompact} that for every $f\in \CC(\K^a, \K^b)$ there is a sequence $(f_n)_{n\in \N}$ in $\mathcal{F}$ that converges uniformly to $f$ on every compact $K\subset \K^a$. In other words, the sequence $(f_n)_{n\in \N}$ can be chosen independently on the compact set $K$ in Definition \ref{def:function_approximator}.
\end{remark}

The following basic lemma will be useful for showing our operator approximation theorem. Similar versions have been derived and used in several other studies for proving universal operator approximation results, see for example \cite{Chen1995_approx_thm, Lu2021_DeepONet, Kovachki2021_approxFNO, Schwab2023, Hua2023}.
We state a general version below which is similar to \cite[Lemma 21]{Kovachki2023}, in which it was shown for Banach spaces $\X$ and $\Y$ using a different proof. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:union_compact_sets}
    Let $\X$ and $(\Y, d_\Y)$ be metric spaces and $K\subset \X$ be compact. Consider a sequence $(G_n)_{n\in \N}$ in $\CC(\X, \Y)$ and some $G\in \CC(\X, \Y)$ such that $G_n$ converges uniformly to $G$ on $K$. Then the following set is also compact:
    \begin{align*}
        V\coloneqq G(K)\cup \left(\bigcup_{n=1}^\infty G_n(K) \right).
    \end{align*}
    \begin{proof}
    Consider a sequence $(g_n)_{n\in \N}$ in $V$. We show that it has a subsequence converging to some $g\in V$. \textbf{Case 1:} If infinitely many $g_n$ belong to 
    \begin{align*}
         V_N \coloneqq G(K) \cup \left(\bigcup_{n=1}^N G_n(K) \right)
    \end{align*}
    for some $N\in \N$, the existence of such a subsequence follows from the compactness of $V_N$. 
    \textbf{Case 2:} Otherwise, assume without loss of generality that $g_n = G_{m(n)}(f_n)$, where $m(n)\in \N$ goes to infinity and $f_n\in K$. By compactness of $K$, there exists a subsequence $(f_{n_k})_{k\in \N}$ converging to some $f\in K$. Therefore, by continuity of $G$ and uniform convergence of $G_n$ to $G$ on $K$, we conclude that 
    \begin{align*}
        d_\Y\big( g_{n_k}  \, ,\, G(f)\big) 
        &\leq  d_\Y\big( G_{m(n_k)}(f_{n_k}) \, ,\, G(f_{n_k})\big) + d_\Y\big( G(f_{n_k})  \, ,\, G(f) \big)  \\
        &\leq \sup_{z\in K} d_\Y\big( G_{m(n_k)}(z) \, ,\, G(z) \big) + d_\Y\big( G(f_{n_k}) \, ,\, G(f) \big) \xrightarrow{k\to\infty} 0.  
    \end{align*}
\end{proof}
\end{lemma}

Some consequence of the previous lemma is the following result, which shows that uniform convergence on every compact set for sequences of operators is preserved under concatenation.

\begin{corollary}\label{coro:sandwich}
    Let $\X,(\Y, d_\Y),(\Z, d_\Z)$ be metric spaces, $G\in\CC(\X, \Y)$ and $H\in \CC(\Y, \Z)$. In addition, let $G_n\in \CC(\X, \Y)$ and $H_n\in \CC(\Y, \Z)$ be sequences converging uniformly on all compact sets to $G$ and $H$, respectively. Then for every compact $K\subset \X$ it holds that
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{f\in K} d_\Z \big( (H\circ G)(f) \, ,\, (H_n\circ G_n) (f) \big) \xrightarrow{n\to\infty} 0.
    \end{align*}
    %Note: The result is also valid for $\X, \Y, \Z, \mathcal{U}$ being metric spaces.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
    Let $K\subset \X$ be compact and $\varepsilon>0$. According to Lemma \ref{lem:union_compact_sets} the set 
    \begin{align*}
        V \coloneqq G(K) \cup \left( \bigcup_{n=1}^\infty G_n(K)\right)
    \end{align*}
    is also compact. Therefore, $H$ is uniformly continuous on $V$, which means that there is some $\delta>0$ such that $d_\Z\big( H (f) , H (g)\big) < \varepsilon / 2$ whenever $f,g\in V$ with $d_\Y\big( f , g \big) < \delta$. Due to uniform convergence of $G_n$ to $G$ on $K$, there exists $N_G\in \N$ such that for all $n\geq N_G$ it holds that
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{f\in K} d_\Y\big( G_n (f) \, ,\, G(f) \big) < \delta.
    \end{align*}
    Therefore, we conclude for those $n\geq N_G$ that
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{f\in K} d_\Z\big( (H\circ G) (f) \, ,\, (H \circ G_n)(f) \big) < \frac{\varepsilon}{2}.
    \end{align*}
    Since $H_n$ converges uniformly to $H$ on the compact set $V$, there exists some $N_H\in \N$ such that for all $n\geq N_H$ it is
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{f\in K} d_\Z\big( (H \circ G_n) (f) \, ,\, (H_n \circ G_n)(f) \big) \leq \sup_{g\in V} d_\Z\big(H (g)\, ,\, H_n (g) \big) < \frac{\varepsilon}{2}.
    \end{align*}
    It follows from the triangular inequality and by choosing $n\geq \max\{N_G, N_H\}$ that
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{f\in K} d_\Z\big( (H \circ G) (f) \, ,\, (H_n\circ G_n)(f) \big) < \varepsilon,
    \end{align*}
    which shows the claim.
\end{proof}


With the preceding considerations, we are now prepared to prove our main result: a universal operator approximation theorem for operators between spaces having the EDAP. It shows that operator approximation is possible by a sequence of encoder-decoder architectures that converge uniformly to the given operator on every compact set, which is statement \ref{fig:approximation_types:(B)} in Figure \ref{fig:approximation_types}. As discussed in Section \ref{sec:approximation_properties}, there are diverse choices for suitable encoders and decoders. As we will point out in Section \ref{sec:examples_architectures}, the theorem is hence applicable, in particular, to many famous architectures used in the field of operator learning, but it is not restricted to neural networks.
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:sequential_density_normed}
    Let $(\X, d_\X)$ and $(\Y,  d_\Y)$ be metric spaces having the EDAP with mappings $D_n^\X,  E_n^\X$ and $D_n^\Y, E_n^\Y$, respectively. 
    Let $\FF$ be a set of universal function approximators. Then for every $G\in \CC(\X, \Y)$ there exists a sequence $\varphi_n\in \FF$ such that for every compact $K\subset \X$ it holds that
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{f\in K} d_\Y\Big( G(f) \,,\, \left(D_n^\Y \circ \varphi_n \circ E_n^\X \right)(f) \Big) \xrightarrow{n\to\infty}0.
    \end{align*}
    In other words, the encoder-decoder architectures $D_n^\Y \circ \varphi_n \circ E_n^\X$ converge to $G$ in the compact-open topology. In particular, the set $\{ D_n^\Y \circ \varphi \circ E_n^\X : \varphi \in \FF \cap \CC(\K^{w_\X(n)}, \K^{w_\Y(n)}), \ n\in \N \}$ is sequentially dense in $\CC(\X,\Y)$ with respect to the compact-open topology.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    Consider some $G\in \CC(\X, \Y)$. For every $n\in \N$ define closed balls 
    \begin{align*}
        B_n \coloneqq \overline{B}_{r(n)}(0) \subset \K^{w_\X(n)}
    \end{align*}
    around $0\in \K^{w_\X(n)}$ with radius 
    \begin{align*}
        r(n)\coloneqq \big\vert E_n^\X (f_0) \big\vert + n L_n^\X,
    \end{align*} 
    where $L_n^\X$ is a Lipschitz constant of $E_n^\X$ at $f_0$, where $f_0$ is chosen as in Definition \ref{def:EDAP_normed_space}. Since $B_n$ is closed and bounded, it is compact in the finite dimensional space $\K^{w_\X(n)}$. Therefore, and by Definition \ref{def:function_approximator} of universal function approximators, for all $n\in \N$ there exists $\varphi_n: \K^{w_\X(n)} \to \K^{w_\Y(n)}$ in $\FF$ that satisfies
    \begin{align}\label{thm:sequential_density_normed_eq11}
        \sup_{x\in B_n} \Big \vert \left(E_n^\Y \circ G \circ D_n^\X\right)(x) - \varphi_n(x) \Big \vert \leq \frac{1}{n L^\Y_n},
    \end{align}
    where $L^\Y_n$ is a Lipschitz constant of $D_n^\Y$.

    Let $K\subset \X$ be compact, so in particular bounded. Therefore, there exists some $N_K\in \N$ such that for all $n\geq N_K$ it is $d_\X( f,f_0) \leq n$ for all $f\in K$. As a consequence,
    \begin{align*}
        \left \vert E_n^\X (f) \right \vert &\leq \left\vert E_n^\X (f_0) \right\vert + \left\vert E_n^\X (f) - E_n^\X (f_0) \right\vert \\
        &\leq \left\vert E_n^\X (f_0) \right\vert +  L_n^\X d_\X( f,f_0) \\
        &\leq r(n).
    \end{align*}
    This implies that for all $f\in K$ and all $n\geq N_K$ it is
    \begin{align}\label{thm:sequential_density_normed_eq22}
        E_n^\X (f) \in B_n.
    \end{align}
    Recalling the Lipschitz constant $L^\Y_n$ of $D_n^\Y$, the preceding observations allow us to make for $n\geq N_K$ the estimates
    \begin{align*}
        &\sup_{f\in K} d_\Y \Big( \left(D_n^\Y \circ E_n^\Y \circ G \circ D_n^\X \circ E_n^\X \right) (f)\, , \, \left(D_n^\Y \circ \varphi_n \circ E_n^\X \right)(f) \Big)  \\
        \leq &L^\Y_n \cdot  \sup_{f\in K} \Big \vert \left(E_n^\Y \circ G \circ D_n^\X \circ E_n^\X \right) (f) - \left(\varphi_n \circ E_n^\X\right)(f) \Big \vert\\
        \overset{(\ref{thm:sequential_density_normed_eq22})}{\leq} & L^\Y_n \cdot  \sup_{x\in B_n} \Big \vert \left(E_n^\Y \circ G \circ D_n^\X\right) (x) - \varphi_n(x) \Big \vert \\
        \overset{(\ref{thm:sequential_density_normed_eq11})}{\leq} &  L^\Y_n \cdot  \frac{1}{n L^\Y_n} = \frac{1}{n} \xrightarrow{n\to\infty}0.
    \end{align*}
    The claim then follows from the Definition \ref{def:EDAP_normed_space} of the EDAP, multiple applications of Corollary \ref{coro:sandwich} and the observation
    \begin{align*}
        &\phantom{+}\sup_{f\in K} d_\Y\Big( G(f) \, , \, \left(D_n^\Y \circ \varphi_n \circ E_n^\X \right)(f) \Big) \\
        \leq &\phantom{+}\sup_{f\in K} d_\Y\Big( G(f) \, , \, \left(D_n^\Y \circ E_n^\Y \circ G \circ D_n^\X \circ E_n^\X \right) (f) \Big) \\&+\sup_{f\in K} d_\Y\Big( \left(D_n^\Y \circ E_n^\Y \circ G \circ D_n^\X \circ E_n^\X \right) (f) \,,\, \left(D_n^\Y \circ \varphi_n \circ E_n^\X \right)(f) \Big).
    \end{align*}
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
    Having a closer look on the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed}, it may be noticed that for the domain space $\X$, only Lipschitz continuity of the encoders $E_n^\X$ is used, but not of the corresponding decoders $D_n^\X$. Conversely, for the range space $\Y$, only the decoders' Lipschitz continuity is required. Therefore, the EDAP (see Definition \ref{def:EDAP_normed_space}) can be relaxed for $\X$ and $\Y$ accordingly. However, for the sake of readability, we have chosen not to introduce different types of EDAP-properties for $\X$ and $\Y$.
\end{remark}

In contrast to most results within the operator learning literature, the choice of the approximating sequence $G_n$ in Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed} is independent of the compact set $K\subset \X$. As pointed out in Theorem \ref{thm:dense_Frechet_hemicompact}, the previous result is hence a stronger result for most spaces $\X$ relevant for operator approximation, for example, whenever $\X$ is an infinite-dimensional normed space.
Nevertheless, in \cite{Schwab2023}, an analogous result has been achieved for separable Hilbert spaces $\X, \Y$ and encoder-decoder architectures based on Riesz-bases, see also Section \ref{sec:examples_frame} for more information. To the best of our knowledge, \cite{Schwab2023} is the only other study showing uniform convergence of encoder-decoder architectures $G_n$ towards $G$ on every compact set $K$. For other operator learning architectures, such as FNOs or WNOs, we have not found any result similar to the theorem above. However, we highly suspect that also for those approaches, it would be possible to derive such a result, which we leave as an interesting future task.

The operators $G$ in Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed} were required to be defined on the whole space $\X$. However, what if $G$ is only defined on a subset $A\subseteq \X$? 
In Equation (\ref{thm:sequential_density_normed_eq11}) in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed}, it was crucial that the decoder $D_n^\X$ maps into the domain of the operator $G$. 
Therefore, one cannot use, in general, the same encoders and decoders for $A$ to obtain a similar result for operators $G\in \CC(A, \Y)$. Further, it is not automatically clear whether the subset $A$ itself has the EDAP. We suspect that this may not be true in general, because of the following analogous fact about the $\lambda$-BAP: There exists a separable Banach space $\X$ having the $\lambda$-BAP, but there is a subspace $A\subset \X$, which has not the $\lambda'$-BAP for any $\lambda'>0$, see for example \cite[Corollary 1.13]{Figiel2011}.
Of course, if also $A$ has the EDAP, then Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed} is applicable with suitable encoders and decoders on $A$.
Nevertheless, for closed $A\subset \X$, one can indeed use the encoders and decoders from the EDAP of $\X$ to approximate operators in $\CC(A, \Y)$, see Corollary \ref{coro:main_dugundji_extension} below. This is due to Dugundji's extension theorem \cite[Theorem 4.1]{Dugundji_Extension}.

\begin{theorem}[\textbf{Dugundji's extension theorem}]
    Let $\X, \Y$ be metric spaces, $A\subseteq \X$ closed and $f:A \to \Y$ be continuous. Then there exists a continuous extension $F:\X\to \Y$ of $f$, which means that $F(x) = f(x)$ for every $x\in A$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}\label{coro:main_dugundji_extension}
    Consider the setup as in Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed}. Let $A\subseteq \X$ be closed. For every $G\in \CC(A, \Y)$ there exists a sequence $\varphi_n\in \FF$ such that for every compact $K\subset \X$ it holds that
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{f\in K} d_\Y \Big( G(f) \, ,\, \left(D_n^\Y \circ \varphi_n \circ E_n^\X \right)(f) \Big) \xrightarrow{n\to\infty}0.
    \end{align*}
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
    According to Dugundji's extension theorem, the operator $G$ can be extended to a continuous operator $\X\to\Y$. Therefore, the claim follows from applying Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed} to the extension of $G$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Approximation depending on compact sets}\label{sec:CEDAP}
In Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed}, the EDAP of the input and output space has been used for constructing a sequence of encoder-decoder architectures that converges to a given operator uniformly on every compact set (statement \ref{fig:approximation_types:(B)} in Figure \ref{fig:approximation_types}). If instead the weaker statement \ref{fig:approximation_types:(A)} is desired, one can derive an analogous approximation theorem, see Theorem \ref{thm:density_metric} below. For this, it is sufficient to consider the following weaker version of the EDAP (recall Definition \ref{def:EDAP_normed_space} of the EDAP). 

\begin{definition}[\textbf{Compact EDAP of metric spaces}]\label{def:CEDAP_metric_space}
    A metric space $(\X, d)$ is said to have the compact EDAP (CEDAP) if for every compact $K\subset \X$, there are mappings $E_{K, n}^\X:\X\to \K^{w_\X(K, n)}$ and $D_{K, n}^\X:\K^{w_\X(K, n)}\to \X$ with the following properties:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[(i)] $E_{K, n}^\X$ is continuous and there is $f_0\in \X$ at which all $E_{K, n}^\X$ are Lipschitz continuous.
        \item[(ii)] $D_{K, n}^\X$ is Lipschitz continuous.
        \item[(iii)] The mappings $T_{K, n}^\X \coloneqq D_{K, n}^\X \circ E_{K, n}^\X$ satisfy
        \begin{align*}
            \sup_{f\in K} d(f\, , \, T_{K, n}^\X f) \xrightarrow{n\to\infty}0.
        \end{align*}
    \end{itemize}
\end{definition}
Evidently, if a metric space $\X$ has the EDAP, it has also the CEDAP.  Further, note that even the AP (Defintion \ref{def:AP}) implies the CEDAP. Therefore, also non-separable spaces can have the CEDAP, whereas this is impossible for the EDAP. The following result generalizes \cite[Lemma 22]{Kovachki2023}, in which Banach spaces $\X$ and $\Y$ having both the AP were considered. The theorem below covers several existing universal approximation theorems of encoder-decoder networks, for example, for classical DeepONets \cite[Theorem 5]{Chen1995_approx_thm} and  \cite[Theorem 2]{Lu2021_DeepONet}, MIONets \cite[Corollary 2.6 (ii)]{Jin2022_MIONET} and BasisONets \cite[Theorem 2.2]{Hua2023}, see Section \ref{sec:examples_architectures}.
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:density_metric}
    Let $\X$ and $(\Y, d_\Y)$ be metric spaces having the CEDAP with mappings $D_{K, n}^\X,  E_{K, n}^\X$ and $D_{K, n}^\Y, E_{K, n}^\Y$, respectively. 
    Let $\FF$ be a set of universal function approximators. Then for every $G\in \CC(\X, \Y)$ and every compact $K\subset \X$, there exists a sequence $\varphi_{K, n}\in \FF$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{f\in K} d_\Y\Big(Gf \, , \, \left(D_{K, n}^\Y \circ \varphi_{K, n} \circ E_{K, n}^\X \right)f\Big) \xrightarrow{n\to\infty}0.
    \end{align*}
    In particular, the set $\{ D_{K, n}^\Y \circ \varphi \circ E_{K, n}^\X : \varphi \in \FF \cap \CC(\K^{w_\X(K, n)}, \K^{w_\Y(K,n)}), \ n\in \N \}$ is dense in $\CC(\X,\Y)$ with respect to the compact-open topology.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    The proof is analogous to the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed}.
\end{proof}

\section{Examples of famous encoder-decoder architectures}\label{sec:examples_architectures}
    As shown in Section \ref{sec:approximation_properties}, there are diverse choices for encoders and decoders in spaces having the EDAP that can all be used in the universal operator approximation Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed}. 
    In this section, we discuss different corresponding encoder-decoder architectures, which cover several famous architectures used in operator learning, such as classical DeepONets \cite{Chen1995_approx_thm, Lu2021_DeepONet}, architectures based on frames or Riesz bases \cite{Schwab2023}, BasisONets \cite{Hua2023} or special cases of MIONets \cite{Jin2022_MIONET}.
    Within the literature, individual approximation results are derived for these architectures. Thus, considering more general architectures, as in Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed}, paves the way for a more unified theoretical framework.


    \subsection{Classical DeepONets}\label{sec:DeepONet_classic}
    As a first example for approximating operators $G:\X\to \Y$, we consider classical DeepONets, which have been presented in \cite{Chen1995_approx_thm, Lu2021_DeepONet, Lanthaler2022}. However, we use a more general definition regarding the range space $\Y$.
    \begin{definition}[\textbf{Classical DeepONets}]\label{def:DeepONets} 
        Let $\Omega_1$ be a compact metric space and $\Y$ be a normed space. Consider sampling points $\{y_1,\dots,y_k\}$ in $\Omega_1$. A classical DeepONet is a mapping
        \begin{align*}
            \CC(\Omega_1, \K^a) &\longrightarrow \Y\\
            f &\longmapsto \sum_{i=1}^p \varphi_{i}\big(f(y_1),\dots,f(y_k)\big) v_i,
        \end{align*}
        where $\varphi_{i}\in \CC(\K^{k\times a}, \K)$ and $v_i\in \Y$.
    \end{definition}


    In \cite{Chen1995_approx_thm, Lu2021_DeepONet}, the range space $\Y=\CC(\Omega_2, \R)$ for compact $\Omega_2 \subset \R^d$ was considered, whereas in \cite{Lanthaler2022} also $\Y=L^2(\Omega_2, \R)$ was studied.
    Note that in the typical definition of DeepONets, $v_i$ and $\varphi_{i}$ are neural networks and called trunk and branch networks, respectively. However, as neural networks are not the only existing universal function approximators, the derived theory in Section \ref{sec:approx_theorem} is also applicable to a more general definition of DeepONets.
    By defining $\varphi \coloneqq \left( \varphi_{1},\dots, \varphi_{p} \right)^\intercal \in \CC(\K^{k\times a}, \K^p),$
    we observe the well-known fact that classical DeepONets are encoder-decoder architectures $D\circ \varphi \circ E$ according to Definition \ref{def:encoderDecoder_Architecture} with an encoder $E$ and a decoder $D$:
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{aligned}
            E: \CC(\Omega_1, \K^a) &\longrightarrow \K^{k\times a} \qquad\qquad&D: \K^p &\longrightarrow \Y\\
            f &\longmapsto \big(f(y_1), \dots, f(y_k)\big)^\intercal \qquad\qquad&\mu &\longmapsto \sum_{i=1}^p \mu_i v_i.
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation*}
    If the sampling encoders and dense decoders from Table \ref{tab:sampling_encoder} and \ref{tab:substitution_encoder} are used in encoder-decoder architectures, this results in classical DeepONets according to Definition \ref{def:DeepONets}.  
    If $\Y$ is a suitable space of functions $\Omega\subset \K^d \to \K^b$, such as Lebesgue spaces, Sobolev spaces or spaces of continuously differentiable functions, there are dense sets $S\subset \Y$ for the dense decoders consisting of, for example, neural networks \cite{Pinkus_1999, DeVore2021relu_overview}, polynomials \cite[Section XIII.3]{Dugundji_Topologie}, splines \cite{Birman_1967, Anderson2014}, or wavelets \cite{Meyer_1993}. Therefore, Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed} immediately leads to a universal approximation theorem for classical DeepONets.

    \begin{corollary}\label{coro:classical_DeepONets}
        Consider sampling encoders $E_n^\X$ for $\X \coloneqq \CC(\Omega_1, \K^a)$ from Table \ref{tab:sampling_encoder}. Further, for a dense set $S\subseteq \Y$ of a separable, normed space $\Y$ having the $\lambda$-BAP, let $D_n^\Y$ be dense decoders from Table \ref{tab:substitution_encoder}.
        Let $\FF$ be a set of universal function approximators. Then for every $G\in \CC(\X, \Y)$ there exists $\varphi^{(n)}\in \FF$ such that for every compact $K\subset \X$ the classical DeepONets $G_n = D_n^\Y \circ \varphi^{(n)} \circ E_n^\X$ satisfy
        \begin{align*}
            \sup_{f\in K} \Big \Vert Gf - G_n f \Big \Vert \xrightarrow{n\to\infty}0.
        \end{align*}
    \end{corollary}
    It is to be stressed again that the choice of $G_n$ is independent on the compact set $K$, which is statement \ref{fig:approximation_types:(B)} in Figure \ref{fig:approximation_types}. This is in contrast to the results in \cite{Chen1995_approx_thm, Lu2021_DeepONet}, in which the weaker statement \ref{fig:approximation_types:(A)} was shown. 
    \begin{remark}
        If $\Y$ is a Banach space that has a Schauder basis, one can also choose a Schauder basis $(v_n)_{n\in \N}\subset S$ of $\Y$ from any dense set $S\subset \Y$. This is because Schauder bases are stable against small perturbations, see for example \cite[Proposition I.1.7]{Lindenstrauß_classical_banach_spaces}.
        Therefore, instead of using the dense decoder $D_n^\Y$ in Corollary \ref{coro:classical_DeepONets}, one could also use the basis decoder from Section \ref{sec:Tn_schauder} corresponding to $(v_n)_{n\in \N}\subset S$ and obtain a similar approximation result.
    \end{remark}

    \subsection{Architectures with Schauder Bases}
    Using a sampling encoder, as for classical DeepONets, may not be possible in every function space. For example, if the input space is a Lebesgue space $\X = L^2(\Omega)$, not every $f\in \X$ has a continuous representative, which makes sampling not well-defined. As a way out, one could use an encoder corresponding to a Schauder basis, which is described below. In Hilbert spaces, such as $L^2(\Omega)$, one can also make use of frame encoders, which will be outlined in Section \ref{sec:examples_frame}.
    Analogously to classical DeepONets, we define Schauder basis DeepONets, which receive Schauder basis coefficients instead of sampling points as an input.

    \begin{definition}[\textbf{Schauder basis DeepONet}]\label{def:Schauder_basis_DeepONet}
        Let $\X$ be a Banach space having a Schauder basis and $\Y$ be a normed space. The coefficient functionals with respect to the basis are denoted by $c_i:\X \to \K$. A Schauder basis DeepONet is a mapping
        \begin{align*}
            \X &\longrightarrow \Y\\
            f &\longmapsto \sum_{i=1}^p \varphi_{i}\big(c_1(f),\dots, c_k(f)\big) v_i,
        \end{align*}
        where $\varphi_{i}\in \CC(\K^k, \K)$ and $v_i\in \Y$.
    \end{definition}
    A similar architecture has been presented in  \cite[Section 3.1]{Jin2022_MIONET} for the case $\Y= \CC(\Omega, \R)$ and for $\varphi_{i}$, $v_i$ being neural networks. They considered the case that $\X$ is a finite cartesian product of Banach spaces $\X_1,...,\X_n$, where the latter all possess Schauder bases. It is easy to see that also $\X$ must then have a Schauder basis, so that the above Schauder basis DeepONets can be considered in Corollary \ref{coro:basis_DeepONets} below. Nevertheless, if more than one $\X_i$ is considered, the Multi-Input Operator Network (MIONet) architectures in \cite{Jin2022_MIONET} are different from Definition \ref{def:Schauder_basis_DeepONet}.

    Every Schauder basis DeepONet is an encoder-decoder architecture $D\circ \varphi \circ E$ with $\varphi \coloneqq \left( \varphi_{1},\dots, \varphi_{p} \right)^\intercal \in \CC(\K^{k}, \K^p)$ as well as the mappings 
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{aligned}
            E: \X &\longrightarrow \K^k, \qquad\qquad&D: \K^p &\longrightarrow \Y,\\
            f &\longmapsto \left( c_1(f),...,c_k(f) \right)^\intercal, \qquad\qquad& \mu &\longmapsto \sum_{i=1}^p \mu_i v_i.
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation*}
    Therefore, Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed} leads to the following result for Schauder basis DeepONets.
    \begin{corollary}\label{coro:basis_DeepONets}
        Let $\X$ be a Banach space having a Schauder basis. Consider corresponding basis encoders $E_n^\X$ of $\X$ from Table \ref{tab:basis_encoder}. Further, for a dense set $S\subseteq \Y$ of a separable, normed space $\Y$ having the $\lambda$-BAP, let $D_n^\Y$ be dense decoders from Table \ref{tab:substitution_encoder}.
        Let $\FF$ be a set of universal function approximators. Then for every $G\in \CC(\X, \Y)$ there exists $\varphi^{(n)}\in \FF$ such that for every compact $K\subset \X$ the Schauder basis DeepONets $G_n = D_n^\Y \circ \varphi^{(n)} \circ E_n^\X$ satisfy
        \begin{align*}
            \sup_{f\in K} \Big \Vert Gf - G_n f \Big \Vert \xrightarrow{n\to\infty}0.
        \end{align*}
    \end{corollary}
    If $\Y$ does also have a Schauder basis, it is possible to consider a similar architecture as in Definition \ref{def:Schauder_basis_DeepONet}, where the $v_i$ are replaced by a Schauder basis $b_i^\Y.$ Then, by choosing a Schauder basis decoder for $\Y$ in Corollary \ref{coro:basis_DeepONets}, we obtain a similar convergence result by Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed}. A special case of these Schauder basis DeepONet architectures was considered in \cite{Castro2023_ONBs}, called Deep-H-ONets, where the authors investigate encoder-decoder architectures between two separable Hilbert spaces and use basis encoders and decoders corresponding to orthonormal bases.


    \subsection{Frame Architectures}\label{sec:examples_frame}
        In this section, we make use of the frame encoders and decoders described in Section \ref{sec:Tn_frames} and introduce in the following encoder-decoder architectures based on frames.
        \begin{definition}[\textbf{Frame Architecture}]\label{def:frame_architectures}
            Let $\X$ and $\Y$ be infinite-dimensional, separable Hilbert spaces, $(f_i)_{i\in \N} \subset \X$ a given frame of $\X$ along with a dual frame $(f_i^*)_{i\in\N}\subset \X$ of $(f_i)_{i\in \N}$ as well as $(g_i)_{i\in \N}\subset \Y$ a given frame of $\Y.$ 
            For $\varphi_{i} \in \CC(\K^k, \K),$ a frame architecture is a mapping
            \begin{align*}
                \X &\longrightarrow \Y\\
                f &\longmapsto \sum_{i=1}^p \varphi_{i} \left( \langle f, f^*_1 \rangle, \dots, \langle f, f^*_k \rangle \right) g_i.
            \end{align*}
        \end{definition}
        By considering the frame encoders and decoders from Section \ref{sec:Tn_frames} given by
        \begin{equation*}
            \begin{aligned}
                E: \X &\longrightarrow \K^k, \qquad\qquad&D: \K^p &\longrightarrow \Y,\\
                f &\longmapsto \big( \langle f, f^*_1 \rangle, \dots, \langle f, f^*_k \rangle \big)^\intercal, \qquad\qquad&\mu &\longmapsto \sum_{i=1}^p \mu_i g_i,
            \end{aligned}
        \end{equation*}
        and defining $\varphi \coloneqq (\varphi_{1}, \dots, \varphi_{p})^\intercal \in \CC(\K^k, \K^p),$ we can observe that frame architectures are encoder-decoder architectures $D\circ \varphi \circ E: \X \longrightarrow \Y.$ Hence, Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed} can be applied and leads to the following universal approximation theorem for these architectures.
        \begin{corollary}\label{coro:frameArchitecture_UOAP}
            Let $\X$ and $\Y$ be infinite-dimensional, separable Hilbert spaces. Further, consider frame encoders $E_n^\X$ and decoders $D_n^\Y$ from Table \ref{tab:frame_encoder} as well as a set of universal function approximators $\FF.$ Then for every $G\in \CC(\X, \Y)$ there exists $\varphi^{(n)}\in \FF$ such that for every compact $K\subset \X$ the frame architectures $G_n = D_n^\Y \circ \varphi^{(n)} \circ E_n^\X$ satisfy
            \begin{align*}
                \sup_{f\in K} \Big \Vert Gf - G_n f \Big \Vert \xrightarrow{n\to\infty}0.
            \end{align*}
        \end{corollary}
        Note that \cite{Schwab2023} considered encoder-decoder architectures based on Riesz bases, which are a special case of the frame architectures described in Definition \ref{def:frame_architectures}. Moreover, \cite{Schwab2023} obtained an analogue convergence result as in Corollary \ref{coro:frameArchitecture_UOAP} by constructing the sequence of Riesz architectures $G_n$ independently of the compact set $K\subset \X$.

    \subsection{BasisONets}\label{sec:basisONet}
    For classical and Schauder basis DeepONets, dense decoders were used for the range space $\Y$ to obtain the approximation results in Corollaries \ref{coro:classical_DeepONets} and \ref{coro:basis_DeepONets}.
    As a last but not least example, we consider the case that, additionally, for the domain space $\X$, dense encoders are used. We will see that this covers the BasisONet architectures presented in \cite{Hua2023}, which we recap in what follows. In \cite{Hua2023}, Lebesgue spaces $\X = L^2(U)$ and $\Y = L^2(V)$ were considered for compact, measurable subsets $U$ and $V$ of Euclidean spaces. It is well-known that there exist sets of neural networks $S_\X$ and $S_\Y$ that are dense in $\X$ and $\Y$, see for example \cite{DeVore2021relu_overview} for ReLU-networks. Making use of this fact, they showed in \cite[Theorem 2.2]{Hua2023} that for every operator $G\in \CC(\X, \Y)$ and every compact $K\subset \X$, there is a sequence of BasisONets, defined below, converging uniformly to $G$ on $K$.
    \begin{definition}[\textbf{BasisONet}]
        Consider Lebesgue spaces $\X=L^2(U)$ and $\Y=L^2(V)$ as well as sets $S_\X\subset\X$ and $S_\Y\subset \Y$ of neural networks. A BasisONet is a mapping
        \begin{align*}
            \X &\longrightarrow \Y\\
            f &\longmapsto \sum_{i=1}^p \varphi_{i}\big(\langle f, u_1 \rangle_\X, ..., \langle f, u_k\rangle_\X \big) v_i,
        \end{align*}
        where $\varphi_{i}\in \CC(\R^k, \R)$, $v_i\in S_\Y$ and $u_1,...,u_k\in S_\X$ are neural networks.
    \end{definition}
    Note that in \cite{Hua2023}, BasisONets are defined with $u_j$ and $v_i$ being so-called neural bases. That is, they approximate finitely many elements of an orthonormal basis. This is analogous to the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:Tn_substitute_dense_set_HilbertSpace}, in which dense enoders and decoders suitable for the EDAP of separable Hilbert spaces were constructed, see Table \ref{tab:dense_encoder}.
    Therefore, it follows again from Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed} that a sequence of BasisONets can be found that approximates a given operator $G\in \CC(\X, \Y)$ on every compact set $K\subset \X$. More generally, not only for neural networks, but for architectures with dense encoders and dense decoders, we have the following result.


    \begin{corollary}
        Let $\X$ be a separable Hilbert space and $\Y$ be a separable normed space having the $\lambda$-BAP. For dense sets $S_\X\subset \X$ and $S_\Y\subset \Y$ consider dense encoders $E_n^\X$ and decoders $D_n^\Y$ from Table \ref{tab:dense_encoder}. Further, let $\FF$ be a set of universal function approximators. Then for every $G\in \CC(\X, \Y)$ there exists $\varphi^{(n)}\in \FF$ such that for every compact $K\subset \X$ the encoder-decoder architectures $G_n = D_n^\Y \circ \varphi^{(n)} \circ E_n^\X$ satisfy
        \begin{align*}
            \sup_{f\in K} \Big \Vert Gf - G_n f \Big \Vert \xrightarrow{n\to\infty}0.
        \end{align*}
    \end{corollary}

\section{Conclusion}
In this study, we derived a new universal operator approximation theorem (Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed}) for continuous operators $G\in \CC(\X, \Y)$ between separable normed or metric spaces $\X$ and $\Y$, in the topology of uniform convergence on compacta (equivalently, the compact-open topology). As outlined in Section \ref{sec:examples_architectures}, this result applies to various encoder-decoder architectures, including DeepONets \cite{Chen1995_approx_thm, Lu2021_DeepONet, Lanthaler2022}, special cases of MIONets \cite{Jin2022_MIONET}, architectures based on frames or Riesz-bases \cite{Schwab2023}, BasisONets \cite{Hua2023}, and Deep-H-ONets \cite{Castro2023_ONBs}.
Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed} ensures that for every operator $G\in \CC(\X, \Y)$, there is a sequence $G_n$ of such encoder-decoder architectures that converges uniformly to $G$ on every compact set $K\subset \X$ (statement \ref{fig:approximation_types:(B)} in Figure \ref{fig:approximation_types}). This is in contrast to most existing results, in which $G_n$ typically depends on the compact set $K$ (statement \ref{fig:approximation_types:(A)} in Figure \ref{fig:approximation_types}). 

Moreover, we analyzed in detail the naturally arising question for which spaces $\X$ and $\Y$ the statement \ref{fig:approximation_types:(B)} is a stronger statement compared to \ref{fig:approximation_types:(A)}. Theorem \ref{thm:dense_Frechet_hemicompact} reveals that in most operator approximation settings, where $\X$ is typically an infinite-dimensional normed space, \ref{fig:approximation_types:(B)} is indeed a stronger universal operator approximation result. 

In Definition \ref{def:EDAP_normed_space}, we introduced the encoder-decoder approximation property (EDAP) of metric spaces, which characterizes how the respective identity operator can be approximated using encoder and decoder mappings. If the domain and range spaces $\X$ and $\Y$ have the EDAP, this guarantees that operator approximation as in statement \ref{fig:approximation_types:(B)} is possible in Theorem \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed}. For instance, all separable normed spaces having the well-known bounded approximation property also have the EDAP. This includes Lebesgue spaces (except $L^\infty$), Sobolev spaces or spaces of continuously differentiable functions.
Moreover, our results apply to non-linear encoders and decoders and do not require the spaces $\X$ and $\Y$ to be complete, which are common assumptions in prior studies.

Furthermore, we introduced a weaker version of the EDAP, namely the compact encoder-decoder approximation property (CEDAP). This property similarly led to a universal operator approximation theorem, Theorem \ref{thm:density_metric}, but as in statement \ref{fig:approximation_types:(A)}, where the approximating sequence of encoder-decoder architectures depends on the compact set. 
In other words, if the identity operators of the domain and range spaces $\X$ and $\Y$ can be approximated in the EDAP or CEDAP sense, then the corresponding encoder-decoder architectures in Theorems \ref{thm:sequential_density_normed} and \ref{thm:density_metric} inherit this type of approximation for the approximation of continuous operators $G\in \CC(\X, \Y)$. This suggests an interesting future direction: investigating whether other variants of the encoder-decoder approximation property can be defined that allows for operator approximation, for example, in Bochner spaces $L^p(\X, \Y; \mu)$.
Finally, if EDAP or CEDAP are modified to incorporate specific convergence rates for approximating the identity operator, it would be interesting to explore whether similar convergence rates can be inherited for approximating operators that satisfy additional regularity conditions, such as Lipschitz continuity or Fr\'echet differentiability. 


