\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Expanding this equation at leading order around $x=0$, we find:
\begin{equation}
(9-40g_{1,2}'(0)-8\tilde{g}_{1,2}'(0))x^{3}+\mathcal{O}(x^{4})\implies \tilde{g}_{1,2}'(0)= \frac{9}{8}-5g_{1,2}'(0),
\end{equation}
which gives us another relation between Taylor coefficients. Using this relation and solving for $g_{2,1}(x)$, we obtain:
\begin{equation}
    g_{2,1}(x)=\frac{
\left(2 - x^2\right) \sqrt{1 + 4x^2} - \left(2 + 3x^2\right)}{12x^3} \quad \implies \quad \tilde{f}_{2,1}(x)=\frac{3}{2}\frac{x^{2}}{1+4x^{2}},
\end{equation}
where now both integration constants of $g_{2,1}$ are fixed by enforcing its vanishing at zero. Then, the next equation for $m_{1}=1$ reads:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&3 
+ x^2 \bigg(
10 + x \big(
3 - 10x + 24x^2 - 80x^3 + 48x^4 
- 12(1 + 4x^2)^2 g_{1,2}'(0)
\big)
