In sum, we draw the following conclusions about efficiency gains from mfBm based on the analytical and simulation results:
\begin{enumerate}
\item If all Hurst exponents are identical, the forecasts based on fBm and mfBm coincide. Hence, there is no efficiency gain.
\item If all correlations are zero, i.e., $\Sigma$ is diagonal, the forecasts based on fBm and mfBm coincide. Hence, there is no efficiency gain. 
\item In a scenario that is not covered by the previous two cases, the forecast efficiency improves compared to the univariate model. Larger differences between Hurst exponents lead to larger efficiency gains. Larger absolute correlations between components lead to larger efficiency gains. 
\item Including more correlated components yields further improvements of the forecasting performance.
\end{enumerate}


\section{Empirical Study\label{sec:6}}
Volatility modeling and forecasting have been subjects of significant interest for decades. Recent advances in continuous-time finance highlight the strong performance of rough fBm and rough fOU processes in volatility forecasting. In this study, we investigate the practical implications of using rough mfBm and its associated theoretical framework. Our analysis focuses on daily realized volatilities of the Dow Jones 30 (DJ30) constituents from March 28, 2013, to August 21, 2021—a span of eight years—using data provided by Risk Lab.\footnote{See Dacheng Xiu's website: \href{https://dachxiu.chicagobooth.edu/\#risklab}{https://dachxiu.chicagobooth.edu/\#risklab}.} 
