\section{Pretopologies}\label{pretop}
We recall the concept of a \emph{pretopological space}, see \cite{J}, also called  a \textit{\v{C}ech closure space} in the literature, after \cite{Cech}.
\begin{defi}
A set $X$ with power set $\mathcal{P}(X)$ is  a \emph{pretopological space} if it possesses a preclosure
 operator   $~ \overline{\bullet}\ $ with the following properties
 \begin{enumerate}
 \item[(i)] $\overline{\emptyset}=\emptyset$.
 \item[(ii)] $A\subset \overline{A}$ for all $A\in \mathcal{P}(X)$.
 \item[(iii)] $\overline{A \cup B}=\overline{A}\cup \overline{B}$ for
   all $A,B \in \mathcal{P}(X)$.
 \end{enumerate}
 $A\in \mathcal{P}(X)$ is called \emph{closed} if $A= \overline{A}$.
\end{defi}
We also recall that such an $X$  is  a topological space iff the preclosure operator in addition
 satisfies
 \begin{enumerate}
 \item[(iv)] $\overline{\overline{A}}=\overline{A}$ for all $A \in \mathcal{P}(X)$.
 \end{enumerate}

 In our context, $\overline{A}$ may be interpreted as  that part of $X$ that you
can reach from $A$ by applying some operation. By (i) then nothing can be
reached from nothing. By (ii), all starting points can be reached. By (iii),  from a union of
starting sets nothing  more can be reached than the combination of what 
can be reached from each single set.  In a  directed graph $\Gamma$,
one can define the preclosure of a set of vertices as the union of this
set with the set of all forward 
 neighbors of these vertices.  Conversely,
from a pretopological space, we can
construct a directed graph by connecting each $x$ with all the other 
elements of $\overline{\{x\}}$.\\
Another  example of a preclosure operator arises from a
dynamical system. For concreteness
\ba
\label{top-ds1}
\dot{x}(t)&=&F(x(t)) \text{ for } x\in \R^d,t>0\\
\label{top-ds2}                 
x(0)&=&x_0
\ea
for some uniformly Lipschitz continuous  $F$, which then possesses a 
unique 
solution  with initial values \rf{top-ds2} for all
$t\ge 0$.  For $A\subset \R^d$, we  put
\bel{top-ds3}
\overline{A}^T:=\{ x(t), 0\le t \le T\} \text{ where } x(t) \text{ is
  a solution of \rf{top-ds1} with } x(0) \in A.
\qe
For each $T>0$, this then defines a preclosure operator. The closed sets
 are the forward invariant sets of  \rf{top-ds1}.\\

We can also consider a collection $X$ of programs, and let
$\overline{A}$ contain the programs that can be reached from those in $A$ by a
predefined number of steps. Depending on which types of concatenation of
programs we allow, however, (iii) need not be satisfied. An example is
genetic recombination of strings. From a single string, one can reach nothing
else by recombination, but from recombining two different ones, one may reach
many others. Thus, we may have to work in a context somewhat more general than
that of pretopological spaces. For a systematic treatment of various notions
of such structures generalizing or extending that of a pretopology, see \cite{Sta1}.\\

\begin{rem}
It is also possible to lift the construction to a categorical setting. We provide a definition that is a slight generalization of the usual notion of a ``universal closure operation'' as given, for example, in \cite[Section 5.7]{Bor}.
\begin{defi}
Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a finitely complete category. A \textit{universal preclosure operation} on $\mathcal{C}$ consists in assigning, for every subobject $S \xhookrightarrow{} C$ (also written $S\subset C$) in $\mathcal{C}$, another subobject $\overline{S} \xhookrightarrow{} C$ called the \textit{preclosure of $S$ in $C$}, subject to the following conditions:  
 \begin{enumerate}
 \item[(i)] $S\subset \overline{S}$ for all subobjects $S$ of $C$.
 \item[(ii)] $S\subset T \Rightarrow \overline{S}\subset \overline{T}$ for
   all subobjects $S,T$ of $C$.
   \item[(iii)] $f^{-1}(\overline{S})= \overline{f^{-1}(S)}$ whenever $f:B\to C$ is a morphism in $\mathcal{C}$.
 \end{enumerate}
\end{defi}
 In the case of a pretopological space, the axioms require $\overline{A \cup B}=\overline{A}\cup \overline{B}$ for $A,B \in \mathcal{P}(X)$ instead of (ii). However, since this implies $A\subset B \Rightarrow \overline{A}\subset \overline{B}$, the above definition generalizes the definition of the pretopological space, while we recover a usual universal closure operation in the sense of \cite{Bor} upon the imposition of idempotency.
\end{rem}
\bigskip
The following Lemma now helps us to relate pretopological spaces to cost functions.
\begin{lemma}
  A relation $c$ with \rf{1}, \rf{2} defines a pretopology in $S$ by putting,
  for some $r>0$,
  \bel{25}
  \overline{A}:=\overline{A}_r:=\{ q\in S: c(p,q)\le r \text{ for some }p\in A\}.
  \qe
  A pretopology is also obtained by putting
   \bel{26}
  \overline{A}:=\bigcap_{r>0}\overline{A}_r
  \qe
  with $\overline{A}_r$ as in \rf{25}.
  \end{lemma}
  \begin{proof}
  Hopefully clear.  
  \end{proof}

  Since a cost function $c$ with \eqref{1} and \eqref{2} satisfies all the properties of a metric except symmetry (and possibly finiteness, but that is not relevant here), we can still define closed (open) balls. But now, two types of balls centered at a point  arise,  outward and inward balls:
\begin{align}
B^+(p,t)&:=\{q\in S:\: c(p,q)\leq t\},&\label{outball}\\
B^-(p,t)& :=\{q\in S:\: c(q,p)\leq t\}.& \label{inball}
\end{align}Bor

\begin{defi}
A map $f:Z\to X$ between pretopological spaces is \emph{continuous} if
\bel{top11}
f(\overline{B})\subset \overline{f(B)}
\qe
for any subset $B$ of $Z$.
\end{defi}
More generally, this is also meaningful when $X$ possesses a preclosure operator
that only satisfies (i) and (ii), but not necessarily (iii). We observe
\begin{lemma}
  A cost morphism $f:(S_1,c_1)\to (S_2,c_2)$ is continuous w.r.t.~the pretopologies induced by the cost functions.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof} Let $r\ge 0$. 
    If $p\in B$ and $q\in \overline{B}$, i.e., $c_1(p,q)\le r$, then $c_2(f(p),f(q)\le r$ since  $f$ is a cost morphism. Hence $f(q)\in \overline{f(B)}$. And by taking limits, this also holds for the pretopology defined in \eqref{26}. 
\end{proof}


\begin{defi}
  A \emph{path} in the pretopological space $X$ is a continuous map
  \bel{top12}
  w:(I,c)\to X
  \qe
  where $c$ is the cost function \rf{2a} and $I$ (here and in the sequel) is equipped with the
  pretopology defined by $c$.\\
    A \emph{loop} with base point $p$ is such a continuous map with $w(1)=w(0)=p$. \\
  The paths $w_1, w_2:I\to X$ with $w_1(0)=w_2(0)=:p, w_1(1)=w_2(1)=:q$ are \emph{equivalent} if there exist continuous maps
  $W_1,W_2:I\times I \to X$ with
  \ba
  W_1(t,0)=w_1(t), & W_1(t,1)=w_2(t),&\\
  W_2(t,0)=w_2(t), &W_2(t,1)=w_1(t)& \text{
    for all }t\in I\\
  W_1(0,s)=W_2(0,s)=p, & W_1(1,s)=W_2(1,s)=q & \text{
    for all }s\in I\ .
  \ea
\end{defi}
In particular, when $\sigma:I\to I$ is a  surjective continuous
  map, the paths $w$ and $w\circ \sigma$ are  equivalent.\\
We note that a continuous map $I\to I$ (where, to repeat,  $I$ is always equipped   with the
pretopology defined by the cost function \rf{2a}) is monotonically
increasing. And we point out that if $w$ as in \rf{top12} is continuous, the map $w^\ast$ defined
by $w^\ast(t)=w(1-t)$ need not be continuous.
\begin{lemma}\label{monoid}
  The equivalence classes of loops with base point $p$ form a monoid $m(p)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
  Such loops $w_1,w_2$ can be composed as
  \be
  w(t):=w_2\circ w_1(t):=\begin{cases} w_1(2t) \text{ for } t\le 1/2\\
    w_2(2t-1) \text{ for } t\ge 1/2.
    \end{cases}
    \qe
    And the constant loop $w_0(t)=p$ for all $t$ is the neutral element, since
    for any loop $w$ with base $p$, $w\circ w_0$ and $w_0\circ w$ are
    equivalent to $w$. 
  \end{proof}
This monoid generalizes the fundamental group in homotopy theory to the non-commutative setting adopted here.

  We point out that in general, the monoids for different points are not
  necessarily isomorphic. For instance, add to $I$ a loop at the end point
  $1$. Then for $t\in I, t<1$, the monoids $m(t)$ are trivial, because there
  are no loops based at $t$, whereas the loop at $t=1$, and also for every
  point on the loop that we have added,  is isomorphic to $\N$. 



  \section{Path spaces as enriched categories}\label{enrich}

  As already mentioned in Section \ref{nonsymm}, Lawvere \cite{lawvere1973metric} observed the analogy between the triangle inequality 
 \begin{equation}\label{hc1}
     c(p,q) + c(q,r) \ge c(p,r)
 \end{equation}
 and the composition rule for Hom-sets
 \begin{equation}\label{hc1a}
     \Hom(p,q)\otimes \Hom(q,r) \to \Hom(p,r)
 \end{equation}
 in a category. Thus, if one says that there is a morphism $p\to q$ whenever $c(p,q)< \infty$, and one 
 notes that unital composition implies
 % requires in addition
 \begin{equation}\label{hc2}
 0\ge c(p,p)
     \end{equation}
     (the inequality $0\le c(p,p)$ follows from \eqref{hc1}),
     then one can work with the $P-$enriched category $S$ (where $P=(([0,\infty],\ge),\otimes)$ is the monoidal poset). Here $\otimes$ is the  monoidal operation (with $0$ as the neutral element), which when $S$ is a set equipped with a cost function, corresponds to addition.  The use of $\otimes$ emphasizes potential noncommutativity, as we wish to distinguish $a \otimes b$ from $b \otimes a$. 
     As mentioned around eq.~\eqref{eq:voidTriangleInequality}, when $c$ is bounded, say $c\le K$, we do not even need \eqref{hc1}, because we can always pass from $c$ to $c_K$ with $c_K(p,q)=c(p,q)+K(1-\delta_{pq})$ which then satisfies \eqref{hc1}.
     %For example, we could replace it by a reference back to the Lawvere metric space, point out that it is based on a monoidal composition rule for hom-sets that can also be non-commutative and then proceed with the paragraph below.}

\bigskip

     But we can consider a more refined structure where $\Hom(p,q)$ consists of all equivalence classes of paths  from $p$ to $q$ (required to be continuous w.r.t.~the cost function $c$), and $\otimes$ then is induced by the concatenation of paths. The neutral element in $\Hom(p,p)$ then is the constant path at $p$. For the concatenation $\gamma(p,r)$ of paths $\gamma(p,q)$ and $\gamma(q,r)$, we then have 
     \begin{equation}\label{hc20}
     \lth(\gamma(p,r))=\lth(\gamma(p,q)) +\lth(\gamma(q,r)). 
     \end{equation}
     And when 
     \begin{equation}\label{hc21}
     c(p,q)=\inf_{\gamma\in  \Hom(p,q)} \lth(\gamma),
     \end{equation}
     we get \eqref{hc1} and \eqref{hc2}again.\\
     A curve $\gamma\in  \Hom(p,q)$ with
     \begin{equation}
         \lth(\gamma)=c(p,q)
     \end{equation}
     then is a tachistic in the sense of Def. \ref{tach}. A chronodesic in the sense of Def. \ref{general} then satisfies the length minimizing property for any two  points on them that are connected by sufficiently short subarcs. \\


     For $p,q,r\in S$, there then exists an arrow $o_{p,q,r}$ from $c(p,q)\otimes c(q,r)$ to $c(p,r)$, standing for the  triangle inequality. When we work with paths between points and consider the object $c_\gamma$, the arrow $c_\gamma\geq 0$ simply means that the length of every path is non-negative and \eqref{hc20} corresponds to the fact that the length of the concatenation of two paths  does not exceed the sum of their lengths. Moreover, for each pair $p,q\in S$, in addition to the two arrows $c(p,q)\geq 0$ and $c(q,p)\geq 0$ we also have  the arrow $c(p,q)\geq c(q,p)$ or $c(q,p)\geq c(p,q)$ (and both in the case of a symmetric cost function). \\
The betweenness relation can be defined as follows.\\
For every $p,q,r \in S$,  $b(p,q,r)$ holds if and only if $o_{p,q,r}$ admits a reverse arrow, that is, there is an arrow $c(p,r)\to c(p,q)\otimes c(q,r)$. \\
When we consider curves $\gamma$ from $p$ to $r$ with finite cost, then any other point $q$ on such a curve would be between $p$ and $r$ w.r.t.~$\gamma$, but here, we do not pursue this more refined version. 


   \section{Curvature}\label{curv}  

The notion of total convexity can be generalized in the following way: \\
$S$ is totally convex, if for every pair $p,r\in S$ (where $S$ is equipped with a cost function $c$) and for every two objects $t_1,t_2\in P  =(([0,\infty],\le),\otimes)$ such that there is a morphism $t_1\otimes t_2\to c(p,r)$, and there exists $q\in S$ such that there are morphisms $t_1\to  c(p,q)$ and $t_2\to c(q,r)$. In the context of a length structure, this implies that any tachistic
with length $\leq t_1\otimes t_2$ can be decomposed (by the means of concatenation) into two tachistics $\gamma_1$ and $\gamma_2$ with lengths $\leq t_1$ and $\leq t_2$ respectively (here,  we recall the noncommutativity assumption). Furthermore, if all arrows are assumed to admit a reverse, then $S$ becomes what we may call a chronodesic space, that is, the analogue of a geodesic space in the case of a metric, that is, a symmetric (finite) cost function.\\
The property of being almost chronodesic can also be defined: $S$ is almost chronodesic if, for every  $p,r\in S$ and $\epsilon >0$, there exists a point $q\in S$ and arrows $t_1\to c(p,q)$ and $t_2\to c(q,r)$ whenever $t_1\otimes t_2=c(p,r)+\epsilon$ (i.e. both arrows $t_1\otimes t_2\to c(p,r)+\epsilon$ and $c(p,r)+\epsilon\to t_1\otimes t_2$ exist). \\












