% Existing approaches and their limitations 
Existing approaches to data imputation range from simple heuristics to advanced generative models. Heuristic techniques—such as mean, median, or mode imputation—are fast and easy to implement~\cite{baraldi2010introduction}, but they treat attributes independently and overlook complex relationships, often leading to biased or inaccurate results~\cite{khan2020sice}.
%
Model-based methods, including regression, expectation-maximization (EM), and matrix factorization, attempt to infer missing values by modeling statistical dependencies among attributes~\cite{little2019statistical}. While generally more accurate, they can be computationally expensive and rely on strong assumptions about the data distribution.
%
Recent deep learning–based approaches, such as autoencoders, GANs, and transformer models, have shown promise in capturing nonlinear patterns, especially in large or multi-modal datasets~\cite{yoon2018gain}. Building on this progress, large language models (LLMs) have emerged as powerful tools for data imputation, thanks to their ability to model complex dependencies and generalize across diverse contexts~\cite{narayan2022can, zhang2024jellyfish, li2024towards}.

However, directly applying LLMs to missing value imputation presents several challenges. First, the full content of a database table often exceeds the model’s context window, making it infeasible to include all relevant information. Second, even when the context window is sufficient, providing the entire table can introduce substantial noise, overwhelming the model with irrelevant or weakly related details,  potentially degrading performance. Third, in large tables, it becomes difficult to determine which parts of the input influenced the imputation, limiting the transparency and explainability of the process. These challenges raise a key research question: \emph{Can we identify and extract a minimal yet sufficient subset of the table that enables more accurate and interpretable imputations? On what factors does this selection depend, and how can we ensure that the extracted context preserves the necessary statistical or semantic cues for reliable inference?}

%\medskip
We hypothesize that, in many cases, missing values can be imputed accurately using a localized context---that is, a small subset of the table that contains the most relevant information for the missing entry. Our approach is to identify this localized context prior to imputation by decomposing the problem into two sub-tasks: (1) selecting a subset of columns that are most relevant to the column with missing values, and (2) identifying a subset of rows that provide sufficient contextual evidence for imputation.
Our extensive evaluation across a variety of datasets demonstrates that using this targeted strategy not only improves performance but also helps explain the predictions by showing which parts of the data influenced the output.

\noindent\textbf{Contributions:}
Our contributions can be summarized as follows:
(1)~We propose a method for selecting a small, informative subset of attributes and tuples to guide LLM-based imputation, improving both accuracy and explainability.
(2)~Our subset selection is designed to handle inconsistencies in real-world data during the selection process, making it more robust to noise.
%(3)~We provide a transparent mechanism to trace which parts of the input influenced the imputation, helping users better understand and trust the results.
(3)~We present a complexity analysis of the proposed method, demonstrating its suitability for large-scale datasets.
(4)~We show that our method can be effectively applied to small local LLMs, offering a practical solution for privacy-sensitive applications.
(5)~Through extensive experiments on real-world datasets from various domains, we demonstrate that LDI outperforms existing state-of-the-art approaches in terms of accuracy and offers higher explainability compared to other LLM-based methods.
(6)~To support reproducibility and further research, we publicly release our code, framework, and datasets.\footnote{\url{https://github.com/soroushomidvar/LDI}}

\section{Related Works} \label{sec:related_works}
Our work is closely related to two main areas of research: (1) data imputation and (2) dependency detection.

\subsection{Data Imputation}
Missing data imputation is a well-researched field, with a wide range of methods developed to address the challenges of handling incomplete datasets. 

\noindent \textbf{Traditional Methods:}
Basic approaches replace missing values with fixed constants, such as zero or the mean, or use strategies like Last Observation Carried Forward (LOCF) and Next Observation Carried Backward (NOCB)~\cite{engels2003imputation, lachin2016fallacies, klamroth2014three}. These approaches are computationally inexpensive and straightforward to implement but rely on restrictive assumptions, such as the randomness of missing values or ordering of tuples, that often lead to biased results~\cite{molnar2008does}, limiting their applicability in real-world scenarios where data is complex and irregular.


\noindent \textbf{Statistical Models:}
While basic methods rely on direct substitution, statistical models enhance imputation accuracy by estimating the underlying data distribution and predicting missing values accordingly. Examples include approaches based on relational dependency networks~\cite{mayfield2010eracer}, factor graphs~\cite{rekatsinas2017holoclean, wu2020attention}, distance likelihood maximization~\cite{song2020imputing}, low-rank matrix completion~\cite{candes2012exact}, and non-linear models such as tree-based ensembles~\cite{stekhoven2012missforest,chen2016xgboost}. While effective in certain scenarios, these approaches often rely on assumptions about data properties, such as normality or linearity, which can limit their applicability to real-world datasets.

\noindent \textbf{similarity-based Approaches:}
Another line of research focuses on similarity-based techniques, where missing values are inferred based on similar neighbors with complete values. Neighbor-based methods, such as k-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)~\cite{altman1992introduction} and its extension, KNN Ensemble (KNNE)~\cite{domeniconi2004nearest}, find the nearest neighbors with the smallest distance on complete attributes and aggregate their values as the imputation for the missing value. Similarly, MIBOS~\cite{wu2012missing} imputes missing values through a similarity-based iterative approach that counts matching attribute values. Instead of relying on individual neighbors, some methods group similar tuples into clusters and perform the imputations within each cluster. For example, CMI~\cite{zhang2008missing} imputes missing values by first clustering the dataset and then generating plausible values using similar tuples in the cluster. MGI~\cite{yan2015missing} applies the Gaussian mixture model (GMM), and IFC~\cite{nikfalazar2017new} uses iterative fuzzy clustering to integrate mean imputation, hot deck, and multiple imputation. However, these similarity-based methods are constrained by their similarity functions, cannot handle semantic similarity, and have limited performance if used directly on the data without additional steps.

\noindent \textbf{Generative Models:}
Beyond these traditional methods, recent advances in generative models have opened new possibilities for data imputation by enhancing the ability to learn data patterns and generate more realistic values. For instance, GAIN~\cite{yoon2018gain} uses the Generative Adversarial Nets (GAN) framework, where a generator imputes missing components and a discriminator ensures the generated data follows the true distribution. MIDA~\cite{gondara2018mida} proposes a multiple imputation model using deep denoising autoencoders, and HI-VAE~\cite{nazabal2020handling} adapts variational autoencoders to work with heterogeneous data. IPM~\cite{mei2021capturing} leverages pre-trained language models to capture semantics for predicting missing values in databases. It introduces IPM-Multi, which models imputation as multiclass classification, and IPM-Binary, which uses binary classification for candidate selection. A more recent work, FMW~\cite{narayan2022can}, also demonstrates that LLMs can handle missing data imputation effectively by framing them as prompting tasks. Although these models achieve state-of-the-art results, their black-box nature lacks explainability, making it difficult to understand how and why specific imputations are generated. The inability to trace an imputed value back to a specific part of the input data prevents validating, correcting, and refining imputations and limits the model's applicability where understanding why a particular value was imputed can be just as important as the imputed value itself.

\subsection{Dependency Detection}

In the context of attribute selection, our method is closely related to research on identifying dependencies between attributes, particularly through the use of \textit{Functional Dependencies (FDs)} and \textit{Approximate Functional Dependencies (AFDs)}. FDs describe exact relationships where the value of one attribute is fully determined by another. Formally, an FD \(X \rightarrow A\) holds in a relation \(r\) if for all tuples \(t\) and \(u\) in \(r\), \(t[X] = u[X]\) implies \(t[A] = u[A]\). FDs are widely used in database design to capture exact deterministic relationships~\cite{papenbrock2016hybrid, bohannon2006conditional, salem2016fixing, al2022data}. In practice, however, FDs are often too strict due to inconsistencies or noise in real-world data. To address this, AFDs have been introduced~\cite{kivinen1995approximate, mandros2017discovering}. An AFD \(X \rightarrow A\) holds approximately if it is satisfied by most of the data, with the quality measured by the error \(e(X \rightarrow A)\), defined as the minimum fraction of tuples that must be removed to make the dependency exact~\cite{king2003discovery}.

FDs and AFDs form the basis of several data cleaning techniques, including sparse regression for dependency discovery~\cite{guo2019learning}, conditional functional dependencies~\cite{bohannon2006conditional}, and dynamic AFDs that tolerate dirty data by incorporating data source information~\cite{ding2024dafdiscover}. However, these approaches are generally designed for structured attributes with consistent formatting and are less effective when applied to unstructured, and possibly lengthy, textual fields. They assume uniform representation and cannot capture dependencies expressed through partial matches or semantically related phrases. In contrast, LLMs are well-suited for such cases, leveraging pretraining on large-scale natural language data to recognize subtle patterns and semantic signals in free-text attributes, enabling more effective tasks such as imputation.

\section{Preliminary}

We now formalize the problem of missing data imputation, introduce the goal of LDI and provide an overview of our solution.

\noindent \textbf{Problem Statement:}
Consider a dataset $\mathcal{D}$ with schema $\mathcal{R}$, consisting of attributes $A_j \in \mathcal{R}$. The dataset is represented as a collection of tuples, where each tuple $t \in \mathcal{D}$ contains a set of cells. A missing value in the $j$-th attribute of the $i$-th tuple is represented as $t_i[A_j] =\varnothing$.  
Let $\hat{\mathcal{D}}$ be an imputed version of $\mathcal{D}$, where for all $i, j$ such that $t_i[A_j]$ is missing in $\mathcal{D}$, there exists an imputed tuple $\hat{t_i} \in \mathcal{\hat{D}}$ where $\hat{t_i} [A_j] \neq \varnothing$. We denote $\mathcal{D}^*$ as the ground truth dataset, where each tuple $t_i^*\in\mathcal{D}^*$ contains the true cell values $t_i^*[A_j]$ for all attributes $A_j\in\mathcal{R}$.

\noindent \textbf{Goals:}
The goal of this paper is to take a dataset $\mathcal{D}$ and predict the entries in the target attribute $A_T$ for each tuple $t_i$ where $t_i [A_T] = \varnothing$, by focusing only on the most relevant attributes and tuples. We introduce a localized imputation method that uses the reasoning capabilities of LLMs while addressing key practical needs. Specifically, our method aims to (1) achieve high imputation accuracy in real-world datasets, even when the missing values depend on complex and unknown relationships, (2) improve explainability by making it possible to trace which parts of the input influenced the prediction and why they were selected, (3) handle common challenges such as formatting inconsistencies or noisy fields without relying on strict assumptions, and (4) minimize the need for external datasets or task-specific resources, allowing the method to be applied more broadly and efficiently.

