\section{Proposed Method}
LLMs have shown strong performance on a range of table reasoning tasks, making them promising candidates for data imputation. However, they often struggle with scalability to large tables~\cite{chen2022large, ji2023survey} and lack interpretability in their imputation decisions.  
To address these issues, our method, LDI, selectively identifies the most relevant attributes and tuples to focus the model’s attention and improve explainability. 
%LLMs are prone to errors when handling lengthy contexts\cite{chen2022large, ji2023survey}. Instead of relying on all data, LDI is designed to identify and extract relevant attributes and tuples to not only enhance LLM capabilities to generate missing values but also improve the model's explainability. 
In the first phase ($\S$\ref{sec:relevant_attribute_identification}), the method captures and retains only the attributes that have an underlying connection with $A_T$ and can contribute to the imputation process. The second phase ($\S$\ref{sec:similar_tuple_selection}) implements a tuple-level filtering mechanism to retain a diverse and relevant set of records. Finally, in the third phase ($\S$\ref{sec:imputation-using-llm}), the refined attributes and tuples are fed into the LLM, leveraging it as a function for imputing missing values. An overview of this process is demonstrated in Figure~\ref{fig:main}. 

