\subsection{Attribute Selection} \label{sec:relevant_attribute_identification}

To identify attributes most relevant to the imputation target, LDI performs attribute selection in three phases: (1) selecting a representative subset of tuples, (2) detecting group-specific patterns in candidate columns, and (3) evaluating approximate dependency relationships. This section introduces the relaxed dependency criterion we use and outlines how it guides attribute selection.

Traditional functional dependencies are commonly used to model relationships between attributes. However, they tend to be too strict for real-world tables that contain noise and long textual values. To better handle such cases, we introduce a relaxed dependency criterion tailored to noisy, text-rich data.


%To identify the most relevant attributes, LDI evaluates the relationship between \(A_T\) and each candidate attribute \(A_C\), aiming to identify informative attributes through text-aware dependency detection when relationships are not explicitly specified. Although we use a specific attribute selection strategy, this phase can be replaced with other methods depending on the type of data, without changing the rest of the approach.

