\subsection{Data Imputation Using LLM}
\label{sec:imputation-using-llm}
In the third phase of our proposed method, we leverage LLMs to impute missing values. This phase employs few-shot learning by providing the LLM with a small set of example tuples that include the relevant attributes identified in \S~\ref{sec:relevant_attribute_identification} and retrieved similar tuples from \S~\ref{sec:similar_tuple_selection}.
The LLM then infers the missing value by recognizing patterns across these examples. 

To prepare the input, we adopt a standard serialization format from prior work~\cite{narayan2022can}, where each tuple is expressed as a sequence of key-value pairs. The input prompt consists of a task description, a list of examples, and a query row with a missing value:


\begin{tcolorbox}[colframe={my_blue_color},colback={my_blue_color}, left=3pt, right=3pt, top=3pt, bottom=3pt] %title=Data Serialization Example
\small
\begin{verbatim}
[Context]
  A short description of the task.
[Examples]
  Example 1:
  Attr. A: Val. A1, Attr. B: Val. B1, ...
  Target Attr.: Target Val. 1,
  Example 2:
  Attr. A: Val. A2, Attr. B: Val. B2, ...
  Target Attr.: Target Val. 2,
  ...
[Query]
  Attr. A: Val. Ax, Attr. B: Val. Bx, ...
  Target Attr.: ?
\end{verbatim}
\end{tcolorbox}

Once the input is constructed, the LLM uses the examples to infer the missing value for the target attribute $A_T$. This allows the model to generalize from limited data and detect complex relationships between attributes.
%Once the examples are serialized, they are fed into the LLM. The LLM uses the provided examples to infer the missing value for the attribute $A_T$ in a given row. This process leverages the LLM's ability to generalize from a few examples and recognize patterns to understand data relationships.

LLMs offer several key advantages in this imputation task. First, they demonstrate strong semantic understanding by leveraging extensive pretraining to infer values based on real-world knowledge. For example, they can associate area codes with their corresponding cities or map currency codes to countries, all without requiring external lookup tables. Second, LLMs are capable of generalizing to unseen cases: even when a specific pattern has not appeared in the provided examples, the model can infer plausible values by drawing on its understanding of similar contexts. Finally, LLMs exhibit robustness to noise and formatting inconsistencies. They can recognize equivalences among different representations of the same entity—for instance, interpreting ‘+1-702’, ‘702/’, and ‘702-’ as referring to the same area code—allowing them to handle noisy or variably formatted data with ease.

