\noindent\textbf{Example 1 (Motivating Example) continued:} 
Our proposed method first groups the data by the values in the \texttt{City} column and selects $m$ groups, each with $n$ records, to generate a representative sample (see Figure~\ref{fig:ex_solv}). It then iterates through all candidate columns, applying the pattern detection method to identify repeated substrings that may serve as group-specific signals. In this example, both the \texttt{Phone} and \texttt{Reviews} columns exhibit recurring patterns within groups. However, during the dependency evaluation step, only the \texttt{Phone} column is selected. Although the \texttt{Reviews} column contains the substring ``\textit{\texttt{[('Rated }}'' across several groups, this pattern is not unique to any specific city, and thus does not meet the criteria for group-level dependency. In contrast, the \texttt{Phone} column shows city-specific area codes that strongly correlate with the target values, and is therefore retained for imputation.
Importantly, the method can handle formatting inconsistencies in the data. For example, phone numbers may include different prefixes (e.g., \textit{+1}) or use varied separators. Despite such variations, our LCS-based approach is able to capture the consistent patterns, such as the core area code, across the grouped entries.

In the second phase, the method uses the selected attribute(s), in this case, \texttt{Phone}, to identify tuples similar to the one with the missing value. It applies a similarity search and selects $k$ diverse examples that are most similar to the incomplete tuple while having distinct values in the target column. This ensures that the chosen examples are both relevant and diverse, providing a balanced context for the final prediction.

In the third phase, these selected tuples are serialized into key-value formats and provided to an LLM in a few-shot prompting setup. The LLM is asked to predict the missing city value based on the patterns observed in the provided examples. By focusing only on relevant attributes and carefully selected examples, the model is guided toward accurate and more explainable imputations. In this example, the LLM would rely solely on the signals in the \texttt{Phone} column, making the basis of the imputed value both precise and traceable. This illustrates how LDI can effectively reduce noise, focus on informative data, and produce traceable results even in the absence of predefined relationships in the dataset.
