\section{Performance Analysis}
In this section, we analyze the computational cost of the main components of our method. We first present supporting lemmas for key steps in each phase.

%%%%

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:gst}
Given a collection of $n$ strings, each of length $\ell$, all substrings that appear in at least $q$ fraction of the strings can be found in time $\mathcal{O}(n \cdot \ell + r)$ and space $\mathcal{O}(n \cdot \ell)$, where $r$ is the number of substrings reported.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
We build a Generalized Suffix Tree (GST) for the $n$ input strings, each of length $\ell$, after appending a unique terminal symbol to each string. This tree can be constructed in $\mathcal{O}(n \cdot \ell)$ time and space using Ukkonen’s algorithm~\cite{ukkonen1995line}. Each leaf is labeled with its corresponding string ID, and a post-order traversal is used to annotate each internal node with the set of string IDs in its subtree. For each node, if the number of distinct string IDs is at least $\lceil qn \rceil$, we report the substring represented by that node. Reporting all such substrings takes $\mathcal{O}(r)$ time, where $r$ is the number of substrings found. Therefore, the total time and space complexity is $\mathcal{O}(n \cdot \ell + r)$ and $\mathcal{O}(n \cdot \ell)$, respectively.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:unique}
Given a collection of $s$ strings, each of length $\ell$, partitioned into $m$ groups, the time to determine whether each string appears uniquely within a single group is $\mathcal{O}(s \cdot \ell)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
To determine whether each string appears in only one group, we use a hashmap that associates each string with the set of group identifiers in which it appears. We iterate over all $s$ strings and, for each one, insert it into the hashmap and record its group identifier. Since each string has length $\ell$, inserting or comparing a string in the hashmap takes $\mathcal{O}(\ell)$ time. Processing all $s$ strings therefore takes $\mathcal{O}(s \cdot \ell)$ time. After building the hashmap, we scan its entries to check whether any string is associated with more than one group. As there are at most $s$ unique strings, this step takes $\mathcal{O}(s)$ time. Hence, the overall time complexity is $\mathcal{O}(s \cdot \ell)$.
\end{proof}


\subsection{Attribute Selection}


The time complexity of the attribute selection phase depends on the number of candidate attributes ($a_c$), the number of groups formed by values in the target attribute ($m$), the number of sampled tuples in each group ($n$), and the average string length in attribute values ($\ell$). The algorithm consists of four main steps: (1) group sampling, (2) pattern detection, (3) uniqueness checking across groups, and (4) an optional redundancy filtering step.

In the first step, the algorithm samples a subset of the dataset by grouping tuples according to their values in the target attribute $A_T$. It then randomly selects $m$ groups and samples $n$ tuples from each group. Letting $|\mathcal{D}|$ be the size of the original dataset, this grouping and sampling process takes $\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{D}|)$ time, as each tuple is processed once.

In the second step, the algorithm detects common substrings that appear in at least $q$ fraction of the tuples within each group. This is done using a GST, which enables efficient substring frequency analysis. According to Lemma~\ref{lem:gst}, the GST can be built in $\mathcal{O}(n \cdot \ell)$ time per group, and all frequent substrings can be extracted in total time $\mathcal{O}(n \cdot \ell + r)$, where $r$ is the number of substrings reported. Applying this across $m$ groups and $a_c$ attributes results in a total time complexity of
$\mathcal{O}(a_c \cdot m \cdot (n \cdot \ell + r))$.

In the third step, the algorithm checks whether each substring appears uniquely within a single group. Lemma~\ref{lem:unique} shows that this can be efficiently done using a hashmap that associates each substring with the set of groups it appears in. Letting $s = m \cdot r$ be the total number of substrings across all groups, this step takes $\mathcal{O}(s \cdot \ell)$ time per attribute. Across all $a_c$ attributes, the total cost becomes: $\mathcal{O}(a_c \cdot m \cdot r \cdot \ell)$.

An optional fourth step removes redundant substrings that are fully contained within longer ones in each group. This improves the clarity and specificity of the selected patterns but does not affect the correctness of the algorithm. This step can be done using pairwise comparisons among the $r$ substrings in each group. The cost of this step is $\mathcal{O}(r^2 \cdot \ell)$ per group. Across all $m$ groups and $a_c$ attributes, this adds an additional cost of $\mathcal{O}(a_c \cdot m \cdot r^2 \cdot \ell)$.

Combining all steps, the total time complexity of the attribute selection phase depends on whether the optional redundancy removal step is applied.

\noindent \textbf{Without the optional step}, the total time complexity of the attribute selection phase is:
\[
%\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{D}| + a_c \cdot m \cdot (n \cdot \ell + r \cdot \ell ))
\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{D}| + a_c \cdot m \cdot \ell \cdot (n + r))
\]

\noindent \textbf{With the optional redundancy removal step}, the total time complexity becomes:
\[
%\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{D}| + a_c \cdot m \cdot (n \cdot \ell + r^2 \cdot \ell))
\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{D}| + a_c \cdot m \cdot \ell \cdot (n + r^2))
\]

\noindent when the optional step is enabled, we incur an additional cost of $r^2 \cdot \ell$ per group to remove substrings contained in longer ones. 

% \[
% \mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{D}| + a_c \cdot m \cdot (n \cdot \ell + r + r \cdot \ell + r^2 \cdot \ell)).
% \]

% \[
% \mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{D}| + a_c \cdot m \cdot (n \cdot \ell + r^2 \cdot \ell)).
% \]



