\subsection{Tuple Selection}

To impute a missing value in a tuple $t_i$, our method selects $k$ similar and diverse examples from the dataset. Let $c$ be the number of complete tuples (i.e., tuples with known values in $A_T$), and let $a_s$ be the number of selected attributes. For each attribute $A$, we compute the similarity between $t_i[A]$ and $t_j[A]$ for all $c$ complete tuples $t_j$.

To do this efficiently, we build a GST over the $c+1$ strings (one from $t_i[A]$ and $c$ from complete tuples) for each selected attribute. Building the GST takes $\mathcal{O}(c \cdot \ell)$ time per attribute (see Lemma~\ref{lem:gst}). After the tree is built, the LCS between $t_i[A]$ and any $t_j[A]$ can be found in $\mathcal{O}(\ell)$ time. Therefore, computing the similarity between $t_i$ and all $c$ complete tuples for all $a_s$ attributes needs a total time of $\mathcal{O}(a_s \cdot c \cdot \ell)$ for the similarity computation.

After calculating all similarity scores, we sort the $c$ tuples in $\mathcal{O}(c \log c)$ time to identify the top $k$ diverse most similar examples. A final scan to ensure diversity in $A_T$ values among the top $k$ adds $\mathcal{O}(c)$ time, which is dominated by the sorting.
Hence, the total time complexity of the tuple selection phase for one incomplete tuple is: 
\[
\mathcal{O}(a_s \cdot c \cdot \ell + c \log c)
\]


