\subsection{Imputation Using LLM}
LDI makes one LLM call per missing value, resulting in a total number of calls equal to the number of missing entries. Each prompt includes a short fixed-length context, $k$ examples, and a query (see Figure~\ref{fig:main}). Both the query and examples in LDI are restricted to $a_s$ selected attributes. Assuming the context contains $\lambda$ tokens and each attribute value has an average of $\tau$ tokens, the total token usage per prompt is approximately:
\[
\lambda + k \times a_s \times \tau.
\]


\section{Experiments}
In this section, we detail an extensive set of experiments designed to evaluate our proposed data imputation method. Our goal is to demonstrate how selecting relevant attributes and tuples affects the performance and explainability of the imputation process.

\subsection{Datasets}
Our experiments were conducted on four real-world datasets from three different domains, as summarized in Table \ref{tab:dataset}. These datasets vary in size, number of attributes, and incomplete data characteristics, reflecting real-world diversity. The incomplete attributes always contain categorical values, with vocabulary sizes ranging from 49 in the Restaurant dataset to 385 in the Phone dataset. Buy and Restaurant contain fewer attributes compared to our other datasets but are widely used in data imputation studies\cite{narayan2022can, chen2024seed, song2020imputing, mei2021capturing}, making them valuable for comparison purposes. In contrast, Zomato and Phone datasets include a larger number of attributes, enabling a more comprehensive evaluation of attribute selection.
