\subsection{Experimental Setup}
The experiments were conducted on a machine with the following specifications: Ubuntu 20.04, AMD Ryzen 9 3900X 12-Core Processor, and 64 GB of memory, using Python version 3.9.20. For the Buy and Restaurant datasets, all available rows were utilized, and for other datasets, 1000 rows were randomly selected to manage the computational cost and latency of LLM calls. To ensure a fair comparison, the same set of randomly sampled rows was used across different settings and each experiment was repeated and averaged at least five times. In the group sampling phase, we set $m=10$ and $n=10$, ensuring $m$ groups that each contained $n$ samples. In cases where the dataset had fewer groups or samples ($<10$), the maximum available was used. The inner threshold $q$ and outer threshold $p$ were both set to 0.9 for the Buy and Phone datasets, and to 0.8 for the Zomato and Restaurant datasets, as these contain more inconsistencies and variation in their values. This setup allows for slight flexibility in pattern matching—e.g., with a 0.9 threshold, up to 1 row per group and up to 1 group across all groups may have no patterns—while still enforcing strong evidence of dependency.
%Both the inner threshold $q$ and outer threshold $p$ were set to $90\%$, unless otherwise specified. This setup allows for up to 1 row in each group to not contain the detected pattern and up to 1 group across all groups to have no patterns. 
