\subsection{Varying p and q}

To study the effect of hyperparameters \textit{p} and \textit{q}, we conduct an experiment on the Zomato dataset, which contains 17 attributes. We chose this dataset specifically because the larger number of columns allows us to better observe how the attribute selection step responds to different values of \textit{p} and \textit{q}. 
We vary both parameters from 0 to 0.75 in increments of 0.25, and for each setting, we evaluate the performance of our method over 5 runs.
We do not report results for settings where \textit{p} and \textit{q} are increased up to 1, as the Zomato dataset is particularly noisy and contains many inconsistencies in its fields. Applying such strict thresholds would result in no attributes being selected in many sampled data, even when some attributes contain informative patterns.
When either \textit{p} or \textit{q} is set to 0, no constraint is imposed on the the dependency relationship, and all attributes are retained.
In particular, setting $q=0$ removes the frequency threshold, allowing any substring that co-occurs with a distinct value in the target column to be accepted as a valid pattern. Similarly, setting $p=0$ eliminates the requirement on the minimum number of distinct target values that must support the dependency.

% check my comments on slack for restructing this section.

In Table~\ref{tab:pq_main}, we evaluate how varying the hyperparameters $p$ and $q$ affects the imputation performance on the Zomato dataset when $k=3$ examples are selected. We compare two example selection strategies: (a) random selection and (b) diverse similarity-based selection. 
For both strategies, we observe a notable performance gain when $p$ and $q$ are set to 0.25 or higher, compared to settings where either $p$ or $q$ is 0 and all attributes are included. The gains are more pronounced in the random selection setting, where attribute filtering plays a greater role in guiding the model. 
In the first setting (Table~\ref{tab:pq_main_rand}), where examples are selected randomly, we observe that using $p=0$ and $q=0$ yields an exact match accuracy of 0.846 and ROUGE-1 score (shown in parentheses) of 0.883. These scores represent the baseline performance without applying our method, and match the results of the FMW. As we increase $p$ and $q$ to 0.25, the performance improves significantly, reaching approximately 0.960 accuracy and 0.978 ROUGE-1. This indicates that even a modest level of filtering can effectively remove distracting features and highly improve model performance. As $p$ and $q$ increase further, performance remains consistently high. Notably, even at $p=0.75$ and $q=0.75$, where the model uses only 2 attributes, reducing nearly 95\% of the input content in terms of characters (as discussed next), the accuracy remains close to 0.960. This shows that our method can retain the critical information required for imputation while drastically reducing the input size.

In the second setting (Table~\ref{tab:pq_main_sim}), where tuples are selected using diverse similarity, the overall performance improves compared to random selection across all values of $p$ and $q$. The accuracy under $p=0$ and $q=0$ reaches 0.944, with a ROUGE-1 score of 0.951, showing that selecting diverse and informative examples enhances performance even without attribute filtering. As $p$ and $q$ increase, performance continues to improve. 
%The best result is achieved at $p=0.75$, $q=0.50$, where the model reaches an accuracy of 0.978 and \mbox{ROUGE-1} of 0.990. This setting requires more than 3 attributes on average (see Table~\ref{tab:pq}). In comparison, the setting $p=0.75$, $q=0.75$ uses only 2 attributes and achieves a very similar performance. 
The best results are achieved at $p = 0.75$, $q = 0.50$ and $p = 0.50$, $q = 0.75$, where the model reaches an accuracy of 0.978 and ROUGE-1 scores of 0.990 and 0.986, respectively. However, these settings require more attributes compared to the setting $p = 0.75$, $q = 0.75$, which uses just 2 attributes (see Table~\ref{tab:pq}) and achieves a very similar performance. 
Given that the difference in performance is minimal, the stricter setting with fewer attributes is a more practical choice, as it reduces input size while maintaining nearly identical accuracy. This can highlight the flexibility of our method in allowing users to balance performance with input efficiency.

To study the effect of varying \textit{p} and \textit{q} on attribute reduction, we observe that as the values of \textit{p} and \textit{q} increase, the algorithm becomes stricter in its selection. The results are shown in Table~\ref{tab:pq}. When either parameter is set to 0, the method selects all 16 attributes. As both parameters increase, the average number of selected attributes steadily decreases. For instance, at $p = 0.50$, $q = 0.50$, only 7.6 attributes are selected on average, and this number drops to just 2 when $p = 0.75$ and $q = 0.75$. This trend confirms that tighter dependency thresholds are effective in filtering out weak or noisy attribute relationships, enabling the model to focus on the most informative signals.


