\section{Conclusion and Future Work}\label{sec:con}
In this paper, we proposed a localized imputation method that improves both the accuracy and explainability of LLM-based data imputation. Instead of relying on all available data or randomly selected samples, our approach selects a small set of informative attributes and representative tuples for each missing value. This focused design helps reduce noise, handle inconsistencies in real-world data, and guide the LLM toward more accurate predictions. Additionally, we make the imputation process more transparent by limiting the parts of the input that influence the prediction and explaining why they were chosen. Experiments on four real-world datasets show that our method achieves state-of-the-art performance while offering better explainability than existing LLM-based approaches.

This work opens several directions for future research: (1)~One direction is to explore more advanced strategies for selecting informative attributes and tuples, particularly in cases where dependencies are conditional or involve more complex patterns. (2)~Another direction is to explore transfer learning to adapt dependencies learned from one dataset to similar domains. (3)~Future work can also enhance model explainability by going beyond limiting the input and identifying dependent parts, and instead, develop richer explanations that show how the model reasons about the data and arrives at each imputation.

